window.MSL_SEARCH={"chapters":[{"id":"welcome","u":"welcome/index.html","t":"Welcome","n":6,"k":"basics"},{"id":"getting-started","u":"getting-started/index.html","t":"Getting Started","n":6,"k":"basics"},{"id":"main-window","u":"main-window/index.html","t":"The Main Window","n":5,"k":"app"},{"id":"applications","u":"applications/index.html","t":"Applications Tab","n":7,"k":"apps"},{"id":"terminal","u":"terminal/index.html","t":"Terminal & Shells","n":5,"k":"shell"},{"id":"overview","u":"overview/index.html","t":"Overview Tab","n":7,"k":"app"},{"id":"instances","u":"instances/index.html","t":"Managing Instances","n":5,"k":"instances"},{"id":"tools","u":"tools/index.html","t":"Tools Tab","n":15,"k":"upkeep"},{"id":"sandbox","u":"sandbox/index.html","t":"Sandbox Tab","n":4,"k":"security"},{"id":"files","u":"files/index.html","t":"MSL Files","n":7,"k":"instances"},{"id":"graphics","u":"graphics/index.html","t":"Graphics & mslgd","n":4,"k":"apps"},{"id":"experiments","u":"experiments/index.html","t":"Experimental Features","n":9,"k":"apps"},{"id":"power","u":"power/index.html","t":"Power & Lifecycle","n":3,"k":"instances"},{"id":"public","u":"public/index.html","t":"Reaching it from anywhere","n":6,"k":"shell"},{"id":"admin-portal","u":"admin-portal/index.html","t":"Admin Portal","n":7,"k":"security"},{"id":"updates","u":"updates/index.html","t":"Updates","n":3,"k":"upkeep"},{"id":"troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting/index.html","t":"Troubleshooting","n":12,"k":"trouble"},{"id":"cli","u":"cli/index.html","t":"Command line","n":1,"k":"shell"},{"id":"reference","u":"reference/index.html","t":"Reference","n":5,"k":"reference"},{"id":"placeholder","u":"placeholder/index.html","t":"Placeholder chapter","n":0,"k":"extras"}],"entries":[{"u":"welcome/welcome.html","a":"Welcome to MSL","h":null,"c":"Welcome","x":"MSL — the Mac Subsystem for Linux — runs real Linux distributions on your Mac. Not an emulator and not a compatibility layer: an actual Linux kernel with an actual Linux userland, running on Apple's own Virtualization framework, so there's no emulation in the way and no virtual-machine window to look after. You get a real Linux shell in about a second, and — experimentally — Linux apps with windows of their own, each with its own Dock tile, its own icon and its own name in the menu bar. ヽ(・∀・)ﾉ A small offering macOS already has plenty of good ways to reach Linux: Docker, UTM, Lima, OrbStack, a VM in a window. MSL isn't here to replace any of them. It's another path — one that tries to make Linux feel like it belongs on the Mac, with a lot of engineering effort, care and getting-it-wrong-first poured into it. If it suits the way you work, lovely. If one of the others suits you better, that's lovely too.","s":"Real Linux on your Mac, as close to native as one project could get it."},{"u":"welcome/welcome.html#what-you-can-do","a":"Welcome to MSL","h":"What you can do with it","c":"Welcome","x":"Open a Linux shell A real login shell with job control, colours and line editing, inside the app or in Terminal.app. Run Linux apps Linux GUI programs open as ordinary Mac windows, each with its own Dock tile. Share files both ways Your Mac home folder appears inside Linux at /mnt/mac, and MSL Files browses both sides. Size each machine Choose CPUs, a fixed or dynamic amount of memory, and how big each disk may grow. Save and roll back Snapshots, hibernation, and disk check-and-repair tools with automatic backups. Cut things off Sandbox switches that detach the network, the home share, graphics or input.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/welcome.html#next","a":"Welcome to MSL","h":"Where to go next","c":"Welcome","x":"New here? The two-minute tour walks the whole app from left to right. Ready to go? Install a distro, then create an instance. Curious how it works? Under the hood explains the moving parts in plain words. Want to know what's solid and what's still experimental? What's ready is honest about it. Searching this guide The search field at the top of the sidebar finds any word in the guide — and it's forgiving: it copes with typos, finishes words as you type, and knows that \"RAM\" means memory and \"VM\" means instance. Press ⌘F in this window to jump to it. More in Using this guide.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/tour.html","a":"A two-minute tour","h":null,"c":"Welcome","x":"MSL has one main window, a file browser, and a few smaller windows. Here's all of it, left to right.","s":"Every window and panel in MSL, in the order you'll meet them."},{"u":"welcome/tour.html#main","a":"A two-minute tour","h":"The main window","c":"Welcome","x":"A sidebar-and-detail window, like Mail or Notes. The sidebar lists your instances — each is one Linux machine with its own name. A coloured badge shows the distro; a small dot shows whether it's running. At the top, a pill like 1/4 shows how many are running out of the most MSL will run at once. At the bottom: New Instance, and a strip of the people who built MSL. The header across the top of the right-hand side shows the selected instance's name, distro and state, with Terminal and Start (or Suspend/Resume, or Install if its distro isn't downloaded). Five tabs sit under the header. More on each below. More detail: The main window.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/tour.html#tabs","a":"A two-minute tour","h":"The five tabs","c":"Welcome","x":"Tab What it's for Applications The Linux GUI apps inside the instance, with their real icons. Open them, add them to your Applications folder, pin them to the Dock. Terminal A full terminal with a real Linux shell in it. Overview The instance's settings: SSH, CPUs and memory, disk size, the shared home folder. Tools The background service, generated apps, snapshots, maintenance and repair, and suspend/hibernate/shut down/remove. Sandbox Switches that cut the instance off from the network, your files, graphics or input — plus the Traffic Monitor.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/tour.html#other-windows","a":"A two-minute tour","h":"The other windows","c":"Welcome","x":"MSL Files (⌥⌘F) — a Finder work-alike that shows your Mac and your running Linux instances side by side, so you can drag files across. See MSL Files. Traffic Monitor — a live log of what MSL and an instance are saying to each other. Opened from the Sandbox tab. See Traffic Monitor. About MSL — what MSL is, its licences, the people who built it, and some stories. It's in the MSL menu. MSL Help — this. It's in the Help menu.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/tour.html#automatic","a":"A two-minute tour","h":"Things that happen on their own","c":"Welcome","x":"MSL's background service, mslhd, starts when you log in. It's what actually runs every instance, and it keeps running when the MSL window is closed — Linux apps you've added to your Dock work without MSL open. When you close the last terminal session on an instance, MSL suspends it a few seconds later so it stops using your processor. Opening a shell or an app wakes it again. See Idle instances. When your Mac sleeps, shuts down or logs out, MSL freezes every running instance first so nothing is mid-write. See Sleep, shutdown and battery.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html","a":"Words you'll see","h":null,"c":"Welcome","x":"A handful of words come up everywhere in MSL. Here they are in the order they start to matter. The glossary has the full A–Z.","s":"Instance, distro, image, guest, host, mslhd, mslgd — what each one means here."},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#instance","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Instance","c":"Welcome","x":"One Linux machine, with a name you chose — work, scratch, gimp-box. Each instance has its own settings, its own saved state, its own apps list and its own sandbox switches. You can have as many as you like.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#distro","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Distro","c":"Welcome","x":"Short for distribution — the flavour of Linux: Alpine, Debian, Ubuntu, Arch, Fedora, Rocky and more. Every instance is one distro, chosen when you create it. See Choosing a distro.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#image","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Image","c":"Welcome","x":"A distro's disk: one big file on your Mac that holds the whole Linux filesystem. You download it once per distro. Instances of the same distro share one disk Two Debian instances use the same Debian image. That keeps your Mac's disk from filling with copies — and it means only one instance of a given distro can run at a time, because two running machines writing to one disk would wreck it. Different distros run side by side happily. See Shared disks.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#guest-host","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Guest and host","c":"Welcome","x":"The guest is Linux, inside the virtual machine. The host is your Mac. \"Guest-side\" means something happens inside Linux; \"host-side\" means your Mac does it from outside, where Linux can't interfere.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#states","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Running, suspended, hibernated, stopped","c":"Welcome","x":"State Dot What it means Running green Linux is up. Starting… yellow On its way up or down. Suspended orange Frozen in memory. Resumes almost instantly, but still holds its memory on your Mac. Stopped grey Not running. It may have a hibernated session saved to disk — then starting it picks up exactly where it left off. The difference between suspend, hibernate and shut down is covered properly in Suspend, hibernate or shut down?.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#mslhd","a":"Words you'll see","h":"mslhd","c":"Welcome","x":"MSL's background service. It hosts every instance, starts at login, and keeps running when the MSL window is closed. If it isn't running, nothing can start. See The background service.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#mslgd","a":"Words you'll see","h":"mslgd","c":"Welcome","x":"MSL's own X11 display server — the part that turns a Linux app's drawing into a real Mac window. It was written from scratch for MSL. See Under the hood.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#snapshot","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Snapshot","c":"Welcome","x":"A saved copy of a whole running machine — memory and disk — that you can go back to. See Snapshots.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/vocabulary.html#gate","a":"Words you'll see","h":"Gate","c":"Welcome","x":"One of the four Sandbox switches: network, Mac home share, graphics, keyboard & mouse. A gate is either open or cut.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html","a":"Under the hood","h":null,"c":"Welcome","x":"You never need to know any of this to use MSL. But when something behaves in a way that seems odd, the reason is usually here.","s":"The moving parts — the VM, the service, the display server, the per-app processes — in plain words."},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html#vm","a":"Under the hood","h":"A real virtual machine","c":"Welcome","x":"Each running instance is a lightweight virtual machine made with Apple's Virtualization framework — the same technology behind most Mac virtualisation apps. Your Mac's processor runs Linux directly; nothing is translated or emulated. That's why a shell opens in about a second, and why Linux programs run at close to full speed. The virtual machine has no window of its own. Everything you see — the terminal, the app windows, the files — reaches your Mac through MSL, not through a screen.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html#service","a":"Under the hood","h":"The background service","c":"Welcome","x":"mslhd runs every virtual machine. It's installed as a login item, so it starts when you log in and keeps going when the MSL window is closed. The app, the msl command, and the Linux apps in your Dock all ask it to do things — start this, suspend that, open a shell — over a private connection on your Mac. Its log is at ~/Library/Logs/MSL/mslhd.log. See The background service.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html#vsock","a":"Under the hood","h":"Talking to Linux","c":"Welcome","x":"The Mac and each guest talk over vsock, a direct channel between a virtual machine and its host that doesn't go through the network at all. Shells, file access, app launching and window traffic all use it. This is why cutting the network in the Sandbox doesn't stop msl shells from working: detaching the network card doesn't touch vsock.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html#files","a":"Under the hood","h":"Your files, both ways","c":"Welcome","x":"Mac → Linux: your home folder is shared into every running instance at /mnt/mac. Linux → Mac: each running instance's filesystem is served back to your Mac as a network volume. Finder can see it, but it's named after a loopback address rather than the instance, which is why MSL Files exists: it shows each instance by name, beside your Mac's folders.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html#mslgd","a":"Under the hood","h":"Windows for Linux apps","c":"Welcome","x":"Linux GUI apps draw through a protocol called X11. On most Macs the X11 answer is XQuartz, which works well — but under it, every Linux app is a window belonging to XQuartz: one Dock tile, one menu bar, one app. MSL tried the other thing. mslgd is an X11 server written from scratch in Swift, drawing straight into AppKit and Core Graphics, so each Linux window becomes a real Mac window. And because macOS gives exactly one Dock tile per process, each Linux app runs in its own small helper process on your Mac — that's how it gets its own tile, icon, name and place in Mission Control. Honest footnote This is the part MSL exists to attempt, rather than a finished claim about how well it does it. Plenty of apps work nicely; some still draw oddly. The About window's Extras section has the full story, swearing included. ( ͡° ᴥ ͡°)","s":""},{"u":"welcome/how-it-works.html#bundles","a":"Under the hood","h":"Apps as Mac apps","c":"Welcome","x":"When you add a Linux app to your Applications folder, MSL writes a real, tiny .app into ~/Applications/MSL/<instance>/. Opening it asks the background service to start the instance if it needs to, then runs the Linux program. That's why Spotlight and the Dock can launch Linux apps with the MSL window closed.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/whats-ready.html","a":"What's ready, what's experimental","h":null,"c":"Welcome","x":"This is MSL-2.0.0 — still held together with love. Here's where things stand, so nothing catches you out.","s":"An honest map of what's solid and what's still experimental."},{"u":"welcome/whats-ready.html#solid","a":"What's ready, what's experimental","h":"Solid","c":"Welcome","x":"Installing distros and creating instances. Shells — in the app, in Terminal.app, over SSH, and with the msl command. Suspend, hibernate, shut down, snapshots, and protecting instances when your Mac sleeps or shuts down. CPU count, manual memory, disk sizing and automatic disk growth. Your home folder inside Linux at /mnt/mac, and MSL Files. The Sandbox gates and the MSL side of the Traffic Monitor. Checking and repairing a disk from the Mac, with automatic backups (needs one free tool — see Checking a disk).","s":""},{"u":"welcome/whats-ready.html#experimental","a":"What's ready, what's experimental","h":"Experimental","c":"Welcome","x":"Linux GUI apps as Mac windows. Many apps work well; some draw imperfectly. It's the most ambitious part of MSL and the most likely to surprise you. Everything in Experimental Features (Window ▸ Experimental Features). Every experiment lives there, and each says whether it starts on or off: - Open links on the Mac (on) — web and email links from Linux apps open in your Mac's browser and mail app, with attachments. - Show folders in MSL Files (on) — \"Open containing folder\" and friends open MSL Files instead of a Linux file manager. Nautilus, Dolphin and the rest still run when you start them yourself. - Linux app menus in the menu bar (off) — a Linux app's menus move into the Mac menu bar. Qt and KDE apps support it on their own; GTK apps need appmenu-gtk3-module in the instance. - Mac shortcuts, text navigation, screenshot keys and Option keys (off) — how Linux apps receive your keyboard. Experiments aren't promises: a future version may build one in properly, change it, or remove it, depending on what the community prefers. The list is specific to each version.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/using-help.html","a":"Using this guide","h":null,"c":"Welcome","x":"Open this guide from anywhere in MSL with Help ▸ MSL Help. From the keyboard, ⇧⌘? opens the Help menu in any Mac app — then choose MSL Help. The same menu also jumps straight to Getting Started, Keyboard Shortcuts, Troubleshooting, and what's ready or experimental.","s":"How search works, what the coloured boxes mean, and the keys for getting around."},{"u":"welcome/using-help.html#searching","a":"Using this guide","h":"Searching","c":"Welcome","x":"Type in the search field at the top of the sidebar. Results appear as you type, each one pointing at the exact section that matches — click one and the guide scrolls there, with your words highlighted. Search is deliberately forgiving: Every word counts. memory dynamic finds places that mention both. If nothing mentions all of them, you get the places that mention some. Half-typed words work. snaps already finds snapshots. Other forms work. restoring, restored and restores all find each other. Typos work. A letter missing, doubled or swapped with its neighbour still finds the word you meant. Everyday words work. ram finds memory, vm finds instances, delete finds remove, fsck finds disk repair, wifi finds the network gate, wireshark finds the Traffic Monitor. Keys work. Search for ⌘ or shortcut to find keyboard shortcuts. ⌘F Jump to the search field ⎋ Clear the search ⇧⌘? Open the Help menu, in any Mac app","s":""},{"u":"welcome/using-help.html#reading","a":"Using this guide","h":"Reading","c":"Welcome","x":"Each article starts with a one-line summary and an \"On this page\" list of its sections — click any of them to jump. Blue links go to other parts of the guide. Related at the bottom suggests where to read next, and the arrows at the very end step through the whole guide in order.","s":""},{"u":"welcome/using-help.html#callouts","a":"Using this guide","h":"The coloured boxes","c":"Welcome","x":"Tip A shortcut, or a nicer way to do something. Note Extra detail worth knowing. Important Something that changes how a feature behaves. Warning Something that can cost you time or work if you miss it. Danger Something you can't undo. Aside MSL being a person for a moment. (˶ᵔ ᵕ ᵔ˶)","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/install-distro.html","a":"Installing a distro","h":null,"c":"Getting Started","x":"Before an instance can start, its distro's disk image has to be on your Mac. It's a one-time download of a few hundred megabytes per distro, shared by every instance that uses that distro.","s":"Download a distro's disk image once, and every instance using it can start."},{"u":"getting-started/install-distro.html#from-the-app","a":"Installing a distro","h":"From the app","c":"Getting Started","x":"Select an instance whose distro isn't downloaded yet. Its header shows **Install Distro** where Start would normally be. Click **Install Distro. The button changes to Downloading…**, and the Applications tab shows the live progress line from the installer. When it finishes, a green banner says \"Distro installed — Instances using it can start now.\" The button becomes Start. The Applications tab offers the same **Install Distro** button while the instance has nothing installed. Verified as it downloads MSL checks each image against the checksum in its manifest before putting it in place, so a download that was cut short or corrupted is refused instead of becoming a broken disk.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/install-distro.html#from-terminal","a":"Installing a distro","h":"From Terminal","c":"Getting Started","x":"The New Instance sheet mentions this route, and it does exactly the same thing: shell msl install debian The name is one of alpine, debian, ubuntu, kali, arch, fedora, rocky, alma, centos, oracle, opensuse or nix.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/install-distro.html#where","a":"Installing a distro","h":"Where images live","c":"Getting Started","x":"In ~/Library/Application Support/MSL, one image file per distro. The Storage card on each instance's Overview tab shows how much space its image really takes on your Mac — often far less than its size, because images only take the space they've actually written. See Disk size and storage. Getting space back Deleting an image frees its space immediately, and the next start downloads it fresh. Every instance of that distro starts over from a clean disk, so only do it for distros whose files you don't need. See Reclaiming disk space.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/create-instance.html","a":"Creating an instance","h":null,"c":"Getting Started","x":"An instance is one Linux machine with its own name. Creating one takes two decisions and no time at all — nothing boots and nothing downloads until you start it.","s":"Name a new Linux machine and pick its distro. It's instant."},{"u":"getting-started/create-instance.html#steps","a":"Creating an instance","h":"Steps","c":"Getting Started","x":"Press ⌘N, choose File ▸ New Instance…, or click New Instance at the bottom of the sidebar. Type a Name. Pick a Distribution from the grid of six. Each tile has its colour, symbol and a one-line description. Click Create (or press Return). A green banner says \"Created name\", and the new instance appears in the sidebar, stopped.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/create-instance.html#names","a":"Creating an instance","h":"Naming rules","c":"Getting Started","x":"Letters, digits, hyphens and underscores only — work, gimp-box, test_2. Each name must be unique, ignoring case: Work and work count as the same. The line under the name field tells you as you type, turning orange if the name won't work: \"There's already an instance called …\" or \"Use letters, digits, hyphens and underscores only.\"","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/create-instance.html#not-downloaded","a":"Creating an instance","h":"If the distro isn't downloaded yet","c":"Getting Started","x":"The sheet says so under the distro grid. Creating the instance still works — you'll get an **Install Distro** button on it afterwards. See Installing a distro. Same distro, same disk The sheet says it too: instances of the same distro share one disk, so only one of them can run at a time. If you want two Linux machines up together, give them different distros. See Shared disks. Why have several instances at all? Each keeps its own settings — CPUs and memory, sandbox switches, SSH shortcut, apps added to your Dock. A sealed untrusted instance and an open work instance can be the same distro; you just can't run both at once.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-start.html","a":"Your first start and your Linux user","h":"Starting","c":"Getting Started","x":"Click Start in the instance's header. The dot turns yellow, then green. A first start takes a little longer than later ones; after that, starts are quick and resuming a suspended instance is nearly instant. You don't always need Start: opening the Terminal tab, clicking Find Apps, or opening a Linux app all start the instance for you. Start is greyed out? Either the instance is busy with something else (there's a small spinner beside it), or every running slot is taken — MSL runs at most four instances at once, and the capacity pill at the top of the sidebar turns orange when it's full. See Starting doesn't work.","s":"Starting an instance, and the one-time question about your Linux username and password."},{"u":"getting-started/first-start.html#user","a":"Your first start and your Linux user","h":"Your Linux user","c":"Getting Started","x":"The first time a distro starts, MSL asks you to create your everyday Linux account — a username and a password, just like setting up a fresh Linux install. It's an administrator account: it joins the distro's admin group, and sudo asks for its password. In the app Clicking Start, Find Apps or opening a Linux app on a distro that has no account yet brings up Create your Linux account first: Username — it's filled in with your Mac's short name if that works on Linux. Change it if you like; it doesn't need to match. Password, and Retype password. Create Account. MSL starts the instance, creates the account, and then carries on with whatever you were doing. A banner confirms it. Cancel leaves the distro without an account; you'll be asked again next time. In a terminal The first interactive shell — the Terminal tab, Terminal.app, or msl work — asks in the terminal itself, with a small ASCII kitty: Welcome to MSL - debian! Please create a default UNIX user account. The username does not need to match your Mac username. It becomes your login for debian from now on, and its password is the one sudo asks for. (root is always available from the Mac: msl <instance> -u root) New UNIX username: Type a username, then a password twice. Nothing appears while you type the password — that's normal.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-start.html#user-rules","a":"Your first start and your Linux user","h":"The rules","c":"Getting Started","x":"A username starts with a lowercase letter or an underscore, and uses only lowercase letters, digits, _ or - — at most 32 characters. Names the system already uses (root, nobody, the built-in msl account, and so on) are refused, and so is a name that already exists in that distro. From then on, every shell on that distro — in the app, in Terminal.app, from the msl command, over SSH — logs you in as that user. One user per distro, not per instance The account belongs to the distro's disk, which every instance of that distro shares. Create it once on work, and scratch (also Debian) already has it. If it can't be created The sheet or the terminal says why. From the terminal, that one shell runs as root instead, and MSL asks again next time. More on users, root and sudo: Linux users and root.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-start.html#greeting","a":"Your first start and your Linux user","h":"The greeting","c":"Getting Started","x":"Every interactive shell opens with a short banner — msl@debian in colour — and a dimmed one-line greeting under it, never the same one twice in a row. Some are sweet, some are cursed, one is extremely tsundere about your uptime. None of them are emoji; MSL speaks kaomoji. (≧▽≦)","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/choosing-distro.html","a":"Choosing a distro","h":null,"c":"Getting Started","x":"MSL runs twelve distributions. Each has its own colour and symbol everywhere in the app, so you can tell them apart at a glance. Distro In the app Installs software with Good for Alpine \"Tiny and fast. The default.\" apk add Small, quick shells; it's what MSL picks first. Debian \"Stable and familiar.\" apt install Things that should just keep working. Ubuntu \"Debian, with more batteries.\" apt install Following tutorials, which mostly assume Ubuntu. Kali \"Debian, with security tooling.\" apt install Security and penetration-testing tools. Arch \"Rolling release, current packages.\" pacman -S The newest versions of everything. Fedora \"Recent kernels and toolchains.\" dnf install Up-to-date compilers and developer tools. Rocky \"Enterprise Linux, community-built.\" dnf install Matching servers that run Red Hat Enterprise Linux. AlmaLinux \"Enterprise Linux, forever free.\" dnf install The same, from a different community. CentOS Stream \"Where Enterprise Linux is made.\" dnf install Seeing what the next Enterprise Linux release will contain. Oracle Linux \"Enterprise Linux from Oracle.\" dnf install Oracle's database and cloud tooling. openSUSE \"SUSE's community release.\" zypper install SUSE's packages and conventions. Nix \"Nix packages on a Debian base.\" nix profile install Environments you can rebuild exactly.","s":"Twelve distributions, from tiny Alpine to the Enterprise Linux family — what each is like and which to pick."},{"u":"getting-started/choosing-distro.html#releases","a":"Choosing a distro","h":"Which releases","c":"Getting Started","x":"The current images are Alpine 3.24, Debian 13, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Kali (rolling), Arch Linux ARM (rolling), Fedora 44, Rocky Linux 10, AlmaLinux 10, CentOS Stream 10, Oracle Linux 10 and openSUSE Leap 16.0. Nix is the Nix package manager on Debian 13.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/choosing-distro.html#unsure","a":"Choosing a distro","h":"If you're not sure","c":"Getting Started","x":"For Linux GUI apps, Debian or Ubuntu have the widest choice of desktop programs. For a quick shell and command-line tools, Alpine is tiny and starts fastest. For the latest versions of things, Arch or Fedora. For matching a Red Hat Enterprise Linux server, Rocky or AlmaLinux. Why there's no Red Hat Enterprise Linux RHEL itself needs a Red Hat subscription, and Red Hat's freely shareable base image is missing tools MSL depends on. Rocky, AlmaLinux, CentOS Stream and Oracle Linux are all built from the same sources. Nix isn't NixOS The Nix instance is Debian with the Nix package manager installed — nix-shell, nix profile and reproducible environments all work, and apt does too. It doesn't boot NixOS's own system configuration. You aren't stuck with the choice: make another instance with a different distro any time. Different distros can run side by side; instances of the same distro can't. One quirk of Alpine Alpine uses a leaner C library (musl) than the others (glibc). Almost everything packaged for Alpine works perfectly; software downloaded as a ready-made Linux binary from a website sometimes expects glibc and won't run. If that happens, a Debian or Ubuntu instance will run it.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-app.html","a":"Your first Linux app","h":null,"c":"Getting Started","x":"A fresh distro has few or no GUI apps. Here's how to add one and open it — using GIMP, the image editor, as the example.","s":"Install a Linux GUI app, find it in MSL, and open it as a Mac window."},{"u":"getting-started/first-app.html#install","a":"Your first Linux app","h":"1. Install it inside Linux","c":"Getting Started","x":"Open the Terminal tab and install it with the distro's package manager: Distro Command Alpine sudo apk add gimp Debian, Ubuntu, Kali, Nix sudo apt install gimp Arch sudo pacman -S gimp Fedora sudo dnf install gimp Rocky, AlmaLinux, CentOS Stream, Oracle Linux GIMP isn't packaged for Enterprise Linux 10, even in EPEL — sudo dnf install firefox works the same way openSUSE sudo zypper install gimp sudo asks for your Linux user's password — the one you chose when you set it up.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-app.html#find","a":"Your first Linux app","h":"2. Find it in MSL","c":"Getting Started","x":"Switch to the Applications tab and click Find Apps (or Refresh if the grid already has apps). MSL reads every installed app's .desktop entry from inside Linux — the same thing a Linux desktop's app menu reads — and shows each one with its real icon.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-app.html#open","a":"Your first Linux app","h":"3. Open it","c":"Getting Started","x":"Double-click GIMP's tile, or hover over it and click the ▶ button. A few moments later it opens as a Mac window, with its own tile in the Dock and its own name in the menu bar.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/first-app.html#keep","a":"Your first Linux app","h":"4. Make it feel at home","c":"Getting Started","x":"Add to Applications puts a real GIMP app in ~/Applications/MSL, so Spotlight finds it. See Adding apps to your Applications folder. Pin to Dock keeps it in your Dock. See Pinning to the Dock. Both work with the MSL window closed. Nothing showing up? If Find Apps says \"No GUI applications found\", nothing installed so far has a .desktop entry — command-line programs don't. Install a GUI app, then Refresh. More in Linux apps won't appear or open.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/custom-images.html","a":"Custom images","h":null,"c":"Getting Started","x":"Besides MSL's own distros, an instance can run a Linux image you made yourself — any distro, any setup, your dotfiles baked in. A custom image is just a folder with three files, and MSL runs it the way it runs its own.","s":"Run your own Linux: a folder with a kernel, an initramfs and a disk."},{"u":"getting-started/custom-images.html#start-from-distro","a":"Custom images","h":"The quickest way: start from a distro","c":"Getting Started","x":"In New Instance, open Start a custom image from a distro, pick an installed distro, give the image a name and choose Create Image. MSL copies that distro into Custom Images as your own image. The copy is instant and takes no extra space until it changes, and everything MSL needs is already inside. Then create an instance from it, install whatever you like, and it stays that way — changes to your image never touch the distro it came from. From Terminal: msl images new my-image --distro debian, then msl new work --distro custom:my-image.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/custom-images.html#from-scratch","a":"Custom images","h":"Making one from scratch","c":"Getting Started","x":"Choose Open Folder under Custom images in New Instance (or run msl images open). MSL opens Custom Images in Finder, with a README and a guest kit inside. Make a folder — its name is the image's id, in letters, digits, - and _ — and put in: File What it is Image The kernel: a raw arm64 kernel Image, not a compressed vmlinuz. initramfs An initramfs that can mount ext4 on an NVMe disk. rootfs.img The root filesystem: a raw ext4 image with no partition table. image.json Optional: a name, a description, and a kernelCommandLine if yours needs one. Choose Rescan, and the image appears. If something's wrong — a compressed kernel, a partitioned disk, a missing file — MSL lists the image with exactly what to fix. MSL's guest daemons MSL talks to Linux through small programs inside the image. An image without them boots, but MSL can't reach it: no terminal, no apps, no files. Run _MSL Guest Kit/provision/provision-msl.sh as root inside the image before packing it (in a container or chroot). Starting from a distro avoids this entirely.","s":""},{"u":"getting-started/custom-images.html#remove","a":"Custom images","h":"Removing one","c":"Getting Started","x":"Right-click an instance and choose Remove … Image. Its instances go; the folder and its files stay — MSL never deletes anything in Custom Images. Delete the folder yourself when you're done with it. Good to know Instances of the same image share its disk, so only one runs at a time. Only arm64 Linux runs on Apple silicon.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/main-window.html","a":"The main window","h":null,"c":"The Main Window","x":"MSL's main window is a sidebar on the left listing your instances, and a detail pane on the right for the one you've selected.","s":"One sidebar of instances, one detail pane with five tabs."},{"u":"main-window/main-window.html#detail","a":"The main window","h":"The detail pane","c":"The Main Window","x":"From top to bottom: The header — the instance's name, distro and state, and its main buttons. See The instance header. The tab bar — Applications · Terminal · Overview · Tools · Sandbox. The tab itself. Applications Linux GUI apps inside the instance — open, add to Applications, pin to Dock. Terminal A real Linux shell, right in the window. Overview SSH, CPUs and memory, storage, the shared home folder. Tools The service, snapshots, maintenance and repair, suspend and shut down. Sandbox Four switches that cut the instance off, and the Traffic Monitor. Tabs start on Applications Selecting a different instance opens it on its Applications tab. Terminal sessions aren't lost when you switch — each instance keeps its own shell running in the background until you end it.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/main-window.html#empty","a":"The main window","h":"With nothing selected","c":"The Main Window","x":"The detail pane says \"No instance selected\" and offers New Instance…. If MSL's background service isn't running, it says that instead and offers Start it — see The background service.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/main-window.html#refresh","a":"The main window","h":"Refreshing","c":"The Main Window","x":"MSL keeps itself up to date: instance states refresh every few seconds on their own. View ▸ Refresh (⌘R) asks right away, which is handy after doing something from Terminal with the msl command.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/sidebar.html","a":"The sidebar","h":"Instance rows","c":"The Main Window","x":"Each row shows: The distro badge — a rounded square in the distro's colour with its symbol. Click it to give the instance its own logo. The name, and the distro under it. A status dot on the right: green running, orange suspended, yellow starting, grey stopped. While the instance is busy — starting, suspending, restoring a snapshot — a small spinner replaces the dot. Click a row to show that instance in the detail pane.","s":"Your instances, how many are running, New Instance, and the people who built MSL."},{"u":"main-window/sidebar.html#capacity","a":"The sidebar","h":"The capacity pill","c":"The Main Window","x":"Beside the Instances heading, a pill like 2/4 shows how many instances are running (or suspended) out of the most MSL will run at once. It turns orange when every slot is taken, and hovering over it explains. Suspended instances count because they still hold their memory on your Mac. See How many can run at once.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/sidebar.html#context-menu","a":"The sidebar","h":"Right-click menu","c":"The Main Window","x":"Right-click (or Control-click) any instance: Item What it does Start Starts or resumes it. Only shown when it isn't running, greyed out when every slot is taken. Suspend Freezes it in memory. Only shown while running. Shut Down Stops it straight away. Only shown while running — read the note on Shut Down first. Open in Terminal.app Opens a shell on it in a Terminal.app window. Show Apps in Finder Opens the folder of Mac apps MSL has made for it. Change Logo… Pick its badge. Same as clicking the badge — see Instance logos. Remove… Removes the instance. See the warning below. Remove happens straight away Despite the \"…\", Remove… doesn't ask first. It unregisters the instance, deletes its saved state and snapshots, and deletes the Mac apps made for it. The distro's disk image — and every file in it — is untouched. See Removing an instance.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/sidebar.html#new","a":"The sidebar","h":"New Instance","c":"The Main Window","x":"At the bottom of the list. Same as ⌘N. See Creating an instance.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/sidebar.html#built-by","a":"The sidebar","h":"Built by","c":"The Main Window","x":"Under that, a strip of small avatars: everyone who has landed a commit in MSL's repository, read live from GitHub. Hover to see their names; click to open the contributors page. If GitHub can't be reached, the strip quietly doesn't appear — nothing in the sidebar ever nags about it. The About window's Contributors section has everyone in full, each with their own colour. ٩(ˊᗜˋ*)و","s":""},{"u":"main-window/instance-header.html","a":"The instance header","h":null,"c":"The Main Window","x":"Across the top of the detail pane: a large distro badge, the instance's name, and \"Distro · State\" under it. On the right, the buttons — which ones depends on the state.","s":"The name, the state, and the buttons that change with it: Start, Suspend, Resume, Install."},{"u":"main-window/instance-header.html#buttons","a":"The instance header","h":"The buttons","c":"The Main Window","x":"Terminal is always there. It switches to the Terminal tab, which opens a shell (starting the instance if needed). The second button changes: When the instance is… You see Clicking it… Stopped, distro downloaded Start starts it (or resumes a hibernated session). Stopped, distro not downloaded **Install Distro** downloads the image. Shows Downloading… meanwhile. Running Suspend ▾ freezes it. Suspended Resume ▾ unfreezes it, almost instantly. Suspend and Resume are split buttons: click the main part for the action, or the small arrow for the same menu as right-clicking the instance in the sidebar — Shut Down, Open in Terminal.app, Show Apps in Finder and Remove…. Why Start is sometimes grey It's disabled while the instance is busy (a spinner shows beside the buttons) and when every running slot is already taken. Hovering over it says which.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/instance-header.html#busy","a":"The instance header","h":"Busy","c":"The Main Window","x":"While MSL works on an instance — starting, suspending, hibernating, restoring a snapshot — a small spinner appears in the header and on its sidebar row, and the buttons wait until it's done.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/instance-logos.html","a":"Instance logos","h":null,"c":"The Main Window","x":"MSL draws every instance as its distro's symbol in its distro's colour. That is right until you have four Debians — then the sidebar is four identical rows and the name is the only thing telling them apart. Click an instance's badge — in the sidebar, or the big one in the instance header — and pick something else. Change Logo… in the right-click menu opens the same panel.","s":"Give an instance its own badge — a symbol and colour, or a picture of your own."},{"u":"main-window/instance-logos.html#picking","a":"Instance logos","h":"What you can pick","c":"The Main Window","x":"Faction Sixty symbols in five groups — Creatures, Banners, Work, Places and Marks. Click the one you've already chosen to clear it. Colour Twelve colours. With none chosen, the badge keeps the distro's own. Open… A picture of your own, from anywhere on your Mac.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/instance-logos.html#pictures","a":"Instance logos","h":"Pictures","c":"The Main Window","x":"Open… takes any image your Mac can read. MSL copies it in rather than remembering where it was, so moving, renaming or deleting the original later doesn't leave you with a blank badge. The copy is squared off from the middle and stored small — a badge is 44 points at most, so there's no point keeping a twelve-megapixel photo for it. A picture covers the symbol, so the faction grid dims while one is showing. Remove Picture brings the symbol back without losing the faction and colour you chose.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/instance-logos.html#reset","a":"Instance logos","h":"Reset to Default","c":"The Main Window","x":"At the top, beside Open…. It drops everything — symbol, colour and picture — and the instance goes back to looking like its distro. It's only on your Mac The logo is MSL's own note about the instance, not something inside Linux. It isn't in snapshots, it doesn't travel in a shipment, and removing the instance forgets it — so a new instance that reuses the name starts plain rather than inheriting a badge.","s":""},{"u":"main-window/banners.html","a":"Banners and messages","h":null,"c":"The Main Window","x":"When something finishes or goes wrong, MSL says so in a banner that slides down from the top of the window, rather than an alert you'd have to click away. Banner Icon Goes away Success — \"Created work\", \"Saved 'before-upgrade'\" green tick by itself, after a few seconds Information — \"No GUI applications found in work\" blue info by itself, after a few seconds Error — \"Couldn't start work\", with the reason under it orange triangle only when you close it Errors stay on purpose: an error you missed is one you'll hit again. Close any banner with the × on its right. Errors with a long reason The detail under an error is the actual reason from MSL's background service. If it's cryptic, the service's log usually has more — see Logs and msl doctor.","s":"The notices that drop in from the top of the window, and which ones stay."},{"u":"main-window/banners.html#in-cards","a":"Banners and messages","h":"Messages inside cards","c":"The Main Window","x":"Some parts of the app report in place instead, right where you clicked: the Maintenance card shows its results in a coloured box under its buttons (green, orange, red or blue), the Storage card shows problems in orange under its controls, and the Terminal tab shows \"Session ended\" over the terminal.","s":""},{"u":"applications/apps-tab.html","a":"The Applications tab","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"The Applications tab shows the graphical apps installed inside an instance — the programs a Linux desktop would list in its app menu. It's the tab MSL opens first.","s":"A grid of the Linux GUI apps inside an instance, with their real icons."},{"u":"applications/apps-tab.html#finding","a":"The Applications tab","h":"Finding apps","c":"Applications Tab","x":"The first time, the tab is empty with a Find Apps button. Clicking it: starts the instance if it isn't running (\"Starting an instance for the first time can take a moment\"), reads every installed app's .desktop entry from inside Linux — the same file a Linux desktop reads to put an app in its menu, fetches each app's icon, and fills the grid. After that the button says Refresh, and \"Updated 5 minutes ago\" beside it says when the list was last read. Refresh after installing or removing software inside Linux. Only apps with windows Command-line programs have no .desktop entry, so they don't appear here — use them from the Terminal. If nothing at all turns up, MSL says \"No GUI applications found\" and suggests installing some. Or just type its name Any shell MSL opens — the Terminal tab, or msl in Terminal.app — can start GUI apps too: type gnome-chess or gimp at the prompt and it opens as a Mac window. From a Mac terminal, msl debian gimp does the same in one step.","s":""},{"u":"applications/apps-tab.html#toolbar","a":"The Applications tab","h":"The toolbar","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Search — \"Search N apps\". Matches app names and descriptions, and the packaging: typing flatpak shows only Flatpak apps. Source picker — All · System · User · Flatpak · Snap. It only appears when the instance has apps from more than one source. See Where apps come from. Find Apps / Refresh on the right.","s":""},{"u":"applications/apps-tab.html#tiles","a":"The Applications tab","h":"Each tile","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Part Means The icon The app's own icon, drawn from its vector artwork when it has one, so it stays crisp. The name Up to two lines. A small grey badge Where it came from — only shown for User, Flatpak and Snap apps. A blue tick in the corner It's in your Applications folder. A small spinner in the corner MSL is fetching a sharper icon for it. Hover over a tile to reveal three small buttons: ▶ open, folder add to or remove from Applications, and pin pin or unpin from the Dock. Hovering also shows the app's description.","s":""},{"u":"applications/apps-tab.html#context-menu","a":"The Applications tab","h":"Right-click","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Open, Add to Applications (or Remove from Applications), Pin to Dock (or Unpin from Dock), and — greyed out at the bottom — the exact command the app runs, for the curious.","s":""},{"u":"applications/opening-apps.html","a":"Opening a Linux app","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"Double-click a tile, click its ▶, or right-click and choose Open. If the instance isn't running, it starts first. A moment later the app appears as a normal Mac window — with its own tile in the Dock, its own name in the menu bar, and its own place in Mission Control and ⌘-Tab.","s":"Double-click a tile, and a Linux app opens as a Mac window with its own Dock tile."},{"u":"applications/opening-apps.html#one-path","a":"Opening a Linux app","h":"Three ways in, one path","c":"Applications Tab","x":"From How The Applications tab Double-click or ▶. Spotlight or Finder Once you've added it to Applications. The Dock Once you've pinned it. When an app has been added to Applications, opening it from the grid goes through that same Mac app — so if it works from one place, it works from all of them. The MSL window doesn't need to stay open Linux apps are run by MSL's background service, not by this window. Close MSL and the apps carry on.","s":""},{"u":"applications/opening-apps.html#fails","a":"Opening a Linux app","h":"If it doesn't open","c":"Applications Tab","x":"An orange banner says \"Couldn't start app\" with the reason. The usual causes, and what to do, are in Linux apps won't appear or open.","s":""},{"u":"applications/app-menu.html","a":"The app right-click menu","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"Right-click any app in the Applications tab (or Control-click, or a two-finger click) for everything MSL can do with that one app.","s":"Everything you can do to one app from its tile in the Applications tab."},{"u":"applications/app-menu.html#items","a":"The app right-click menu","h":"What is on it","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Item What it does Open Launches it. The same as double-clicking. Add to Launchpad Makes a real Mac app for it in your Applications folder, so Spotlight and Launchpad find it. Remove from Launchpad Takes that away again. Add to Dock Keeps it in the Dock, so it is one click away whether or not MSL is open. Pin to Top Moves it to the front of this list. For the handful you actually use. Graphics Whether this app's OpenGL runs on the GPU or the CPU. Copy Command The command its desktop entry runs, for pasting into a terminal. Copy Launch Command The same, wrapped in msl <instance> ... so it runs from your Mac. Refresh This List Scans the instance for applications again. The command itself is shown at the bottom of the menu, so you can see what an app will actually run without copying it anywhere.","s":""},{"u":"applications/app-menu.html#graphics","a":"The app right-click menu","h":"Graphics, per app","c":"Applications Tab","x":"The Graphics submenu is the useful one when something will not render: Choice Means Default Follow the GPU acceleration switch in Experimental Features. GPU Always the Mac's graphics hardware. OpenGL 4.1 at most. CPU Always software rendering. OpenGL 4.5, slower for heavy 3D. This is how you keep one app working while everything else uses the GPU — Blender wants OpenGL newer than the GPU path offers, so pinning it to CPU is the fix. It applies from that app's next launch.","s":""},{"u":"applications/app-menu.html#badges","a":"The app right-click menu","h":"The badges on a tile","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Badge Means A tick It is in your Applications folder. A pin It is pinned to the top of this list. A spinner MSL is fetching a better icon for it from the instance. System, Flatpak, Snap, User Where the app came from. CPU or GPU next to that This app has been pinned to one graphics path.","s":""},{"u":"applications/app-menu.html#custom-command","a":"The app right-click menu","h":"Changing how one app starts","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Custom Launch Command replaces what MSL runs when you open this app — for a game that needs a particular engine build, or a binary that wants an environment variable. See Changing how an app starts.","s":""},{"u":"applications/add-to-applications.html","a":"Adding apps to your Applications folder","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"Add to Applications — the folder button on a tile, or the right-click menu — writes a small, real Mac app for the Linux program into ~/Applications/MSL/<instance>/. A banner confirms it: \"App added to your Applications — It's in ~/Applications/MSL/instance and searchable from Spotlight.\" The tile gets a blue tick. From then on it behaves like any other Mac app: find it with Spotlight, open it from Finder, drag it wherever you like. Opening it starts the instance if needed, then the app — with the MSL window closed or open.","s":"Turn a Linux app into a real Mac app that Spotlight, Finder and Launchpad can find."},{"u":"applications/add-to-applications.html#icon","a":"Adding apps to your Applications folder","h":"A sharper icon","c":"Applications Tab","x":"The grid uses modest icons so it fills quickly. When you add an app, MSL fetches the best icon the app has from inside Linux, so the Mac app looks right at every size. That can mean starting the instance for a moment. When every slot is full If starting the instance would go over the four-instance limit, MSL still adds the app, using the icon it already has, and says \"Using app's existing icon\". Add it again later for the sharp one.","s":""},{"u":"applications/add-to-applications.html#remove","a":"Adding apps to your Applications folder","h":"Removing it","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Remove from Applications deletes that Mac app, and unpins it from the Dock if it was pinned. Deleting the app in Finder does the same thing. Nothing inside Linux changes — the Linux program stays installed. Tidy up after an instance Removing an instance deletes every Mac app made for it, so nothing is left pointing at a machine that no longer exists. See Removing an instance.","s":""},{"u":"applications/pin-to-dock.html","a":"Pinning to the Dock","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"Pin to Dock — the pin button on a tile, or the right-click menu — puts the app in your Dock. Unpin from Dock takes it out. Pinning needs a real Mac app to point at, so if the app isn't in your Applications folder yet, MSL adds it first. Pinning implying \"add\" is less surprising than refusing. The Dock restarts once macOS has no way for an app to add a Dock tile directly, so MSL updates the Dock's settings and restarts it — once per pin or unpin. You'll see the Dock blink away and come back. That's expected. Unpinning never starts an instance. Pinned apps work with the MSL window closed.","s":"Keep a Linux app in your Dock, like any Mac app."},{"u":"applications/app-sources.html","a":"Where apps come from","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"Linux apps can be installed several ways, and MSL finds all of them: Source Installed by Badge System the distro's package manager (apt, apk, pacman, dnf) none — most apps are these User you, for your Linux user only (~/.local/share/applications) User Flatpak flatpak install Flatpak Snap snap install Snap Only non-system apps get a badge; on most instances every app is a system one, and a badge on all of them would be noise.","s":"System, User, Flatpak and Snap — and how to tell two copies of one app apart."},{"u":"applications/app-sources.html#duplicates","a":"Where apps come from","h":"The same app twice","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Install GIMP from the package manager and from Flatpak and you'll see two GIMP tiles — one plain, one badged Flatpak. They're genuinely different installs, and each can be added to Applications or pinned separately. When there's more than one source, a picker appears in the toolbar — All · System · User · Flatpak · Snap — to show just one. Typing flatpak or snap in the search field does the same.","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":null,"c":"Applications Tab","x":"Linux app windows are drawn by mslgd, MSL's own display server, as real Mac windows. This is the most experimental part of MSL — here's what to expect.","s":"How Linux app windows behave on a Mac — Dock, menu bar, closing, copy and paste."},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#own-app","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"Each app is its own Mac app","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Its own Dock tile, with its own icon. Its own name in the menu bar when it's in front. Its own place in Mission Control and ⌘-Tab. That works because each Linux app runs in its own small helper process on your Mac: macOS gives exactly one Dock tile per process.","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#input","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"Input","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Keyboard, clicking, dragging, the scroll wheel and trackpad scrolling, and middle-click all reach the app.","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#closing","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"Closing","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Clicking a window's close button asks the app to close that window, the same as its own close button would on Linux — so an app with unsaved work gets its chance to ask \"Save changes?\".","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#clipboard","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"Copy and paste","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Not across apps yet Copy and paste works within one Linux app. Between two different Linux apps, or between a Linux app and a Mac app, it doesn't yet — that bridge isn't built. To move text across for now, save it to a file in your home folder (Linux sees it at /mnt/mac).","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#awake","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"While apps are open","c":"Applications Tab","x":"An instance with a Linux app open stays awake — it isn't suspended for being idle while an app is running.","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#shutdown","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"When the Mac shuts down","c":"Applications Tab","x":"Save Linux work before shutting down When your Mac shuts down, restarts or logs out, MSL asks open Linux apps to close first, so apps with unsaved work can ask to save. Then it saves the instance. But the windows themselves don't come back afterwards — even though the instance resumes, the connection each window had to your Mac is gone. See Sleep, shutdown and battery.","s":""},{"u":"applications/linux-windows.html#sandbox","a":"Living with Linux windows","h":"Things that affect windows","c":"Applications Tab","x":"The Graphics gate on the Sandbox tab stops new windows opening; open ones keep drawing. The Keyboard & mouse gate stops input reaching them; they keep drawing. If an app draws strangely Some apps use corners of X11 that mslgd doesn't draw perfectly yet. That's mslgd's fault, not yours, and not the app's. Trying the same app from another source (a Flatpak instead of the package, say) occasionally helps. ( ˘•ω•˘ )","s":""},{"u":"terminal/terminal-tab.html","a":"The Terminal tab","h":null,"c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"The Terminal tab is a complete terminal with a real Linux shell in it — job control, colours, line editing and full-screen programs like vim and htop all work, because it's a real terminal talking to a real shell.","s":"A full terminal inside the window, with a real Linux login shell in it."},{"u":"terminal/terminal-tab.html#opening","a":"The Terminal tab","h":"Opening a shell","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Just open the tab. It connects straight away, starting the instance if it isn't running — or click Terminal in the header from any tab. If the instance can't start (every running slot is taken, say), the reason is printed right there in the terminal, where you're already looking.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/terminal-tab.html#appearance","a":"The Terminal tab","h":"Appearance","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"The terminal uses the system's monospaced font and follows light and dark mode with the rest of MSL, rather than being a black rectangle in a light window.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/terminal-tab.html#toolbar","a":"The Terminal tab","h":"The toolbar buttons","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"While the Terminal tab is showing, two buttons appear in the window's toolbar: End Session — closes the shell. It sends the same hang-up signal a terminal window sends when you close it, so programs inside wind down the way they normally would. Open in Terminal — opens another shell on the same instance in Terminal.app. See Using Terminal.app.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/terminal-tab.html#ended","a":"The Terminal tab","h":"When the session ends","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Type exit, or end the session, and a panel appears over the terminal: \"Session ended\" (or \"Session ended (exit n)\" if it ended with an error), and a Reconnect button. The scrollback stays visible behind it, so anything printed on the way out is still readable.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/terminal-tab.html#persistent","a":"The Terminal tab","h":"Sessions keep running","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Each instance has its own terminal session, kept alive in the background. Switch tabs, switch instances, come back — the shell is exactly where you left it, scrollback and all. Removing an instance ends its session. An open shell keeps the instance awake While any shell is open on an instance, it isn't suspended for being idle. When the last one closes, MSL suspends it a few seconds later.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/terminal-app.html","a":"Using Terminal.app","h":null,"c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Prefer Terminal.app, or want several shells side by side? Any of these opens a Terminal.app window running a shell on the instance: Open in Terminal.app in the instance's right-click menu in the sidebar (or the header's Suspend/Resume menu). Open in Terminal in the toolbar while the Terminal tab is showing. The first time, macOS asks MSL opens the window by asking Terminal.app to run a command, so the first time macOS asks whether MSL may control Terminal. Allow it. If you said no by accident, turn it back on in System Settings ▸ Privacy & Security ▸ Automation.","s":"Open an instance's shell in Terminal.app, or type msl there yourself."},{"u":"terminal/terminal-app.html#typing","a":"Using Terminal.app","h":"Or just type it","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"In any terminal app — Terminal, iTerm, whatever you use — this opens a shell on an instance called work: shell msl run work msl work does the same thing — the instance name alone is the short form, and it is what the app's Copy Shell Command used to put on your clipboard. Typing msl on its own prints what you have and what to type next, rather than opening anything. Every new tab can do the same; each is its own shell. And one-off commands work like ssh: shell msl run work uname -a msl run work ls -la /mnt/mac/Desktop The exit code comes back too, so msl fits in scripts. Everything else it can do is in The msl command.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/linux-users.html","a":"Linux users and root","h":"Your default user","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"The first interactive shell on a distro asks you to create a user — see Your first start. That user becomes the default login for the distro: every shell, in the app or out of it, logs in as them. It belongs to the distro, not the instance, because instances of a distro share one disk. Set it up once on any Debian instance, and every Debian instance has it.","s":"Your everyday Linux user, sudo and its password, root, and the extra 'msl' user."},{"u":"terminal/linux-users.html#sudo","a":"Linux users and root","h":"sudo","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Your account is an administrator, the usual Linux way: it's in the distro's admin group — sudo on Debian, Ubuntu, Kali and Nix, wheel on the rest — and sudo asks for its password: shell sudo apt install gimp [sudo] password for abi: After that, sudo remembers you for a few minutes, so a run of commands only asks once.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/linux-users.html#root","a":"Linux users and root","h":"Root","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"From the Mac, -u picks the user — and -u root needs no password, because the Mac is the machine's owner. It's the same escape hatch WSL has with wsl -u root: shell msl work -u root msl work -u root apk add htop","s":""},{"u":"terminal/linux-users.html#forgot-password","a":"Linux users and root","h":"Forgot your password?","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Set a new one as root, from the Mac: shell msl work -u root passwd abi Replace abi with your username. It asks for the new password twice.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/linux-users.html#one-off","a":"Linux users and root","h":"One-off commands","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"msl work <command> runs as the default user too, once there is one — before that, as root.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/linux-users.html#msl-user","a":"Linux users and root","h":"The msl user","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Every distro also has an ordinary unprivileged user called msl, for when you want a clean account with no admin rights: msl work -u msl.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/ssh.html","a":"Connecting over SSH","h":null,"c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"The Connect over SSH card on the Overview tab gives every instance a ready-made SSH shortcut. MSL does all the setup; you get one command: shell ssh msl-work It works anywhere SSH does — a terminal, scp, rsync, and editors like VS Code's Remote-SSH, which will list msl-work as a host.","s":"One command, nothing to configure — for scp, VS Code Remote-SSH, and anything else that speaks SSH."},{"u":"terminal/ssh.html#what","a":"Connecting over SSH","h":"What MSL sets up","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Its own key, at ~/Library/Application Support/MSL/ssh/msl_ed25519. MSL never touches your personal ~/.ssh keys — a key MSL owns can be replaced without affecting anything else you use. The key inside Linux, for your Linux account — so ssh logs you in as you. (Before the distro has an account, it uses the built-in msl one; Set up again switches it once yours exists.) The SSH server inside Linux, started. A shortcut — a Host msl-work entry in ~/.ssh/config. Nothing else in that file is touched. Then it checks the address actually answers before calling it ready.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/ssh.html#automatic","a":"Connecting over SSH","h":"Automatically","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"Set this up automatically when an instance starts (on by default, and it applies to all instances) does all of the above whenever an instance starts, so the shortcut is just there. Turn it off and each card offers Set up now instead, which works while the instance is running.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/ssh.html#card","a":"Connecting over SSH","h":"Reading the card","c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"You see Means \"Not set up yet\" Nothing's been done. Set up now needs the instance running. \"Setting up…\" Working on it. Green tick, \"Ready.\" Checked just now and answering. Open Terminal connects in Terminal.app. Grey clock, \"Last known details…\" From the last time it ran — it's checked again once the instance is up. Orange triangle, \"isn't answering on port 22\" Set up, but not reachable right now. Under the command: the Address (user and IP), the Key path, and the Host key fingerprint. Copy copies the command; Copy all details copies everything; Set up again redoes it all. Addresses move; the shortcut follows An instance's address can change between runs. MSL rechecks it at start and rewrites the ~/.ssh/config entry in place, so ssh msl-work keeps working. Not answering? The usual reason is the Network gate on the Sandbox tab — cutting it unplugs the network SSH uses. The app's own terminal still works regardless. More in SSH won't connect. From the command line, msl ssh work connects — setting everything up first if it's the first time — and msl ssh work --print just prints the command.","s":""},{"u":"terminal/command-line.html","a":"The msl command","h":null,"c":"Terminal & Shells","x":"The app and the msl command are two faces of the same thing: both ask MSL's background service to do the work. Anything you do in one shows up in the other. The full list is in the reference — msl command reference — but these are the ones you'll use: Command Does msl What you have and what to type next. msl run work A shell on work. msl work is the short form. msl run work <command> Runs one command, like ssh. Its exit code comes back. msl run A shell on whichever instance you used last. msl fedora An instance named after a distro just works — no setup needed. msl run work -u root A shell as root. msl list Every instance. msl list online Every distro you can install (also msl distros). msl status work Running, suspended or stopped. msl suspend work / msl resume work Freeze and unfreeze. msl hibernate work Save to disk and stop. msl --shutdown work Shut Linux down properly. With no name, every running instance. msl install debian Download a distro. msl ssh work Connect over SSH, setting it up if needed. msl doctor Check the installation for problems. See Logs and msl doctor. Typos are caught msl lsit doesn't quietly create an instance called \"lsit\" — it says \"no instance named 'lsit' — did you mean msl list?\" To really make an instance with an odd name, use msl new <name>. Run msl help for the complete usage.","s":"Everything the app does, from any terminal — and a few things it doesn't."},{"u":"overview/overview-tab.html","a":"The Overview tab","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"The Overview tab is a stack of cards, top to bottom: Card What's on it More Instance Name, distribution and current state. — Connect over SSH A ready-made ssh command for this instance, and its details. SSH Processor & memory How many virtual CPUs, and how much memory — fixed or dynamic. CPUs, Memory Storage How full the disk is, how big it may get, and what it really costs on your Mac. Storage Shared with macOS Where your Mac home folder appears inside Linux. Home folder Concurrency How many instances are running out of the most MSL allows. Concurrency Most settings apply at the next start A virtual machine's CPUs, memory and disk size are fixed when it boots. Change them while an instance is running and MSL saves the change for its next start — each card says so in its footnote.","s":"Six cards describing and configuring one instance."},{"u":"overview/processors.html","a":"Virtual CPUs","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"The Virtual CPUs row at the top of the Processor & memory card sets how many processors Linux sees. Use the stepper arrows; the number beside them is the current value.","s":"How many processors an instance gets, and why it's fine to be generous."},{"u":"overview/processors.html#how-many","a":"Virtual CPUs","h":"How many to choose","c":"Overview Tab","x":"The stepper goes from 1 up to this Mac's number of logical cores — the caption under it tells you how many that is. If you've never changed it, an instance gets half of your Mac's cores, but at least 2 and never more than 8. That's a good default for almost everything; compiling large projects is the main reason to go higher. CPUs are shared, not reserved As the caption says: vCPUs are time-shared, not reserved. An instance with 8 vCPUs that's sitting idle costs your Mac nothing, and several instances can each have several. Unlike memory, being generous here doesn't take anything away from macOS until Linux is actually busy.","s":""},{"u":"overview/processors.html#apply","a":"Virtual CPUs","h":"Applying it","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Click Apply at the bottom of the card. The change takes effect the next time the instance starts; a green Saved confirms it. Revert puts back what was saved. A hibernated session is discarded If the instance was hibernated, its saved session can't be resumed into a machine of a different size, so it's discarded and the instance starts fresh. Save your work inside Linux first.","s":""},{"u":"overview/memory.html","a":"Memory: manual mode","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"The Memory picker on the Processor & memory card has two modes: Manual and Dynamic. This page is about Manual; Dynamic has its own. Manual is a fixed amount, reserved on your Mac the whole time the instance is running. Type a number of megabytes in the Memory field and click Apply. Megabytes Is 1024 1 GB 2048 2 GB 4096 4 GB 8192 8 GB If you've never changed it, an instance gets a sixth of your Mac's memory, at least 1 GB and at most 8 GB — enough for desktop apps, and small enough that four instances together can't crowd macOS out.","s":"A fixed amount of memory, and why MSL warns above three fifths of your Mac's RAM."},{"u":"overview/memory.html#warnings","a":"Memory: manual mode","h":"The warnings","c":"Overview Tab","x":"MSL checks the number as you type and explains anything worrying in a coloured box under it. Orange: more than three fifths of your Mac's memory A virtual machine's memory is wired down — macOS can't compress it or page it out to make room for anything else. Give an instance more than three fifths of your Mac and macOS is left with the rest for itself and everything you're running. Expect heavy paging, beachballs, and possibly an unstable machine. MSL still lets you — the box says \"It will still start.\" Red: numbers that can't work Too small for the Virtualization framework to start a VM, more than it allows for one VM, or more than your Mac has in total. Apply stays greyed out until the number is possible. Pressing Return in the field also snaps a value that's out of range back to the nearest allowed one.","s":""},{"u":"overview/memory.html#when","a":"Memory: manual mode","h":"When it takes effect","c":"Overview Tab","x":"At the instance's next start. A running instance keeps the memory it booted with; a hibernated session is discarded rather than resumed into a differently sized machine.","s":""},{"u":"overview/memory-dynamic.html","a":"Memory: dynamic mode","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"Dynamic memory gives an instance a Minimum and a Maximum. It starts with the maximum, and MSL keeps watching how much Linux actually needs, handing back what it isn't using and letting it have more again as it needs it — never above the maximum, never below the minimum.","s":"Start at a maximum and hand back whatever Linux isn't using, continuously."},{"u":"overview/memory-dynamic.html#numbers","a":"Memory: dynamic mode","h":"Choosing the numbers","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Maximum is what your Mac sets aside when the instance starts, and the most it can ever have. Minimum is as far down as MSL will ever squeeze it. The same warnings as manual mode apply to the maximum, and the minimum can't be larger than the maximum. It can only give back, never grow past the maximum The Virtualization framework fixes a VM's size when it boots. Dynamic memory works by letting Linux return memory it isn't using — a balloon inside the guest that inflates to hand memory back to your Mac and deflates to give it back to Linux. The balloon moves within the maximum; nothing can take an instance above it. So set the maximum to the most you'd ever want it to have.","s":""},{"u":"overview/memory-dynamic.html#how","a":"Memory: dynamic mode","h":"How MSL decides","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Rather than guessing from one number, MSL looks at how much memory Linux is really using, how much it could free if it had to, and — most tellingly — whether programs inside are stalling while they wait for memory. It gives memory back gently when there's plenty spare, and returns it quickly the moment Linux starts to struggle. Linux images don't use swap, so MSL never squeezes an instance below what its programs are actually holding.","s":""},{"u":"overview/memory-dynamic.html#live","a":"Memory: dynamic mode","h":"The live readout","c":"Overview Tab","x":"While a dynamic instance is running, the bottom of the card updates every few seconds: Row Meaning Allocated right now What Linux currently has from your Mac. Guest is using What its programs are really using, out of what it can see. Guest stalling on memory The share of time programs spend waiting for memory. Only shown when it isn't zero. Last change Why MSL last moved the number, in words. Adjustments this session How many times it has moved since the instance started. If the guest has swap turned on, a note says so: MSL still sizes it conservatively, but swap changes what running out of memory feels like.","s":""},{"u":"overview/memory-dynamic.html#when","a":"Memory: dynamic mode","h":"When it takes effect","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Switching between Manual and Dynamic, or changing the numbers, applies at the next start — like every memory change.","s":""},{"u":"overview/storage.html","a":"Disk size and storage","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"The Storage card on the Overview tab shows how full an instance's disk is and sets how big it may get. Storage belongs to the distro Every instance of a distro shares one disk, so this card is really about that distro's disk. Resize it from work and scratch (the same distro) gets the bigger disk too.","s":"How full the disk is, how big it may get, dynamic versus fixed, and automatic growth."},{"u":"overview/storage.html#reading","a":"Disk size and storage","h":"Reading the card","c":"Overview Tab","x":"The usage bar — \"X used inside name\" and \"N% of total\", with when it was last measured. The bar turns orange once the disk is 85% full. Until the instance has run once, it says \"Start this instance once and MSL will measure how full it is.\" Guest disk size — how big Linux thinks its disk is. Actually used on your Mac — what the image file really takes on your Mac right now. For a dynamic disk this is usually far smaller than the disk size; a fixed disk adds \"(reserved)\".","s":""},{"u":"overview/storage.html#modes","a":"Disk size and storage","h":"Dynamic or fixed","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Dynamic (the default) Fixed On your Mac Takes only the space Linux has actually written. Reserves the whole size up front. The slider sets the Limit the Size Good for Almost everyone. Making sure the space is there, however full your Mac gets. A new disk is dynamic, with a 64 GB limit. Switching to fixed takes a while Reserving space means writing out the whole disk — \"Reserving space on your Mac… this writes the whole disk, so it can take a while.\" For a large disk that can be minutes. The card stays usable; the rest of the app does too.","s":""},{"u":"overview/storage.html#bigger","a":"Disk size and storage","h":"Making it bigger","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Drag the slider (4 GB to 512 GB, in steps of 4) and let go. If the instance is stopped, the image grows right away; if it's running, the change waits for its next start. Either way, Linux's filesystem is stretched to fill the new size during the next start, and until then the card says \"The guest filesystem is extended the next time this instance starts.\" Disks only grow A disk can be made bigger but never smaller. Drag below its current size and the card says so and puts the slider back.","s":""},{"u":"overview/storage.html#auto-grow","a":"Disk size and storage","h":"Growing automatically","c":"Overview Tab","x":"With a dynamic disk, Grow automatically when it runs low (on by default) looks after a disk that's smaller than its limit: whenever the instance starts with its disk at least 85% full, MSL grows it by 8 GB, up to the limit. When it's already at the limit, nothing grows and the log notes it.","s":""},{"u":"overview/storage.html#free-space","a":"Disk size and storage","h":"Your Mac's free space","c":"Overview Tab","x":"The footnote shows how much space is free on your Mac. A dynamic disk can't grow past what your Mac has room for, so keep an eye on both. See The disk is full.","s":""},{"u":"overview/home-share.html","a":"Your home folder inside Linux","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"Every running instance can see your Mac home folder at /mnt/mac. The Shared with macOS card on the Overview tab shows both ends: Home folder (inside Linux) /mnt/mac Host path (on your Mac) your home folder, e.g. /Users/you So ~/Documents/report.txt on your Mac is /mnt/mac/Documents/report.txt in Linux. Changes on either side show up on the other straight away — it's the same file, not a copy. shell cd /mnt/mac/Desktop ls","s":"Your Mac home folder appears at /mnt/mac in every running instance."},{"u":"overview/home-share.html#scope","a":"Your home folder inside Linux","h":"Only your home folder","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Folders outside your home — /Applications, other drives, /usr/local — aren't visible inside Linux. That's why MSL Files greys out Open Folder in MSL for them.","s":""},{"u":"overview/home-share.html#off","a":"Your home folder inside Linux","h":"Turning it off","c":"Overview Tab","x":"The Mac home share gate on the Sandbox tab revokes it for one instance: /mnt/mac stops resolving. Anything in the middle of reading a file there sees I/O errors rather than a clean unmount, so close things first. The other direction To reach Linux's files from your Mac, use MSL Files (⌥⌘F).","s":""},{"u":"overview/concurrency.html","a":"How many can run at once","h":null,"c":"Overview Tab","x":"MSL runs at most four instances at once. The Concurrency card on the Overview tab shows \"Running now: n of 4\", and the capacity pill at the top of the sidebar shows the same, turning orange when all four are in use.","s":"MSL runs at most four instances together, and why suspended ones count."},{"u":"overview/concurrency.html#why","a":"How many can run at once","h":"Why there's a limit","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Every running or suspended instance keeps its memory on your Mac, and virtual machine memory can't be paged out. Without a limit, it would be easy to start enough instances to leave macOS gasping. The default memory size is chosen so that four instances together stay well within your Mac.","s":""},{"u":"overview/concurrency.html#what-counts","a":"How many can run at once","h":"What counts","c":"Overview Tab","x":"State Uses a slot? Running Yes Suspended Yes — it's frozen, but its memory is still held. Hibernated or stopped No","s":""},{"u":"overview/concurrency.html#full","a":"How many can run at once","h":"When it's full","c":"Overview Tab","x":"Start is greyed out on stopped instances, and trying anyway says \"4 instances are already running … Suspend or shut one down first.\" Things that would start an instance — Find Apps, a sharper icon for a Dock tile — say the same, or quietly fall back (an app added while the slots are full keeps its existing icon). To free a slot, hibernate or shut down something. Suspending doesn't free one. See Suspend, hibernate or shut down?.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-rename.html","a":"Renaming and copying","h":null,"c":"Managing Instances","x":"msl rename <instance> <new name> msl clone <instance> <new name> Rename moves everything: the disk, accounts, files, resource settings, sandbox gates, per-app graphics and launch commands, port forwards, the lot. Clone makes a second instance from the first — its disk is an instant copy, so it costs almost nothing until the two start to differ.","s":"Give an instance another name, or make a second one from it."},{"u":"instances/instances-rename.html#stopped","a":"Renaming and copying","h":"Both need the instance stopped","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Copying a filesystem while something is writing to it captures it mid-write, which is the same corruption as two instances sharing one disk. So both refuse a running instance and say so.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-rename.html#hibernate","a":"Renaming and copying","h":"Renaming cannot carry saved memory","c":"Managing Instances","x":"A rename is refused while an instance has hibernated memory or a snapshot, and the reason is worth knowing: a guest's network address is derived from its name, and macOS will not restore a saved virtual machine whose address has changed. Renaming anyway would produce an unhelpful error on the next start, so MSL asks you to start it once — which uses the saved state up — and delete any snapshots first. A clone is unaffected: the original keeps its name, and the copy starts cold.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-rename.html#clone-keeps","a":"Renaming and copying","h":"What a clone keeps, and what it does not","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Kept Not kept Files, accounts, installed packages The original's name, obviously Resource settings, sandbox gates, per-app settings Its SSH record — msl ssh rebuilds it Its place in the family it came from Saved memory and snapshots The copy keeps the original's SSH host key until the guest makes a new one, so msl ssh may warn about a changed key the first time.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-rename.html#apps","a":"Renaming and copying","h":"Launchpad and the Dock","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Renaming removes the instance's generated app shortcuts and Dock pins rather than rewriting them. A generated app launches an instance by name, so one left pointing at a name that no longer exists would quietly bring that instance back as an empty new one. Open the Applications tab after a rename and they are scanned again.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-rename.html#policy","a":"Renaming and copying","h":"Under a policy","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Rules are written about instance names, so renaming or cloning out of a rule would be a way around it. MSL refuses that, and names the capability: ERROR refused by MSL Admin Portal: instance.delete is denied for 'Ubuntu' but not for 'Ubuntu2', so this would be a way around it Moving into a more restricted name is fine — that takes things away, not back.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-identity.html","a":"Where an instance came from","h":null,"c":"Managing Instances","x":"msl identity [instance] Every instance is given a signed identity when it is created. It records the family it belongs to, how many times it has been copied, and the name it was born under. Ubuntu: an original lineage vtp6ismgxwsxmj4kcmvx7hojug copies 0 (0) born as Ubuntu on machine b8072e055c9775b6 A copy says copy 0.1 of its lineage and keeps its parent's lineage, so a family stays recognisable however far it spreads. The dots are the copies: 0.1.2 is the second copy of the first copy of an original.","s":"Every instance carries a signed record of its origin, and MSL can tell a copy from an original."},{"u":"instances/instances-identity.html#why","a":"Where an instance came from","h":"What it is for","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Denying instance.clone stops the Clone button. It does not stop somebody copying a disk file by hand, or exporting a shipment where copying is allowed and importing it where it is not. An identity makes those answerable: a copy that arrives any other way still carries the family it came from, and a Mac can refuse it.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-identity.html#notices","a":"Where an instance came from","h":"What MSL notices","c":"Managing Instances","x":"What you see A copied disk with a new registry entry no identity recorded — it makes no claim to be an original A copied identity file too ALSO CLAIMED BY … — two instances cannot be the same instance An edited identity the signature does not match An imported shipment arrives as a copy, one generation deeper","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-identity.html#marker","a":"Where an instance came from","h":"The marker inside the guest","c":"Managing Instances","x":"MSL also writes the identity to /etc/msl-instance inside the instance. The host record does not travel with a disk; that does. If somebody swaps the disk under a name whose identity is perfectly valid, the two disagree and MSL says so. A missing marker is reported and never rewritten. Writing it back would stamp this instance's identity onto whatever disk is actually there, which is the exact thing the marker exists to catch. If you know the disk is right — a cleanup removed the file, say — put it back deliberately: msl identity <instance> --rewrite-marker Instances made before this existed have no identity, and nothing refuses them. MSL gives identities to whatever is on the Mac once, when the feature arrives, and records that it did — so an instance turning up without one afterwards is a different and more interesting fact.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-protect.html","a":"Protecting an image","h":null,"c":"Managing Instances","x":"msl protect <instance> msl unprotect <instance> Moves the instance's disk into an encrypted container. A copy of the file — on a USB stick, in a backup, in a support upload — is then useless on any other Mac.","s":"Put an instance's disk in an encrypted container, so a copy of the file is useless."},{"u":"instances/instances-protect.html#cost","a":"Protecting an image","h":"What it costs","c":"Managing Instances","x":"A real copy of the whole disk, because MSL's instant-copy trick cannot cross into a container. Minutes for a large instance, and that much free space while it runs. Opening it at start is well under a second. The original is removed only after the copy inside has been read back and checked, so there is no moment where both are gone.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-protect.html#limits","a":"Protecting an image","h":"What it does and does not buy","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Closed Still open Dragging the disk to a USB stick, AirDrop, email Anything inside the instance, to anyone who can open a shell in it Time Machine and every backup service copying it in the clear An administrator of this Mac, who can reach the key A guest copying it out of /mnt/mac That last one is worth its own sentence. The Mac's home folder is shared into every instance, writable, and instance disks live inside it — so any Linux terminal can copy any instance's disk unless you deny sharing.macHome. msl doctor says so if you have asked for an image to be protected and left that open. Protection is a consequence, not a switch Deny instance.clone or shipment.export for an instance and MSL treats it as one you mean to keep uncopyable, and reports anything still open.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-protect.html#adminheld","a":"Protecting an image","h":"A key only you hold","c":"Managing Instances","x":"msl vault <instance> status | adopt | unlock | lock By default the key is derived from a secret on this Mac, which closes every accidental route but not a determined administrator of it. Adopt moves the container to a passphrase you type instead. After that nothing on the Mac can open it until somebody supplies it, including MSL after a reboot. There is no recovery. If the passphrase is lost, the disk is unreadable permanently — MSL keeps only a wrapped copy of the key, and that is the entire point of this mode. Saved memory from hibernating goes inside the container too. A .state file is the guest's whole RAM, and encrypting the disk while leaving that beside it in the clear would protect very little.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-forward.html","a":"Reaching a port inside Linux","h":null,"c":"Managing Instances","x":"msl forward <instance> 8080:3000 msl forward <instance> list msl unforward 8080 localhost:8080 on your Mac then reaches port 3000 inside the instance — so a development server in Linux opens in a Mac browser. One number means the same port both sides. The port is bound to your Mac only. Nothing else on the network can see it.","s":"Open a port on your Mac that reaches a server running in an instance."},{"u":"instances/instances-forward.html#routes","a":"Reaching a port inside Linux","h":"Two routes, and why you may need the second","c":"Managing Instances","x":"Route Reaches the default A server bound to 0.0.0.0 inside the guest --ssh Everything, including one bound to 127.0.0.1 Most development servers bind 127.0.0.1 by default — Flask, Vite, Rails and manage.py runserver all do — and nothing outside the guest can reach a loopback socket. So if the default route connects to nothing, add --ssh: it goes through the instance's own SSH server, which makes the connection from inside. MSL checks when you set one up and tells you which to use rather than leaving you with a connection that resets.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-forward.html#persist","a":"Reaching a port inside Linux","h":"They come back","c":"Managing Instances","x":"A forward is remembered and restored when the instance next starts. list shows which are live and which are waiting: localhost:8080 -> work:3000 localhost:9000 -> work:5432 through SSH (not running - starts with the instance)","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-forward.html#limits","a":"Reaching a port inside Linux","h":"What it will not do","c":"Managing Instances","x":"A forward does not keep an instance awake. An idle instance still suspends, and the listener is taken down with it so a request is refused at once rather than hanging. It comes back when the instance does. It is refused while the instance is sandboxed. A forwarded port is a hole in exactly the box those gates draw — see The four gates. Ports below 1024 are reserved by macOS and MSL cannot bind them.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-custom-command.html","a":"Changing how an app starts","h":null,"c":"Managing Instances","x":"Right-click an app in the Applications tab and choose Custom Launch Command to replace what MSL runs when you open it. Some apps simply will not start from their own desktop entry without help: a game that needs a particular build of its engine, a binary that wants an environment variable set, an app whose packaged entry points at the wrong path. Those are one-line fixes if you can edit the command, and otherwise they are \"this app does not work on MSL\".","s":"Replace the command MSL runs for one Linux app."},{"u":"instances/instances-custom-command.html#examples","a":"Changing how an app starts","h":"Examples","c":"Managing Instances","x":"love11 /opt/mari0 GDK_BACKEND=x11 inkscape /opt/thing/bin/thing --no-sandbox It runs in the same environment MSL would have used, so the display, the session bus and the graphics mode are all already set up.","s":""},{"u":"instances/instances-custom-command.html#everywhere","a":"Changing how an app starts","h":"It applies everywhere","c":"Managing Instances","x":"The override is keyed to the app's original command, so it follows the app whether you open it from the Applications tab, a generated app in Launchpad, the Dock, or msl gui-native. Changing it in one place changes it everywhere, and clearing it puts the app back to its own command. This runs an arbitrary command inside the instance, so an administrator can deny it with apps.customCommand. Clearing one is always allowed — otherwise a policy arriving after somebody set an override would trap it in place.","s":""},{"u":"tools/tools-tab.html","a":"The Tools tab","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Card What's on it More Background service Whether MSL's service is running, and whether it starts at login. The background service Generated applications Where the Mac apps made for this instance live. Generated apps Snapshots Saved machine states, to go back to. Snapshots Maintenance Disk check and repair, maintenance boot, and fix-it utilities. Maintenance Instance Suspend, Hibernate, Shut Down, Remove…. Stopping, Removing","s":"Five cards for looking after an instance."},{"u":"tools/shipments.html","a":"Shipments","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"A shipment is one instance packed into a single file. Everything it has goes with it: the applications you installed, your files, your Linux account, how much disk and memory it was given, and which experimental features it was running. Someone else's MSL can install that file and get a working machine - even if their Mac has never downloaded that distribution. It is, honestly, MSL's weirder and worse version of what Docker does. There are no layers, no registry, no build file, and nothing clever about it: it is one machine, copied whole. Docker is a better tool for almost everything a shipment could be used for. What a shipment is good at is the thing Docker makes awkward - handing a colleague the exact environment you are sitting in, graphical applications and all, and having them open it on their Mac without learning anything first.","s":"A whole instance in one file, for carrying to another Mac."},{"u":"tools/shipments.html#making-one","a":"Shipments","h":"Making one","c":"Tools Tab","x":"From the app, in the Tools tab, or from the menu beside the Terminal button: Create Shipment…. From a terminal: msl work --shipment msl work --shipment -o ~/Desktop/work.mslship The instance has to be shut down first. A shipment taken from a running machine catches its filesystem halfway through a write, which makes a file that looks fine and is not. Expect it to take a few minutes and produce a file a few hundred megabytes to a gigabyte or so, depending on how much is in the instance. Most of that time is compression.","s":""},{"u":"tools/shipments.html#installing-one","a":"Shipments","h":"Installing one","c":"Tools Tab","x":"New Instance → Install MSL Shipment…, or: msl shipment install work.mslship msl shipment install work.mslship --name borrowed If the name inside is already taken, MSL picks a free one rather than writing over the instance you have. To see what is in a file before committing to it: msl shipment info work.mslship That reads only the small manifest at the front of the file, so it answers immediately even for a very large shipment.","s":""},{"u":"tools/shipments.html#what-travels-and-what-it-does-on-arrival","a":"Shipments","h":"What travels, and what it does on arrival","c":"Tools Tab","x":"The filesystem Everything - applications, files, configuration Your Linux account The one you log in as, so the instance opens as you CPUs, memory, disk size Applied to the new instance Sandbox seals Applied, but see below Experimental features Applied to that instance only Experiments do not change your MSL. The settings in a shipment are recorded as overrides for the instance it created and nothing else, so installing a shipment from someone who runs MSL differently from you leaves your own switches exactly as they were. Only the differences are stored, so an instance is not frozen against settings its author never touched. Some things cannot be reproduced on every Mac. If a shipment wanted GPU acceleration and this Mac has no renderer installed, that one is skipped, and MSL says which and why rather than pretending. Seals only travel in the safe direction. A sealed shipment installs sealed. An open one does not unseal anything - it leaves your defaults alone.","s":""},{"u":"tools/shipments.html#before-you-install-someone-else-s","a":"Shipments","h":"Before you install someone else's","c":"Tools Tab","x":"A shipment is a complete Linux system somebody else assembled. It can contain anything they put in it, and installing one is closer to running their computer than to opening their document. Install shipments from people you trust. The checksum inside a shipment is there to catch a damaged or truncated file. It is not a signature, and it says nothing about who made it.","s":""},{"u":"tools/shipments.html#where-the-file-goes","a":"Shipments","h":"Where the file goes","c":"Tools Tab","x":"A .mslship is a plain zip. You can look inside with Archive Utility or unzip -l and find two things: msl.json, which is the manifest above, and the compressed disk. Nothing is hidden or encrypted.","s":""},{"u":"tools/background-service.html","a":"The background service","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"mslhd is MSL's background service. It runs every instance — the app, the msl command and the Linux apps in your Dock are all just ways of asking it to do things. Without it running, nothing can start.","s":"mslhd runs every instance. What it is, how it starts, and what to do when it isn't running."},{"u":"tools/background-service.html#card","a":"The background service","h":"The card","c":"Tools Tab","x":"The Background service card at the top of the Tools tab: Start MSL's service automatically — whether it starts when you log in (as a login item, at ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.msl.mslhd.plist). On by default. Status — Running or Not running. Log — ~/Library/Logs/MSL/mslhd.log, where it writes what it's doing. Turning it off sticks MSL normally re-installs the login item every time the app opens, so it can repair itself. Switching it off here is remembered, so the app won't quietly turn it back on. Linux apps in your Dock need it With the service off, Linux apps you've added to Applications or pinned to the Dock can't start either, because it's the service that runs them.","s":""},{"u":"tools/background-service.html#not-running","a":"The background service","h":"When it isn't running","c":"Tools Tab","x":"With no instance selected, the main window says \"MSL's background service isn't running\" and offers Start it. If it keeps stopping, the log says why — see The service isn't running.","s":""},{"u":"tools/background-service.html#updates","a":"The background service","h":"After updating MSL","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Updating the app installs the new service, but the one already running carries on until it next starts — at your next login. Until then, newer features that need it (like the Maintenance card) may say the service is \"an older version\". Logging out and back in sorts it.","s":""},{"u":"tools/generated-apps.html","a":"Generated apps","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Every Linux app you add to Applications or pin to the Dock becomes a small Mac app in ~/Applications/MSL/<instance>/. The Generated applications card shows where: Location — that instance's folder. Show in Finder — opens it. Open Logs — opens ~/Library/Logs/MSL. A Mac app started from the Dock has no terminal to report problems in, so it writes them here instead. They're ordinary Mac apps: deleting one in Finder is exactly the same as Remove from Applications.","s":"Where the Mac apps made for an instance's Linux programs live."},{"u":"tools/snapshots.html","a":"Snapshots","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"A snapshot saves a machine's state — everything in its memory, every running program — under a name, so you can return to that exact moment.","s":"Save a machine's state under a name and go back to it — and the one rule that keeps it safe."},{"u":"tools/snapshots.html#saving","a":"Snapshots","h":"Saving one","c":"Tools Tab","x":"The instance has to be running or suspended — a stopped one has no memory to save. (The card says \"Start name to take a snapshot.\") Type a name in New snapshot name — letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, like before-upgrade. Click Save (or press Return). Saving stops the instance Saving pauses the machine, writes its memory to a file, and then stops it. That's deliberate — a saved state is only reliable if the machine it came from doesn't carry on running. Start it again afterwards if you want to keep working.","s":""},{"u":"tools/snapshots.html#restoring","a":"Snapshots","h":"Restoring one","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Click Restore beside it. Whatever the instance is doing is replaced — it stops if it was running — and it comes back exactly as it was when you saved. As the card says: restoring discards everything since.","s":""},{"u":"tools/snapshots.html#disk","a":"Snapshots","h":"The rule that keeps it safe","c":"Tools Tab","x":"A snapshot doesn't copy the disk A snapshot holds the machine's memory and running state. It does not keep a separate copy of the disk — the disk is the distro's shared disk, and it carries on changing when anything runs on it. If the disk changes after you save — you start the instance again, or use another instance of the same distro — then restoring puts back a memory that remembers a different disk. Linux can get confused about its own files, which can damage the filesystem. Restore is only safe if nothing has run on that distro since the snapshot was saved. If you've restored anyway and things seem odd, check the disk. For \"undo\" after changing files, a copy of those files is often the better tool. For the disk as a whole, Repair Disk keeps a real copy of the disk before it changes anything.","s":""},{"u":"tools/snapshots.html#where","a":"Snapshots","h":"Where they live","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Each snapshot is a file in ~/Library/Application Support/MSL, named vm-<instance>-<snapshot>.state, about the size of the instance's memory. There's no delete button yet; removing the instance deletes all of its snapshots. See Reclaiming disk space.","s":""},{"u":"tools/stopping-instances.html","a":"Suspend, hibernate or shut down?","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"The Instance card at the bottom of the Tools tab has Suspend, Hibernate and Shut Down. The header and the sidebar's right-click menu have some of them too. Suspend Hibernate Shut Down What happens Frozen, kept in memory. Memory written to a file, then stopped. Stopped at once. Coming back Almost instantly, exactly where it was. A few seconds, exactly where it was. A fresh start of Linux. Holds memory on your Mac Yes No No Uses one of the four slots Yes No No Disk space on your Mac None A file about the size of its memory None Available when Running Running or suspended Running or suspended","s":"Three ways to stop an instance, what each costs, and which to use."},{"u":"tools/stopping-instances.html#suspend","a":"Suspend, hibernate or shut down?","h":"Suspend","c":"Tools Tab","x":"For a short break. Nothing is saved to disk, so it's instant both ways — but the instance keeps its memory and its slot. MSL also suspends instances by itself when they're idle.","s":""},{"u":"tools/stopping-instances.html#hibernate","a":"Suspend, hibernate or shut down?","h":"Hibernate","c":"Tools Tab","x":"For a longer break. The machine's whole memory is written to your Mac's disk and the instance stops, giving back its memory and its slot. Start brings it back exactly as it was — shells, programs and all. Saving takes longer the more memory the instance has. This is also what MSL does to every running instance when your Mac shuts down, restarts or logs out. See Sleep, shutdown and battery.","s":""},{"u":"tools/stopping-instances.html#shut-down","a":"Suspend, hibernate or shut down?","h":"Shut Down","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Shut Down stops it straight away Shut Down in the app stops the virtual machine immediately — like holding down a computer's power button. Linux doesn't get the chance to finish writing to its disk first. Its filesystem recovers on the next start, but anything that hadn't been written yet is lost, and stopping a machine this way over and over is how disks get damaged. To shut Linux down properly, do one of these instead: • Run sudo poweroff in the instance's Terminal tab, and let it stop by itself. • Or, in Terminal.app, run msl --shutdown work — it asks Linux to power off first, then stops the machine. • Or Hibernate, which loses nothing at all. Shutting down also throws away a hibernated session, if there was one: after a power-off, the saved memory no longer matches the disk.","s":""},{"u":"tools/stopping-instances.html#which","a":"Suspend, hibernate or shut down?","h":"Which one?","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Back in a minute → Suspend, or just leave it — idle instances suspend themselves. Done for the day, want the memory back → Hibernate. Want a clean start of Linux → sudo poweroff inside it, then Start.","s":""},{"u":"tools/removing-instances.html","a":"Removing an instance","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Remove… is in the instance's right-click menu, the header's menu, and the Instance card on the Tools tab. It happens straight away Remove doesn't ask for confirmation, despite the \"…\" on its name. Click it only when you mean it.","s":"What Remove deletes, what it keeps, and why it doesn't ask first."},{"u":"tools/removing-instances.html#deleted","a":"Removing an instance","h":"What's deleted","c":"Tools Tab","x":"The instance itself — its name disappears from the sidebar. Its hibernated session, if it had one. All of its snapshots. Every Mac app made for it in ~/Applications/MSL/<instance>, and its cached list of apps. Its terminal session in the app. If it was running, it's stopped first.","s":""},{"u":"tools/removing-instances.html#kept","a":"Removing an instance","h":"What's kept","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Your files are on the distro's disk, not the instance Removing an instance never touches the distro's disk image — the files inside Linux stay, and every other instance of that distro carries on as before. Create a new instance of the same distro and your files are all still there. To really delete a distro's files and free the space, see Reclaiming disk space.","s":""},{"u":"tools/uninstalling-msl.html","a":"Uninstalling MSL","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Window ▸ Remove MSL…, or msl uninstall in a terminal. Both build the same itemised list and remove the same things, so the two can never disagree about what MSL leaves behind. The window shows the choice first — Keep my Linux or Remove everything — then every item with its size, and removes nothing until you have read it. Deleting your Linux asks again, with the instance count and the total. Your Linux is kept by default Uninstalling removes MSL, not the Linux you installed with it. Every instance, disk image, custom image, account and setting stays in ~/Library/Application Support/MSL. Install MSL again and it all comes back exactly as it was.","s":"Remove MSL and keep every instance and image - or remove all of it."},{"u":"tools/uninstalling-msl.html#removed","a":"Uninstalling MSL","h":"What goes","c":"Tools Tab","x":"MSL.app itself. The background service and the login items, stopped first. The Mac apps made for your Linux apps, in ~/Applications/MSL. MSL's caches, logs and saved window state. The msl, mslhd, mslgui and msl-applauncher programs. The Admin Portal's password and signing keys, and any encrypted containers, in /Users/Shared/MSL. Those are MSL's own records about who administers this Mac, not your Linux, so they go in both modes. An enrolled policy in /Library/Application Support/MSL. Things that are not files: Dock tiles for your Linux apps, the app's registration with macOS so it stops appearing in Spotlight and Launchpad, and the installer's receipt so a reinstall does its own work. A few of those belong to the system, so macOS asks for your password once at the end. MSL finishes the job itself rather than printing a sudo rm -rf for you to paste — an uninstaller that stops and hands you a command has not finished. If an instance is in an encrypted container and you are keeping your Linux, its disk is taken back out first. The key lives in /Users/Shared/MSL, which is about to go, and leaving a disk inside would make it permanently unreadable — see Protecting an image. Running instances are shut down properly first - not hibernated, because a saved session with no MSL to restore it is worse than a clean power-off. Anything mounted in Finder is unmounted before that.","s":""},{"u":"tools/uninstalling-msl.html#kept","a":"Uninstalling MSL","h":"What stays","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Every distro disk image, and everything inside it. Your instances, the accounts you made, and each instance's storage settings. Your custom images, in Custom Images. MSL's own settings, so a reinstall doesn't switch something back on that you switched off.","s":""},{"u":"tools/uninstalling-msl.html#policy","a":"Uninstalling MSL","h":"Under a policy","c":"Tools Tab","x":"An administrator can deny system.uninstall. The menu item is still there and the refusal says who decided and why, because hiding it would leave you hunting for something that is not there — see Reading a refusal.","s":""},{"u":"tools/uninstalling-msl.html#upgrading","a":"Uninstalling MSL","h":"Upgrading is not uninstalling","c":"Tools Tab","x":"To move to a newer MSL, just install it over the old one. The app and your Linux live in different places, so nothing is lost and there is no uninstall step. See The background service for why MSL restarts it after an update.","s":""},{"u":"tools/uninstalling-msl.html#everything","a":"Uninstalling MSL","h":"Removing all of it","c":"Tools Tab","x":"msl uninstall --everything, or the checkbox in the sheet, deletes the Linux side too - every image, instance and setting. It asks you to type a word first, the same one Removing an instance uses, because nothing here comes back. msl uninstall --dry-run prints both lists and changes nothing.","s":""},{"u":"tools/maintenance.html","a":"The Maintenance card","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"The Maintenance card on the Tools tab collects tools for keeping an instance healthy. It's laid out by when each one can run, because that decides whether a button can work at all: Disk Check and repair the Linux filesystem from your Mac. The instance must be shut down. Maintenance Boot Start Linux in a minimal mode and check its disk with Linux's own tools. While It's Running Four fix-it utilities that run inside a running instance. Nothing changes without asking As the card's footnote says: nothing here changes a disk without asking first, and every repair keeps a copy of the disk from before it.","s":"Disk check and repair, a maintenance boot, and fix-it utilities — organised by when they can run."},{"u":"tools/maintenance.html#reasons","a":"The Maintenance card","h":"Greyed-out buttons explain themselves","c":"Tools Tab","x":"A disabled button always has a line saying why, rather than just going grey: You see Why \"Shut the instance down first — a disk that's in use can't be checked safely.\" Disk tools need it stopped. \"This instance has a hibernated session…\" Its saved memory and disk are a pair. Start it, then shut it down, before maintenance. \"Maintenance is already running on this disk.\" One job at a time per disk. \"e2fsck isn't installed…\" with Copy Install Command The Mac-side check needs a free tool. See Checking a disk. \"Start the instance to use these.\" The utilities run inside Linux.","s":""},{"u":"tools/maintenance.html#results","a":"The Maintenance card","h":"Results","c":"Tools Tab","x":"When a tool finishes, a coloured box appears at the bottom of the card: Colour Means Green Healthy, or done. Orange Something needs your attention. Red It couldn't do what it was asked. Blue For your information. Show details opens the tool's full output; the × dismisses the box. Instances that share the disk wait While a disk tool is working, no instance using that distro's disk can start — they all share it.","s":""},{"u":"tools/disk-check.html","a":"Checking a disk","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Check Disk in the Maintenance card's Disk section examines the instance's Linux filesystem from your Mac and reports what it finds. It never changes anything.","s":"Check Disk reads the Linux filesystem from your Mac, without changing anything."},{"u":"tools/disk-check.html#before","a":"Checking a disk","h":"Before you start","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Shut the instance down. Checking a disk while Linux is using it gives meaningless answers — it's only safe when nothing has it open. No hibernated session. If the instance is hibernated, start it and shut it down first. Install e2fsprogs on your Mac (see below).","s":""},{"u":"tools/disk-check.html#e2fsprogs","a":"Checking a disk","h":"Installing e2fsprogs","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Linux disks are checked with a tool called e2fsck. macOS doesn't come with it, but it's free through Homebrew. Until it's installed, the card says so and offers Copy Install Command, which copies this: shell brew install e2fsprogs Paste it into Terminal. MSL finds it where Homebrew puts it — no other setup needed. No Homebrew? The maintenance boot checks the disk with Linux's own copy of the tool, so it doesn't need anything on your Mac.","s":""},{"u":"tools/disk-check.html#results","a":"Checking a disk","h":"What the results mean","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Result Colour Means No problems found green The filesystem is consistent. Problems found orange \"Nothing was changed. Run Repair to fix them — a backup of the disk is taken first.\" The check didn't run red Something stopped the check itself, and the result \"says nothing about the disk\". The reason follows. Show details has e2fsck's own output. A big disk can take a few minutes — \"Checking the disk… a large disk can take a few minutes.\"","s":""},{"u":"tools/disk-repair.html","a":"Repairing a disk","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Repair Disk… fixes whatever the filesystem check finds. It asks first — \"Repair this instance's disk?\" — and explains what happens next.","s":"Repair Disk fixes the filesystem — after taking an instant, free backup you can go back to."},{"u":"tools/disk-repair.html#what","a":"Repairing a disk","h":"What it does","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Takes a backup. An instant copy of the whole disk, made with an APFS clone: it appears immediately and costs no space on your Mac until the disk starts to differ from it. Repairs. e2fsck fixes everything it can. Reports what happened. The same prerequisites as Checking a disk apply: shut down, no hibernated session, e2fsprogs installed. No backup, no repair If MSL can't make the clone — the disk isn't on an APFS volume, or the backup would have to be copied to another volume — it refuses to repair rather than risk the disk without a way back.","s":""},{"u":"tools/disk-repair.html#results","a":"Repairing a disk","h":"Results","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Result Means Problems found and fixed Done. The backup is kept until you discard it. Problems fixed Fixed, and e2fsck wants a restart before the disk is used — starting the instance now is that restart. Some problems couldn't be fixed It fixed what it safely could. What's left needs a closer look; the backup is kept. The check didn't run Nothing was repaired. The reason follows.","s":""},{"u":"tools/disk-repair.html#backup","a":"Repairing a disk","h":"The backup","c":"Tools Tab","x":"After a repair, the Disk section shows \"A copy of the disk from before the last repair is kept.\" with two buttons: Restore… — puts the old disk back. It asks first: \"Put the old disk back?\" — anything written to the disk since that repair is lost. Discard — deletes the backup. Do this once you're happy the repaired disk is fine. The backup sits beside the disk image, with .pre-repair on the end of its name. If one already exists when you repair again, MSL keeps the existing one instead of replacing it — so it's always from before the first repair since you last discarded. Discard when you're sure The clone is free at first but grows as the repaired disk changes, up to the size of the whole disk. Once the instance has been working happily for a while, discard it.","s":""},{"u":"tools/check-at-start.html","a":"Checking the disk at every start","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Check the disk every time this instance starts — a switch in the Maintenance card's Disk section. Off by default. When it's on, every start first runs a full check of the disk from your Mac. If the disk is clean, the instance starts as usual. If it isn't, MSL refuses to start it rather than risk making things worse, with a message that begins \"didn't start: the filesystem check at start found a problem\". Then repair it. It needs e2fsprogs on your Mac. If you turn it on and later remove e2fsprogs, starts are refused until you reinstall it or turn the switch off — the message says which. It adds the length of a full check to every start. On a big disk that's noticeable. Resuming a hibernated session skips the check: the saved memory and the disk have to match exactly, and a check can't change that. It's set per instance.","s":"An optional full check before each start, which refuses to boot a damaged disk."},{"u":"tools/maintenance-boot.html","a":"Maintenance boot","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Check from Linux and Repair from Linux… start the instance in a minimal maintenance mode: the disk read-only, nothing else running. Linux checks (or repairs) its own disk with its own tools, reports back, and shuts itself down. It's useful when your Mac doesn't have e2fsprogs, and it's a second opinion from Linux itself.","s":"Start Linux in a minimal mode and check or repair its disk with Linux's own tools."},{"u":"tools/maintenance-boot.html#repair","a":"Maintenance boot","h":"Repairing","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Repair from Linux… asks first, then takes the same instant backup as Repair Disk before starting Linux. The backup appears in the Disk section afterwards, with Restore… and Discard.","s":""},{"u":"tools/maintenance-boot.html#results","a":"Maintenance boot","h":"What you might see","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Result Means The usual check or repair results It ran; the same meanings as Checking a disk. Maintenance boot couldn't start (red) Linux stopped before the tools could run — often because the disk is too damaged to start from. If e2fsprogs is on your Mac, Check Disk looks at it without starting it. No result from the maintenance boot (red) It didn't report back within the time limit and was stopped. If this was a repair, check the disk before relying on it. The same prerequisites apply as for the Mac-side tools: shut down, no hibernated session.","s":""},{"u":"tools/guest-utilities.html","a":"Utilities for a running instance","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"The While It's Running section of the Maintenance card has four utilities. Each has a Run button, enabled while the instance is running.","s":"Sync the clock, repair the package manager, clear caches, and look for disk errors."},{"u":"tools/guest-utilities.html#clock","a":"Utilities for a running instance","h":"Sync clock with the Mac","c":"Tools Tab","x":"An instance resumed from hibernation keeps the time it was saved at, which can confuse web certificates, builds and git. This sets Linux's clock to your Mac's and tells you how far off it was — or \"The clock was already in sync.\" Usually automatic MSL already corrects the clock of running instances after your Mac wakes. This is for when something slips through.","s":""},{"u":"tools/guest-utilities.html#packages","a":"Utilities for a running instance","h":"Repair package manager","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Finishes interrupted installs and clears a stale lock left behind by a crash, using the distro's own package manager — the fix for \"could not get lock\" and \"interrupted\" errors. It can take several minutes.","s":""},{"u":"tools/guest-utilities.html#caches","a":"Utilities for a running instance","h":"Clear caches inside Linux","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Clears the package manager's downloaded files and trims the system journal. Frees space inside Linux, not on your Mac This makes room inside the instance. The disk image on your Mac doesn't shrink — see Reclaiming disk space.","s":""},{"u":"tools/guest-utilities.html#disk-errors","a":"Utilities for a running instance","h":"Check for disk errors","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Reads Linux's kernel log for filesystem and input/output errors. It only reads — if it finds any, shut the instance down and check the disk.","s":""},{"u":"tools/usb-devices.html","a":"USB devices","h":null,"c":"Tools Tab","x":"Some devices macOS simply has no driver for: a development board, a programmer, a vendor-specific dongle, a piece of lab equipment whose manufacturer only ever shipped a Linux driver. Plug one into a Mac and nothing happens. Linux, very often, knows exactly what it is. MSL can hand the device over. It carries it into the instance over USB/IP, which has been part of Linux since 2008 and is built into every image MSL ships - so there is nothing to install on the Linux side and no driver to find.","s":"Hand a USB device on your Mac to a Linux instance - including hardware macOS has no driver for."},{"u":"tools/usb-devices.html#seeing-what-is-there","a":"USB devices","h":"Seeing what is there","c":"Tools Tab","x":"msl usb list Column What it tells you LOCATION The device's location ID, which is what you pass to attach. It is the port it is plugged into, so it changes if you move it. ID Vendor and product, as vendor:product. You can use this instead of the location ID. macOS DRIVER Which of macOS's own drivers is holding it, or none - native. ATTACHED TO Which instance has it, if any. That third column is the one that matters, because it decides which of two quite different things happens next.","s":""},{"u":"tools/usb-devices.html#a-device-macos-does-not-know","a":"USB devices","h":"A device macOS does not know","c":"Tools Tab","x":"msl usb attach 00100000 work This is the case MSL is built for. Nothing on your Mac has claimed the device, so MSL opens it and serves it to the instance directly. No password, no approval, nothing to grant. It appears in the guest a moment later and lsusb lists it like any other device.","s":""},{"u":"tools/usb-devices.html#a-device-macos-does-know","a":"USB devices","h":"A device macOS does know","c":"Tools Tab","x":"Most things you own are in this group: anything speaking USB serial (macOS ships drivers for the FTDI, CH34x, CP210x and Prolific chips), anything CDC-ACM, and every flash drive. macOS has already claimed it, so MSL has to take it, and taking hardware away from the system needs an administrator password. MSL asks for it with a prompt that says what it is for, and only a small helper program runs with those privileges - never the whole app, and never anything between one attach and the next. The attachment lasts as long as that command: press Ctrl-C, or close the window, and the device goes back. Note: a disk in this group is unmounted the hard way when it is taken. Eject it in Finder first, or anything still writing to it loses whatever it had not flushed. MSL warns you before asking for the password.","s":""},{"u":"tools/usb-devices.html#giving-it-back","a":"USB devices","h":"Giving it back","c":"Tools Tab","x":"msl usb detach 00100000 work macOS picks the device up again by itself. So does stopping or hibernating the instance - nothing is left stranded, and you never have to unplug something physically to get it back. An instance holding a device will not be paused or hibernated by the idle timer, because a paused machine cannot answer the device and it would appear to die.","s":""},{"u":"tools/usb-devices.html#checking-it-works-with-nothing-plugged-in","a":"USB devices","h":"Checking it works, with nothing plugged in","c":"Tools Tab","x":"msl usb test work This attaches a device MSL makes up: it has no hardware behind it, and exists so the whole path - the connection into the guest, the kernel attach, the protocol - can be checked on a Mac with nothing connected. If it appears in the instance, passthrough itself is working, and anything that then fails is about that particular device.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox.html","a":"The Sandbox tab","h":null,"c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"The Sandbox tab decides what an instance can reach. It has three parts: a header saying how locked-down the instance is, the four Gates, and Watch, which opens the Traffic Monitor.","s":"Four switches that decide what one instance can reach, and one button to seal it."},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox.html#posture","a":"The Sandbox tab","h":"The header","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"A lock icon and one word: Posture Icon Means Open open lock, grey Every gate is open. The instance has the network, your home folder, graphics and input. Partly sealed lock with clock, orange Some gates are closed — the line under it says how many. Sealed closed lock, red Every gate is closed: \"no network, no share, no new windows, no input.\" On the right, one button does everything at once: Seal it closes all four gates; Open everything opens them all.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox.html#gates","a":"The Sandbox tab","h":"The gates","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"Four switches, each Open or Cut: Network, Mac home share, Graphics (mslgd), and Keyboard & mouse. When one is cut, a line under it says exactly what that does. Each is covered in The four gates.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox.html#persist","a":"The Sandbox tab","h":"Gates stick","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"As the card's footnote says, gates are kept across restarts and re-applied every time the instance starts or resumes — \"so hibernating won't quietly hand anything back.\" You can close gates on a stopped instance, too; the header then says what it will have when it starts, which is the way to sandbox something before it ever runs.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox.html#limits","a":"The Sandbox tab","h":"What the Sandbox is — and isn't","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"Switches on top of the virtual machine Every gate is enforced by your Mac, from outside Linux. Nothing asks Linux to behave, so software inside it can't undo a gate. But they are switches on top of the virtual machine, not the security boundary itself — the virtual machine is that. They don't harden the VM against escape. The tab says this too when anything is closed. The switches show the truth, not the request The gates reflect what the virtual machine's devices are actually doing, as reported by MSL's background service — not just what was last asked for. If something settles differently from what you asked, a banner says \"The sandbox settled differently\".","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html","a":"The four gates","h":null,"c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"Each gate is honest about its scope, because \"cut the internet\" and \"unplug this VM's network card\" are not the same promise.","s":"Exactly what cutting the network, home share, graphics or input does — and doesn't do."},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html#network","a":"The four gates","h":"Network","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"The virtual network card is detached. The guest keeps its IP and routes but no packet reaches the host, so it looks like an unplugged cable rather than a firewall block. Linux can't reach the internet, your local network, or your Mac over the network. SSH stops working, because SSH uses the network. The SSH card says the address isn't answering and names this gate as a likely reason. msl shells, the Terminal tab and MSL Files keep working. They use a direct channel between your Mac and the virtual machine (vsock) that doesn't go through the network card at all.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html#home-share","a":"The four gates","h":"Mac home share","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"/mnt/mac stops resolving. Anything already reading a file there will see I/O errors, not a clean unmount. Linux loses access to your Mac home folder. Close anything using files in /mnt/mac before cutting it.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html#graphics","a":"The four gates","h":"Graphics (mslgd)","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"mslgd stops accepting new X11 connections. Windows that are already open keep their connection and keep drawing. New Linux app windows can't open. Apps already on screen carry on — cut this before launching things if you want none of them.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html#input","a":"The four gates","h":"Keyboard & mouse","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"Keyboard and pointer events are dropped on the host side, before they are encoded. The guest sees an idle input device, not a disconnected one. Linux app windows still draw, but typing and clicking in them do nothing. Handy for watching something run without nudging it by accident.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html#combining","a":"The four gates","h":"Combining them","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"You want Cut No internet, but keep working in the shell Network Keep a program away from your personal files Mac home share Stop an instance from putting windows on your screen Graphics Look but don't touch Keyboard & mouse Everything, for something you don't trust Seal it","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/sandbox-gates.html#protect","a":"The four gates","h":"This one matters for a protected image","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"The Mac home share is how instance disks are reachable from inside a guest: they live in your home folder, and the share is writable. If you are keeping an image from being copied, cutting this gate is the first step — see Protecting an image.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/traffic-monitor.html","a":"The Traffic Monitor","h":null,"c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"The Traffic Monitor is a small, live view of what's happening between your Mac and an instance. Open it from the Watch card on the Sandbox tab — Open, or ⇧⌘T while the Sandbox tab is showing. It slides down over the window; Done closes it. Along the top: Pause/Resume (freezes the list so you can read it), Clear (empties the log), and Done. Under that, two lanes.","s":"A live, minimalist log of what MSL and an instance are doing — and what Linux has connected."},{"u":"sandbox/traffic-monitor.html#msl-activity","a":"The Traffic Monitor","h":"MSL activity","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"MSL's own traffic: the things your Mac and the instance actually say to each other through MSL. Newest at the top, each with the time, a coloured icon and a short summary. Repeated events fold together with a count like ×12 instead of flooding the list. Category Colour Covers Control blue Commands to the background service: start, suspend, snapshot. Files green The file bridge — MSL Files and Finder reading and writing guest files. Display purple X11 connections, app windows, mslgd. Lifecycle orange The virtual machine itself: booted, paused, restored, stopped. Sandbox red Gates opening and closing. Click a category's chip to hide or show it. This instance only (on by default) hides other instances' events — MSL-wide events, like the service starting, always show because they're context. With nothing to show yet it says \"Listening…\" — start the instance, open a file or launch an app and it'll appear. The footer counts what's shown. Not a packet capture This lane shows MSL's own traffic, which your Mac can genuinely see. It doesn't show Linux's own internet connections — those live inside the virtual machine's network stack. That's the second lane.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/traffic-monitor.html#guest-connections","a":"The Traffic Monitor","h":"Guest connections","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"What Linux itself has open, read from Linux's own network stack once a second: Each row: TCP or UDP, then either \"listening on address:port\" or local → remote, the program that owns it, and its state (listening ones in green). Show processes matches each connection to the program that owns it. That means looking through every open file of every program in Linux, so it's only done while you're looking. Hide loopback (on by default) hides connections that never leave the guest, and says how many it hid. This lane reports sockets, not packets: that something is connected to an address, and who owns it — not what was sent. Only while it's running If the instance isn't running, this lane says so — and won't start it just to look, since that would change the very thing you're watching.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/guest-vpn.html","a":"A VPN inside one instance","h":null,"c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"The Sandbox tab can install the hide.me VPN client inside one instance. What it covers is the important part.","s":"Installing the hide.me client in an instance, and exactly whose traffic it covers."},{"u":"sandbox/guest-vpn.html#scope","a":"A VPN inside one instance","h":"It covers that instance, and nothing else","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"Not your Mac, and not your other instances The tunnel is a WireGuard interface inside that one Linux. Your Mac's own traffic is untouched, and so is every other instance. A VPN switch in a Mac app looks like it covers the Mac, so this is worth being clear about. That is also what makes it useful: one instance can be somewhere else while everything else on your Mac carries on normally.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/guest-vpn.html#what-msl-does","a":"A VPN inside one instance","h":"What MSL does and does not do","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"MSL does MSL does not Install the official client, at a pinned version whose exact contents it checks Handle your account, password or access token Report whether the client is installed, whether the kernel can do WireGuard, whether you are logged in and whether a tunnel is up Wrap the client's own commands in a button Tell you the next step, in the client's own words Connect or disconnect for you Connecting is you, in your own instance, with your own credentials: sudo hide.me token <server> sudo hide.me connect <server> The card tells you which of those is next.","s":""},{"u":"sandbox/guest-vpn.html#checks","a":"A VPN inside one instance","h":"The checks it reports","c":"Sandbox Tab","x":"Check Why it matters Installed The client is present in the instance. WireGuard The instance's kernel can carry the tunnel. Without it the client cannot connect at all. Logged in An access token is present. Connected A tunnel interface exists right now. WireGuard is checked before you get as far as an account, because finding out afterwards is a poor way to spend ten minutes. It needs the network gate open A VPN is still network traffic. With the Sandbox's Network gate closed nothing connects, including this.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-window.html","a":"The MSL Files window","h":null,"c":"MSL Files","x":"MSL Files is a file browser that can see both sides of MSL: your Mac's folders and the filesystem inside every running instance, in one window. Open it with Window ▸ MSL Files, or ⌥⌘F. It deliberately is a Finder look-alike — the same four views, the same sidebar, the same shortcuts — so there's nothing new to learn. What it adds is the part Finder can't do. Why not just Finder? Finder can reach a running instance — its files are served to your Mac as a real network volume — but it appears under a loopback IP address with no hint of which instance it is. MSL Files shows each instance by name, next to your own folders, and lets you drag between them.","s":"A Finder work-alike that shows your Mac and your running Linux instances side by side."},{"u":"files/files-window.html#layout","a":"The MSL Files window","h":"The window","c":"MSL Files","x":"The sidebar — Recents, your folders, Linux (one entry per running instance), and your Finder tags. See The Files sidebar. The toolbar — back, forward, enclosing folder, open; the four view buttons; sort; new folder, Quick Look, share, tags, trash; and a search field. The path bar under the toolbar — every folder above this one, each clickable. The status bar at the bottom — how many items (or \"3 of 12 selected\"), whether the place is read-only, and how much space is available. With no instance running and nothing selected, it says \"Nothing to browse — Start an instance to see its Linux filesystem here.\"","s":""},{"u":"files/files-window.html#search","a":"The MSL Files window","h":"Searching","c":"MSL Files","x":"The search field in the toolbar (⌘F) filters what's in the current folder by name, as you type. For searching everything, use Recents or a tag in the sidebar, which are Spotlight searches over your home folder.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-window.html#shortcuts","a":"The MSL Files window","h":"Finder's shortcuts","c":"MSL Files","x":"Every Finder shortcut you know works here. They're all listed in Keyboard shortcuts.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-sidebar.html","a":"The Files sidebar","h":null,"c":"MSL Files","x":"From top to bottom: Section Contains (no heading) Recents — files you've used lately, from Spotlight. Favorites Your home folder, Desktop, Documents, Downloads and Applications — whichever exist. Locations Macintosh HD, your Mac's whole disk. Linux Each running instance, by name, with an orange icon. \"No running instances\" when there are none. Tags Your Finder tags, each with its colour dot. Clicking one lists everything with that tag. A small lock beside a place means it's read-only for you; the status bar says \"Read-only\" too, and anything that would change it is greyed out. Tagging by dropping Drop files onto a tag in the sidebar to apply that tag, just like Finder.","s":"Recents, your folders, your Linux instances by name, and your tags."},{"u":"files/files-sidebar.html#instances","a":"The Files sidebar","h":"Instances come and go","c":"MSL Files","x":"The Linux section follows your instances: start one and it appears; stop or suspend it and it leaves. MSL Files never starts an instance by itself. The Go menu lists them too, under Finder's usual destinations.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-views.html","a":"Views, sorting and getting around","h":"The four views","c":"MSL Files","x":"View Shortcut as Icons ⌘1 as List ⌘2 as Columns ⌘3 as Gallery ⌘4 Or use the view buttons in the toolbar, or the View menu.","s":"Icons, list, columns and gallery; sorting and grouping; back, forward and Go to Folder."},{"u":"files/files-views.html#sorting","a":"Views, sorting and getting around","h":"Sorting and grouping","c":"MSL Files","x":"The arrows button in the toolbar (or View ▸ Sort By and Group By): Group by — None, Name, Kind, Date Modified, Size, or Tags. Sort by — Name, Size, Kind, Date Modified, or Date Added. Ascending, and Keep Folders on Top. Show Hidden Files — also ⇧⌘., Finder's own shortcut for it.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-views.html#show","a":"Views, sorting and getting around","h":"Showing and hiding","c":"MSL Files","x":"Shortcut Hide or show the sidebar ⌃⌘S Show Path Bar ⌥⌘P Show Status Bar ⌘/ Show Hidden Files ⇧⌘.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-views.html#navigating","a":"Views, sorting and getting around","h":"Getting around","c":"MSL Files","x":"Shortcut Back ⌘[ Forward ⌘] Enclosing folder ⌘↑ Open the selection ⌘↓, or double-click Go to Folder… ⇧⌘G — type a path like / or ~/ Recents ⇧⌘F Documents · Desktop · Home ⇧⌘O · ⇧⌘D · ⇧⌘H Downloads ⌥⌘L Computer · Applications · Utilities ⇧⌘C · ⇧⌘A · ⇧⌘U iCloud Drive ⇧⌘I, if you use it Click any folder in the path bar to jump straight to it. The Go menu also lists every running instance. Return renames, like Finder Return starts renaming the selected item; it doesn't open it. ⌘↓ or a double-click opens.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-moving.html","a":"Moving files between Mac and Linux","h":"Drag and drop","c":"MSL Files","x":"Drag files from Finder, or from anywhere in MSL Files, into a folder — on your Mac or inside an instance. They're copied; the originals stay where they were. An empty folder says \"Drag files here to copy them in.\" Places that are read-only won't accept a drop.","s":"Drag, copy and paste, duplicate, new folders, rename, compress, share."},{"u":"files/files-moving.html#copy-paste","a":"Moving files between Mac and Linux","h":"Copy and paste","c":"MSL Files","x":"Select files and press ⌘C, go somewhere else, and press ⌘V — within MSL Files, across the Mac–Linux boundary either way. The Edit menu's Select All Items (⌥⌘A) selects everything in the folder.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-moving.html#editing","a":"Moving files between Mac and Linux","h":"Making and changing things","c":"MSL Files","x":"Action How New folder ⇧⌘N, or the toolbar's folder button New folder containing the selection ⌃⌘N Rename Return, or right-click ▸ Rename… Duplicate ⌘D Compress into a zip File ▸ Compress Share the toolbar's share button, or File ▸ Share… Copy the path ⌥⌘C (File ▸ Copy as Pathname)","s":""},{"u":"files/files-moving.html#looking","a":"Moving files between Mac and Linux","h":"Looking closer","c":"MSL Files","x":"Quick Look — Space, or ⌘Y. A preview opens; Done closes it. Get Info — ⌘I. The item's thumbnail and name; General (Kind, Size, Where, Created, Modified); and Tags, as colour swatches to click. Reveal in Finder — ⌘R shows it in a real Finder window. The other way round Linux can already reach your Mac home folder at /mnt/mac, so for files under your home folder there's often nothing to copy at all. See Your home folder inside Linux.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-tags.html","a":"Tags","h":null,"c":"MSL Files","x":"MSL Files uses Finder's real tags — tag something here and Finder sees it, and the other way round.","s":"Finder tags that work in both directions, on your Mac's files."},{"u":"files/files-tags.html#adding","a":"Tags","h":"Adding and removing","c":"MSL Files","x":"The tag button in the toolbar, File ▸ Tags, or right-click ▸ Tags: choose a tag to add it; choose one with a tick to remove it. Clear Tags removes them all. Get Info (⌘I) shows the standard colours as swatches — click one to toggle it. Drop files onto a tag in the sidebar.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-tags.html#finding","a":"Tags","h":"Finding tagged things","c":"MSL Files","x":"Each tag in the sidebar lists everything carrying it, using Spotlight across your home folder. Group by ▸ Tags groups a folder's contents by tag.","s":""},{"u":"files/files-deleting.html","a":"Deleting files","h":null,"c":"MSL Files","x":"Move to Trash — ⌘⌫, the toolbar's trash button, or right-click — works as you'd expect on your Mac's files: they go to the Trash, and you can get them back. Inside Linux there's no Trash An instance's filesystem has no Trash. Deleting there asks first — \"This volume has no Trash.\" with **Delete n Items Permanently** — and \"Deleting here cannot be undone\" means exactly that. A safety net for big clean-ups Before deleting a lot inside Linux, consider a snapshot — or copy what matters to your Mac first.","s":"The Trash on your Mac, and permanent deletion inside Linux."},{"u":"files/files-open-in-msl.html","a":"Opening a folder in a Linux shell","h":null,"c":"MSL Files","x":"Right-click a folder in MSL Files for two ways into a terminal: Open Folder in MSL — a Linux shell in Terminal.app, already in that folder. Open in Terminal — a normal Mac shell in Terminal.app, in that folder.","s":"Right-click a folder to open a Linux shell already sitting in it."},{"u":"files/files-open-in-msl.html#which","a":"Opening a folder in a Linux shell","h":"Which instance?","c":"MSL Files","x":"The folder is… Open Folder in MSL… Inside an instance opens there: \"Open Folder in MSL (work)\". Under your Mac home folder, one instance running opens in that one, through /mnt/mac. Under your home folder, several running offers a submenu to choose. Under your home folder, none running is greyed out — /mnt/mac only exists inside a running instance. Outside your home folder (/Applications, another drive) is greyed out — Linux can't see it. So a project in ~/Code/thing opens as /mnt/mac/Code/thing inside Linux: same files, Linux tools.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/mslgd.html","a":"mslgd, the display server","h":null,"c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Linux apps draw through mslgd, an X11 display server written for MSL. It is not XQuartz and does not need it: a Linux app connects to mslgd, and mslgd puts a real Mac window on your screen.","s":"What draws Linux windows on your Mac, and why each app gets its own process."},{"u":"graphics/mslgd.html#per-app","a":"mslgd, the display server","h":"One process per app","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Every Linux app runs in its own process on the Mac side. That is what gives each one its own Dock tile, its own icon, its own name in the menu bar and its own tiles in Mission Control — instead of everything appearing as \"MSL\". It has one visible consequence: two Linux apps cannot see each other's clipboard directly, because X11 clipboards pass between programs rather than being stored anywhere. The shared clipboard is what joins them up, through the Mac's.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/mslgd.html#support","a":"mslgd, the display server","h":"What it supports","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Enough of X11 that ordinary desktop applications work: windows, input, fonts, images, shaped windows, the extensions toolkits check for on startup, and OpenGL through GLX. GTK, Qt and the toolkits built on them are what it is tested against. X11, not Wayland Apps that only speak Wayland will not find a display. Most Linux desktop software still speaks X11, or speaks both and will use X11 when that is what it finds.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/mslgd.html#gate","a":"mslgd, the display server","h":"Turning it off for one instance","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"The Sandbox tab's Graphics gate stops an instance opening new windows at all — see The four gates. Windows already open keep drawing.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/opengl-apps.html","a":"Running OpenGL apps","h":null,"c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"OpenGL apps work. There are two ways they can render, and which is better depends on the app.","s":"How 3D apps render here, which path suits which app, and what to do when one refuses to start."},{"u":"graphics/opengl-apps.html#paths","a":"Running OpenGL apps","h":"The two paths","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Path OpenGL version Good for CPU (the default) 4.5 Anything, including apps needing newer OpenGL. Slower for heavy 3D. GPU (opt in) 4.1 Apps that stay at 4.1 or below and want the speed. The CPU path is software rendering — correct, complete, and slow in proportion to how much the app draws. The GPU path sends OpenGL to your Mac's real graphics hardware, and is capped at OpenGL 4.1 by the Mac side of it. Newer than 4.1 will not run on the GPU This is the single most useful thing to know here. Blender wants OpenGL 4.3: on the GPU it reports unsupported hardware and will not start properly, and on the CPU it runs. If a 3D app refuses to start only when GPU acceleration is on, this is why. Turning the GPU path on at all is GPU acceleration for OpenGL in Experimental Features. Individual apps can override it from their right-click menu, which is how you keep one app on the CPU while everything else uses the GPU.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/opengl-apps.html#checking","a":"Running OpenGL apps","h":"Checking what an app is getting","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"From a terminal in the instance: glxinfo | grep -E \"OpenGL (vendor|renderer|version)\" A renderer naming llvmpipe is the CPU path. Anything else is the GPU path. glxgears is the quickest way to see whether GL works at all. If glxinfo is missing, install mesa-utils.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/opengl-apps.html#wont-start","a":"Running OpenGL apps","h":"When an app will not start","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"It says Try Unsupported hardware, or needs OpenGL 4.2 or newer Put that app on the CPU Cannot open display Check the Graphics gate on the Sandbox tab Nothing, and exits immediately Launch it from a terminal to see its own message Lost connection to rendering server The GPU renderer is not reachable; use the CPU Launching from a terminal in the instance is worth doing early. A Linux app that fails on startup almost always says why, and that message never reaches the Mac side.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/libgl-tricks.html","a":"Environment tricks for stubborn apps","h":null,"c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Most apps need none of this. When one misbehaves, these are the levers, and they are the same ones you would use on any Linux machine with software rendering. Run an app with a variable set from a terminal in the instance: LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 some-app Or, from your Mac, through the msl command: msl <instance> env LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 some-app","s":"The handful of variables that rescue an app which will not render, scale or start."},{"u":"graphics/libgl-tricks.html#variables","a":"Environment tricks for stubborn apps","h":"The useful ones","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Variable What it does Reach for it when LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 Forces Mesa's software renderer An app renders wrongly on the GPU path GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe Picks the software driver outright The same, more firmly GSK_RENDERER=cairo GTK4 draws without OpenGL A GTK4 app shows a blank or black window QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software Qt Quick draws without OpenGL A Qt app's interface is blank or garbled SDL_VIDEODRIVER=x11 Stops SDL choosing Wayland An SDL game exits saying it cannot find a display SDL_RENDER_DRIVER=software SDL draws without GL An SDL game opens a window and draws nothing GDK_BACKEND=x11 Stops GTK choosing Wayland A GTK app cannot find a display QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb The same for Qt A Qt app cannot find a display MSL already sets the first few of these for apps it launches, so an app started from the Applications tab usually has sensible values. These are for when you are running something by hand, or an app needs something unusual.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/libgl-tricks.html#love","a":"Environment tricks for stubborn apps","h":"Older games and LOVE apps","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Older games, and anything built on an old LOVE (love2d) runtime, are the most common thing to need help. They tend to ask for an OpenGL setup that assumes a real graphics card and give up quietly when they do not get it. In rough order of what to try: Run it from a terminal, so you can see what it actually says. SDL_VIDEODRIVER=x11 — many exit immediately without this because they try Wayland first. LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 — on the CPU path, where OpenGL 4.5 is available. SDL_RENDER_DRIVER=software if it opens a window but draws nothing. If it is packaged as a Flatpak or Snap, try the plain distribution package instead: sandboxed packages bring their own graphics libraries, which is one more thing between the app and mslgd. Flatpaks and Snaps are a different environment A sandboxed app cannot see /tmp the way an ordinary one can, and brings its own copy of Mesa. When an app works as a distribution package and not as a Flatpak, that difference is usually why — it is not a fault in either.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/libgl-tricks.html#persisting","a":"Environment tricks for stubborn apps","h":"Making it stick","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Adding a line to your shell profile in the instance makes a variable apply to everything you start from a shell: echo 'export LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1' >> ~/.profile For a single app, a better place is the app's own .desktop entry, so launching it from the Applications tab gets the same treatment.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/mslgd-quirks.html","a":"Known quirks","h":null,"c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"mslgd is a display server written from scratch, and a few things are still rough. None of these damage anything; they are worth recognising so you do not spend time chasing them.","s":"The rough edges worth recognising, so you can tell a quirk from something broken."},{"u":"graphics/mslgd-quirks.html#quirks","a":"Known quirks","h":"Things you might meet","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"What you see What it is A Wayland-only app finds no display mslgd is X11. Try GDK_BACKEND=x11 or QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb, or use an X11 build. An app repaints only part of its window Usually an OpenGL app on the GPU path. Try it on the CPU. Menus in the window and in the menu bar The app publishes menus and also draws them. Turn off the menu bar switch for now. An app's menus are missing entirely The same switch, the other way: turn it off and the app draws its own again. A dialog opens behind its window Some toolkits place dialogs themselves; moving it is harmless. Very old toolkits look plain Motif and similar are supported enough to run, not to look modern.","s":""},{"u":"graphics/mslgd-quirks.html#logs","a":"Known quirks","h":"Reading what an app actually said","c":"Graphics & mslgd","x":"Each Linux app that MSL launches keeps its own log. If an app closes on its own or never appears, the log is where its last words are — see Logs and doctor for where to find them. Starting the app from a terminal inside the instance is the other half of this, and often quicker: you see its output as it happens. Try it without MSL's help first If an app behaves oddly, run it from a terminal in the instance with nothing set. That separates \"this app is unhappy here\" from \"one of MSL's switches does not suit this app\", and the two have very different fixes.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/experiments.html","a":"What experimental means here","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Experimental Features is in the sidebar, under the instance list. It is the only place any of these change, and it says how many are on.","s":"Why these are switches rather than simply how MSL works, and what to do when one misbehaves."},{"u":"experiments/experiments.html#stability","a":"What experimental means here","h":"They are on, and they still might bite","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Most of these start on, because MSL is much less pleasant without them: a Mac keyboard that does nothing in Linux apps, a clipboard that stops at the window edge, menus drawn inside the window instead of in the menu bar. They are still called experimental, and that word is doing real work: What \"experimental\" is warning you about Each of these reaches into software MSL did not write — a toolkit's idea of where its file dialog comes from, an app's idea of what Ctrl+C means, a desktop's idea of who owns the menus. MSL can be completely correct and an app can still do something odd, because the app has its own opinion. So they can have occasional stability issues: an app whose menus do not appear, a dialog that opens in the wrong place, a shortcut that lands somewhere unexpected. None of it damages anything, and every one of them is a switch. If something behaves strangely, turn the matching switch off. Everything goes back to how that app behaves on an ordinary Linux desktop. That is the whole point of them being switches, and it is a better first move than working around the symptom.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/experiments.html#applies","a":"What experimental means here","h":"When a change takes effect","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Each switch carries a small badge saying when it applies: Badge Means Live Straight away, including for apps already open. New apps Apps launched after the change. Ones already running keep the old behaviour. Restart The instance has to be restarted. A switch that says New apps has not failed if nothing changes in the window you are looking at — close the app and open it again.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/experiments.html#groups","a":"What experimental means here","h":"Grouped by what they change","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Group What it covers Graphics and input (mslgd) How Linux windows look and how they take the keyboard and mouse. Peripherals (mslps) The Mac's camera and microphone, for Linux apps. MSL Where Linux and the Mac meet: links, files, the clipboard, the menu bar. Reset all at the top puts every switch back to what MSL ships. Settings you have chosen are kept When a later version of MSL turns something on by default, it changes only that switch. Anything you have set for yourself stays as you left it.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Three switches, all on by default, that let a Mac keyboard drive a Linux app.","s":"Command-key shortcuts, Mac text navigation, and why terminals are treated differently."},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html#shortcuts","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":"Mac shortcuts in Linux apps","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Command-key shortcuts arrive as the Control-key ones Linux apps expect. You press The Linux app sees ⌘C, ⌘V, ⌘X Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+X ⌘Z, ⇧⌘Z Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z ⌘S, ⌘O, ⌘N, ⌘W Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+N, Ctrl+W ⌘F, ⌘A, ⌘P Ctrl+F, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+P Anything else with Command follows the same rule, keeping Shift and Option.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html#reserved","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":"macOS keeps its own","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Some shortcuts never reach Linux, because they are the Mac's: Kept by macOS What it still does ⌘Q Quits the app, with its usual \"save your work?\" prompt ⌘H, ⌥⌘H Hide, Hide Others ⌘M, ⌥⌘M Minimise, Minimise All ⌘` Cycle that app's windows anything with ⌃⌘ Full screen, lock screen, the character picker ⌘Space, ⌘Tab Spotlight and app switching, which macOS takes first Plain Control is never touched — it is Linux's own modifier, and Ctrl+C in a Linux app means what it has always meant.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html#terminals","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":"Terminals are different, on purpose","c":"Experimental Features","x":"In a terminal, Ctrl+C is not copy. It interrupts whatever is running. A straight Command-to-Control mapping would mean pressing ⌘C to copy killed your build, ⌘D closed the shell and ⌘Z suspended it. So MSL recognises terminal emulators and treats them separately: Terminal What ⌘C does GNOME Terminal, Konsole, kitty, alacritty, foot, Tilix, Terminator and similar Arrives as Ctrl+Shift+C, which is what those terminals bind copy to xterm, urxvt, st Nothing at all Why xterm gets nothing Ctrl+Shift+C is a convention the newer terminals adopted; the classic X terminals never did. In those, Shift does not change the character sent, so Ctrl+Shift+C would arrive as a plain Ctrl+C and interrupt the running command anyway. Doing nothing is the safe answer. Those terminals have never copied with a key combination: select text with the mouse to copy it, and paste with the middle button. In the terminals that do bind them, ⌘⌫ clears to the start of the line and ⌥⌫ deletes the word before the cursor, which is what a shell prompt understands.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html#text","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":"Mac text navigation","c":"Experimental Features","x":"The Mac's text moves, translated for Linux text fields: You press The Linux app sees ⌘← / ⌘→ Home / End ⌘↑ / ⌘↓ Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End (start and end of the document) ⌥← / ⌥→ Ctrl+← / Ctrl+→ (a word at a time) ⌘⌫ Delete to the start of the line ⌥⌫ Delete the word before the cursor Holding Shift extends the selection, as it does everywhere else.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html#option","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":"The Option keys","c":"Experimental Features","x":"A Mac's Option key does two jobs a PC keyboard splits between two keys: it is Alt for shortcuts, and it types characters like a-acute and the trademark sign. The Option keys choice decides which job each one does in Linux apps: Choice Left Option Right Option Like a PC (default) Alt Types special characters (AltGr) Both are Alt Alt Alt Like a Mac app Special characters Special characters","s":""},{"u":"experiments/mac-keyboard.html#screenshots","a":"The Mac keyboard in Linux apps","h":"Screenshot keys","c":"Experimental Features","x":"With this on, ⇧⌘3, ⇧⌘4 and ⇧⌘5 are sent through to Linux as Print, Shift+Print and Alt+Print — the keys Linux screenshot tools listen for. macOS takes these first It is on by default and usually does nothing, because macOS claims those combinations for its own screenshots before any app sees them. It only takes effect once you have turned the macOS ones off in System Settings, under Keyboard, Keyboard Shortcuts, Screenshots.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/shared-clipboard.html","a":"The shared clipboard","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Copy something in a Linux app and it is on your Mac's clipboard immediately. Copy on the Mac and Linux apps can paste it. On by default. It is also what lets two Linux apps share a clipboard at all: every Linux app gets its own process, and X11 clipboards are passed directly between programs rather than stored anywhere, so without a bridge the two could not see each other.","s":"Copy in Linux and paste on the Mac, and back again — text, images and files."},{"u":"experiments/shared-clipboard.html#what","a":"The shared clipboard","h":"What crosses","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Copied Linux to Mac Mac to Linux Text Yes Yes Images Yes, as PNG Yes, as PNG Files Use MSL Files Yes, from your Mac home folder A copy that is both text and a picture keeps both, so whichever an app asks for is the one it gets.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/shared-clipboard.html#files","a":"The shared clipboard","h":"Files, from the Mac","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Copy a file in Finder and a Linux app can paste it. The path a Linux app receives points into /mnt/mac, which is where your Mac home folder appears inside every instance. Only files under your Mac home folder A file on an external disk, or in another user's home folder, has no Linux equivalent — Linux genuinely cannot reach it. Those are left out rather than handed over as a path that does not exist. It is always a copy, never a move, whatever you pressed. Nothing crossing this boundary will delete the original on the other side. Going the other way — Linux files onto the Mac's clipboard — is MSL Files' job. It is the file browser for both sides and can put real files on the clipboard itself.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/shared-clipboard.html#limits","a":"The shared clipboard","h":"What it does not cover","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Selecting text is not copying X11 has a second clipboard: text you have merely selected with the mouse, pasted with the middle button. MSL deliberately does not bridge it, because it changes every time you drag across any text — mirroring that onto the Mac would overwrite whatever you had copied, constantly. Programs that only use it, like xterm and some very old ones, will not reach the Mac's clipboard. Copy from their own menu where they have one. Items marked private by password managers are skipped on purpose, so they are never carried into an instance.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/msl-files-integration.html","a":"MSL Files as the Linux file browser","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"With Set MSL Files as the default file browser for Linux on — which it is by default — Linux apps use MSL Files instead of a Linux file manager.","s":"What turning Linux's file manager and file dialogs into MSL Files actually changes."},{"u":"experiments/msl-files-integration.html#what","a":"MSL Files as the Linux file browser","h":"What changes","c":"Experimental Features","x":"In a Linux app You get Opening a folder, or \"show in folder\" MSL Files, at that folder An app's Open or Save As dialog MSL Files, as the dialog Starting the file manager from the Linux applications menu MSL Files, at that instance's home folder The dialog is the same browser as the rest of MSL Files — the same sidebar, view modes, sorting and search — with Cancel and Open along the bottom. It can see your Mac's disk as well as the instance's, which a Linux file chooser cannot.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/msl-files-integration.html#why","a":"MSL Files as the Linux file browser","h":"Why it is experimental","c":"Experimental Features","x":"It reaches into each toolkit There is no single way to ask \"every Linux app\" where its file dialog comes from. MSL uses four different mechanisms for GTK, Qt, Firefox and sandboxed apps, and an app that does something unusual can end up with its own dialog anyway — or, rarely, with one that behaves oddly. If an app's Open or Save starts acting strangely, turn this off. Everything goes back to that app's own dialog, which always works. Some apps will keep their own dialog whatever this is set to, because they draw it themselves in a way nothing outside them can redirect. That is not a fault; those apps simply work as they do on Linux.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/msl-files-integration.html#off","a":"MSL Files as the Linux file browser","h":"Turning it off","c":"Experimental Features","x":"With it off, folders open in whatever Linux file manager is installed (Nautilus, Dolphin, and so on), and Open and Save dialogs are the app's own. MSL Files still works as a Mac app — it is only the Linux side that stops pointing at it.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/global-menu-bar.html","a":"Linux menus in the Mac menu bar","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"A Linux app's menus — File, Edit, View — appear in the Mac's menu bar at the top of the screen, the way a Mac app's do, instead of being drawn inside the window. On by default. This is what makes a Linux window feel like a window rather than a window with a menu bar inside it.","s":"Moving an app's File and Edit menus to the top of the screen, and which apps can do it."},{"u":"experiments/global-menu-bar.html#fitting","a":"Linux menus in the Mac menu bar","h":"When there are more menus than fit","c":"Experimental Features","x":"A long menu bar runs into a MacBook's notch. MSL keeps what fits and tucks the rest behind one final menu, so nothing is lost on a smaller screen.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/global-menu-bar.html#support","a":"Linux menus in the Mac menu bar","h":"Not every app can do it","c":"Experimental Features","x":"The app has to publish its menus MSL reads menus over D-Bus, which means the app has to export them. Most GTK and Qt applications do. Some do not, and a few publish only part of what they show. When an app does not, its menus stay inside its window and everything still works — you have just not gained anything for that app. If an app's menus are missing from the menu bar and from its window, that is worth turning the switch off for: some apps hide their in-window menu bar as soon as anything claims to be handling menus for them, and then publish nothing. Applies to apps launched after the change, so close an app and reopen it to see the difference.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/sign-in-pages.html","a":"Signing in to Linux apps","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Links from Linux apps open on your Mac - that is the point of the integration. Sign-in pages are the one exception, and they have to be.","s":"Why a sign-in page opens in Linux when every other link opens on your Mac."},{"u":"experiments/sign-in-pages.html#why","a":"Signing in to Linux apps","h":"Why the Mac cannot finish a sign-in","c":"Experimental Features","x":"A \"sign in with...\" flow ends by handing its answer back to the machine that started it, in one of two ways: How it comes back What it means To 127.0.0.1 on some port The Linux app is listening there. Your Mac is not. To a private address like discord:// Only Linux knows which app that belongs to. Open that page on your Mac and the answer arrives on your Mac: the browser either shows an error, or silently hands it to a Mac app of the same name. The Linux app never hears anything and waits forever. So the sign-in page - and only that page - opens in the instance's own browser, where the whole round trip stays on one machine.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/sign-in-pages.html#what","a":"Signing in to Linux apps","h":"What you will see","c":"Experimental Features","x":"You press \"Sign in\" in a Linux app. A browser window opens inside Linux, not on your Mac. You sign in there. The app picks up where it left off. The one cost is that this browser is not your Mac's, so it has none of your saved passwords. It is usually a one-time step per app.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/sign-in-pages.html#ordinary","a":"Signing in to Linux apps","h":"What still opens on your Mac","c":"Experimental Features","x":"Everything else. MSL decides by reading the link, and only diverts one that carries the marks of an authorisation request - a sign-in address to return to, or an app identifier with a response type. An ordinary link, a documentation page, a search result, a link in an email: all still open in your own browser. It needs a browser in the instance With no browser installed in Linux there is nothing to open the page in, so the link goes to your Mac as before. That will not complete the sign-in, but a page that opens somewhere beats a page that opens nowhere.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/sign-in-pages.html#wrong","a":"Signing in to Linux apps","h":"If it gets one wrong","c":"Experimental Features","x":"This is new, and it judges by reading the link. If an ordinary page starts opening in the guest's browser, turn off Sign-in pages open in Linux in Experimental Features - every link then goes to your Mac again, and sign-ins go back to not completing.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/gpu-acceleration.html","a":"GPU acceleration for OpenGL","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Off by default. With it on, Linux OpenGL is rendered on your Mac's real GPU through Metal instead of on the CPU.","s":"Running Linux OpenGL on the Mac's real GPU, and the version ceiling that decides whether you want to."},{"u":"experiments/gpu-acceleration.html#ceiling","a":"GPU acceleration for OpenGL","h":"The ceiling, which is the whole decision","c":"Experimental Features","x":"The GPU path stops at OpenGL 4.1 The Mac side goes through Apple's OpenGL, which caps at 4.1. The CPU path — the default — offers 4.5. So anything that needs newer than 4.1 will not run on the GPU at all, while it runs fine on the CPU. Blender is the usual example: it wants 4.3 and reports unsupported hardware on the GPU, while working on the CPU. Good on the GPU Better on the CPU Chromium's GL, many games, older CAD Blender and anything else needing OpenGL 4.2 or newer Anything that stays at OpenGL 4.1 or below Anything that renders incorrectly on the GPU","s":""},{"u":"experiments/gpu-acceleration.html#renderer","a":"GPU acceleration for OpenGL","h":"It needs a piece that is installed separately","c":"Experimental Features","x":"The GPU path needs a renderer on the Mac side. Without it this switch does nothing at all and apps stay on the CPU — quietly, which is the right failure: you get working software rather than a broken window.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/gpu-acceleration.html#per-app","a":"GPU acceleration for OpenGL","h":"Per-app, not just global","c":"Experimental Features","x":"This switch is the default for every app, but each app can be set on its own from its right-click menu in the Applications tab — see The app right-click menu. An app pinned to the CPU stays there whatever this switch says, which is how you keep Blender working while everything else uses the GPU. Takes effect after the instance restarts.","s":""},{"u":"experiments/native-scaling.html","a":"Native scaling","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Off by default. Linux windows are normally drawn one X pixel per point, which is what every app has been tested at. With this on they are drawn closer to the Mac's own interface size. Worth trying if Linux apps look small next to Mac ones. Some apps lay out well when scaled and some do not — toolkits vary in how much they believe the display's reported size — which is why it is off by default rather than on. Applies to apps launched after the change.","s":"Drawing Linux windows closer to the Mac's own interface size."},{"u":"experiments/camera-and-mic.html","a":"The camera and microphone prompt","h":null,"c":"Experimental Features","x":"Off by default. When a Linux app opens the camera or the microphone, MSL Peripherals asks you first. With this on, it does not. Right for something that starts camera nodes over and over — robotics work is the usual case — and wrong if you would rather be asked. The normal answer to \"a Linux app wants your camera\" is to be asked. macOS still asks, and the lights still show This only skips MSL's prompt. macOS asks for its own permission once, which nothing can skip, and the camera light and the microphone indicator still appear whenever something is actually capturing.","s":"Skipping MSL's own permission prompt when a Linux app opens the camera or microphone."},{"u":"power/power-events.html","a":"Sleep, shutdown and battery","h":null,"c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"A virtual machine that's writing to its disk when the power goes is how Linux disks get damaged. So whenever your Mac is about to sleep or stop, MSL's first move is to freeze every running instance — near-instant, with no disk activity — so none of them is mid-write. Only then does it do anything slower.","s":"MSL freezes every instance before your Mac sleeps, shuts down, logs out or runs out of battery."},{"u":"power/power-events.html#table","a":"Sleep, shutdown and battery","h":"What happens when","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Your Mac… MSL… Sleeps (lid closed, Apple menu ▸ Sleep) Freezes every instance. They stay in memory. Shuts down or restarts Freezes every instance, then hibernates each one to disk. Logs out The same as shutting down. Is on battery at 10% or below The same as shutting down, before the battery gives out. Switches to another user Nothing — instances keep running for you, like everything else in your session. MSL never holds up your Mac shutting down.","s":""},{"u":"power/power-events.html#wake","a":"Sleep, shutdown and battery","h":"Waking up","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"After your Mac wakes: Instances that were frozen stay Suspended until something uses them — open a shell or an app, or click Resume. Open terminal sessions have ended — the connection didn't survive the sleep. The Terminal tab shows \"Session ended\"; click Reconnect. MSL corrects the clock of instances that are running, since a frozen instance's clock stops while your Mac sleeps.","s":""},{"u":"power/power-events.html#shutdown","a":"Sleep, shutdown and battery","h":"Shutting down","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Shutdown, restart and logout hibernate every running instance, so next time you start one it picks up exactly where it was. Before that, MSL asks open Linux apps to close, the way their own close buttons would — so an app with unsaved work gets its chance to ask. Linux app windows don't come back The instance resumes, but its app windows don't: each window's connection to your Mac ended when the Mac shut down. Save your work in Linux apps before shutting down or restarting. If time runs out macOS only waits so long. If an instance can't finish saving in time, it was still frozen first, so its disk is safe — it simply starts fresh next time instead of resuming, and Linux tidies its filesystem up as it starts, as it would after a power cut. Trying it without shutting down msl power-test sleep (or shutdown, logout, lowbattery, wake) runs MSL's response by hand, without your Mac actually doing it. msl power-test battery shows what the battery watcher sees.","s":""},{"u":"power/idle.html","a":"Idle instances","h":null,"c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"A running instance uses processor time and battery even when nothing's happening inside it. So MSL tidies up after you.","s":"Instances nobody's using suspend themselves, and wake the moment you need them."},{"u":"power/idle.html#last-session","a":"Idle instances","h":"When the last session closes","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"When the last shell or Linux app on an instance closes, MSL suspends it about three seconds later. The dot turns orange. Using it again resumes it straight away — opening the Terminal tab, msl work in a terminal, opening one of its apps, or clicking Resume. Resuming from suspend is almost instant, so mostly you won't notice. What keeps an instance awake: an open shell, in the app, in Terminal.app, or from msl; an open Linux app.","s":""},{"u":"power/idle.html#long-idle","a":"Idle instances","h":"Left alone for a while","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Instances that have been left alone for a while may also be hibernated, to give their memory back to your Mac. Starting one resumes it exactly where it was — it just takes a few seconds rather than being instant.","s":""},{"u":"power/idle.html#power","a":"Idle instances","h":"Around sleep and shutdown","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"While your Mac is going to sleep or shutting down, idle suspending stands aside so it can't get in the way of MSL protecting instances.","s":""},{"u":"power/shared-disks.html","a":"Shared disks","h":null,"c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Each distro has one disk image on your Mac, and every instance of that distro uses it. Two Debian instances, one Debian disk.","s":"Instances of the same distro share one disk — so only one of them can run at a time."},{"u":"power/shared-disks.html#why","a":"Shared disks","h":"Why","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Distro disks are big, and a copy per instance would fill your Mac quickly. Sharing means a new instance costs almost nothing.","s":""},{"u":"power/shared-disks.html#means","a":"Shared disks","h":"What it means","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Only one instance per distro can run at a time. Two running machines writing to one disk would wreck it, so MSL refuses: \"'other' is already running and shares distro's disk with 'this' — running both at once would corrupt it; stop 'other' first.\" Files are shared. Anything saved inside Linux on work is there on scratch, if they're the same distro. So is your Linux user. Disk size and storage settings are shared — see Disk size and storage. Disk maintenance blocks the whole distro while it runs.","s":""},{"u":"power/shared-disks.html#separate","a":"Shared disks","h":"What's separate","c":"Power & Lifecycle","x":"Each instance still has its own name, CPUs and memory, sandbox gates, SSH shortcut, hibernated session, snapshots, and Mac apps. Two Linux machines at once Use two different distros — they have separate disks and run side by side happily. Snapshots and shared disks Because the disk is shared, running any instance of a distro changes the disk every snapshot of that distro was taken against. See the snapshot rule.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-what.html","a":"What publishing does","h":null,"c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"MSL's normal SSH gets you into an instance from this Mac. Publishing gets somebody in from anywhere — a laptop on another network, a phone, a colleague's machine. That turns an instance into a small server: something you leave running and reach when you need it. msl public expose Ubuntu You get back an address and the command to use it: abinaash@mlisv-218-212-52-212.run.pinggy-free.link:38003 Anyone with that address, and a key you've allowed, can log in with ordinary ssh. They need no account, no MSL, and nothing installed — which is the whole point of doing it this way.","s":"Gives an instance an address that works from any computer, anywhere."},{"u":"public/public-what.html#how","a":"What publishing does","h":"How it works","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"The tunnel runs on your Mac, not inside the instance. MSL already reaches the guest's SSH port on your local network, so it forwards that through a relay and out to the internet. The instance itself is never changed to make this work, and nothing is installed in it.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-what.html#limits","a":"What publishing does","h":"What it costs","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"By default MSL uses a free public relay, which means two honest limits: Free relay Your own server Address Random, and changes Yours, and stays Session About an hour, then renewed at a new address No limit Needs Nothing A machine you can already ssh to The free relay is right for \"let me show you this\". For something you actually depend on, publish through a server you own — see Through your own server. The Mac has to stay awake While anything is published, MSL stops your Mac going to sleep on its own, and keeps the instance from hibernating. Closing the lid still sleeps it — a laptop in a bag is not a server. MSL reconnects when it wakes, at a new address if it is using the free relay.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-what.html#local","a":"What publishing does","h":"Just reaching it from this Mac","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"If you only want a server inside Linux to open in a browser on this Mac, you do not need any of this — see Reaching a port inside Linux.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-publishing.html","a":"Publishing and stopping","h":"In the app","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"Select the instance, and look for Reach this from anywhere under the SSH card. Press Publish…, confirm, and the address appears. Stop puts everything back.","s":"One command or one button, and the same to undo it."},{"u":"public/public-publishing.html#cli","a":"Publishing and stopping","h":"From the command line","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"Command What it does msl public expose <instance> Publishes it, and prints the address msl public status <instance> Shows the current address msl public revoke <instance> Stops publishing, and undoes the changes msl public keys <instance> Lists the computers allowed in msl public authorize <instance> <key.pub> Lets another computer in msl public expose asks for confirmation first. Add --yes in a script.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-publishing.html#persistence","a":"Publishing and stopping","h":"It stays published","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"Publishing survives restarts. If you shut the Mac down with an instance published, MSL starts that instance and publishes it again when it comes back — because a machine you are using as a server should come back as a server, without you being there. With the free relay the address will be a different one, so check msl public status after a restart.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-publishing.html#reconnecting","a":"Publishing and stopping","h":"It repairs itself","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"A free relay ends each session after about an hour. MSL notices and opens a new one, so the instance stays published — at a new address each time. The app shows Published, but not reachable this moment during the handover rather than showing you an address that no longer works.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-publishing.html#stopping","a":"Publishing and stopping","h":"Stopping","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"msl public revoke closes the tunnel, deletes the drop-in MSL added to the SSH server, and removes the key MSL made for your Mac. Keys you authorized stay, because those are the list of people allowed into that instance, and losing them because a tunnel closed would be the wrong thing to do. Remove them with msl public deauthorize.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-keys.html","a":"Letting another computer in","h":null,"c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"A published instance accepts keys only. Passwords are turned off before anything is published. MSL makes one key for the Mac doing the publishing, so you can get in straight away. That key does not survive a restart, on purpose — it is stored outside your home folder where no instance can read it, and that place is cleared when the Mac restarts. For any computer you actually intend to use, authorize its key instead.","s":"Each computer keeps its own key; you paste its public half."},{"u":"public/public-keys.html#other","a":"Letting another computer in","h":"On the other computer","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"Get its public key. If it has none: ssh-keygen -t ed25519 cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub That prints one line starting ssh-ed25519. That line is public — it is safe to email, paste in chat, or put in a ticket.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-keys.html#here","a":"Letting another computer in","h":"On this Mac","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"msl public authorize Ubuntu ~/their-laptop.pub work-laptop Or paste it into the app: Reach this from anywhere ▸ Let another computer in… Never send a private key The file without .pub is the private half, and it should never leave the computer it was made on. MSL refuses one if you paste it by mistake.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-keys.html#roster","a":"Letting another computer in","h":"Who can get in","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"msl public keys Ubuntu Each line shows a name and a fingerprint. Remove one with: msl public deauthorize Ubuntu work-laptop This is also how you'd run an instance for a team: one key each, each removable on its own.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-email.html","a":"Emailing the address out","h":null,"c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"On the free relay the address changes about once an hour. Rather than you fielding \"what's the address now?\", MSL can email it. Each person gets their own key the first time, attached to the email, with the exact commands. After that they only get a short note when the address moves — same key, new port.","s":"MSL can email the address to whoever needs it, so nobody has to ask you."},{"u":"public/public-email.html#setup","a":"Emailing the address out","h":"Setting it up","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"MSL sends through Resend, which is free. Make an account Verify a domain you own at resend.com/domains Make an API key at resend.com/api-keys msl public notify setup Then add people: msl public notify add Ubuntu sam@work.com Sam Or in the app: Reach this from anywhere ▸ Email the address to someone… A domain is required to email anyone else This is Resend's rule, not MSL's. Their free sender (onboarding@resend.dev) can only deliver to the address your own Resend account uses. With a domain verified — the free plan includes one — you can email anybody. Without one you can still set this up to email yourself, which is useful on its own, since you're the person being asked.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-email.html#emails","a":"Emailing the address out","h":"What gets sent","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"When What they get You add them, or first publish Their key attached, chmod 600, and the ssh line The address changes Just the new command — nothing to save Nobody gets an endless drip: rotation emails are capped per person per day, and the cap lifts once somebody actually connects.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-email.html#key","a":"Emailing the address out","h":"Where the API key is kept","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"In /Users/Shared/MSL, readable only by you. Deliberately not in Application Support: MSL shares your whole home folder into every instance as /mnt/mac, so a key there could be read from inside any Linux instance. /Users/Shared sits outside that share.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-email.html#commands","a":"Emailing the address out","h":"Commands","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"Command What it does msl public notify setup Connect Resend msl public notify test <you@…> Prove it works before anyone relies on it msl public notify add <instance> <email> [name] Email this person the address msl public notify list <instance> Who's on the list msl public notify remove <instance> <email> Stop emailing them msl public notify forget Remove the Resend setup entirely Taking somebody off the list does not revoke their key — that's msl public deauthorize, so the two are never confused.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-server.html","a":"Through your own server","h":null,"c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"The free relay changes your address about once an hour. If you want one that stays put, publish through a machine you already have — a cheap VPS, a box at the office, anything with a public address. msl public expose Ubuntu --via=root@my.server.com:2222 People then connect to my.server.com on port 2222, and land in your instance.","s":"An address that is yours and does not change."},{"u":"public/public-server.html#requirements","a":"Through your own server","h":"What the server needs","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"That you can already ssh to it. MSL runs ssh as you, so your own config, keys and agent apply exactly as they do when you type it yourself. There is no MSL account, nothing to install, and no credential for MSL to keep. One setting on that server matters. In its /etc/ssh/sshd_config: GatewayPorts yes Without it, SSH binds the forwarded port to the server's own loopback and nobody else can reach it — the tunnel works perfectly and answers no one. MSL checks the port really answers before telling you it published, and says so if this is why it didn't. Pick a port above 1024 Ports below 1024 need root on that server, and its SSH won't bind one for an ordinary account.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-server.html#benefits","a":"Through your own server","h":"What you get","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"Address Fixed — put it in ~/.ssh/config, give it to people, point a name at it Expiry None In the path Only your own machine","s":""},{"u":"public/public-safety.html","a":"What MSL changes to keep it safe","h":null,"c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"A port 22 that faces the internet starts receiving automated password guessing within minutes. MSL's instances ship with password logins on, which is fine on your own network and not fine on a public address — so publishing changes that first.","s":"Password logins off, keys only, and nothing you cannot undo."},{"u":"public/public-safety.html#changes","a":"What MSL changes to keep it safe","h":"What it changes","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"MSL writes one file in the instance, /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-msl-public.conf: Setting Why PasswordAuthentication no Password guessing becomes pointless KbdInteractiveAuthentication no The same thing by another name PermitRootLogin prohibit-password root by key only, never by password MaxAuthTries 3 Fewer guesses per connection LoginGraceTime 20 Connections that never log in get dropped AllowAgentForwarding no, AllowTcpForwarding no A borrowed key reaches less Then it asks the SSH server what it actually believes, rather than trusting that writing the file was enough. If passwords are somehow still on, MSL refuses to publish and tells you. Writing a setting and having it take effect are not the same thing, and this is the difference between secured and apparently secured.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-safety.html#reach","a":"What MSL changes to keep it safe","h":"What someone who logs in can reach","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"More than the Linux machine. MSL puts your Mac's home folder inside every instance, at /mnt/mac, and it is writable — that's what makes Linux apps able to open your Documents and save back to them. An SSH login has exactly the same access an app in that instance does. So publishing an instance shares those files with whoever you let in, and the people you authorize should be people you would hand your Mac to. Worth knowing before you hand out an address. It is not something MSL changes for you.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-safety.html#never-locked-out","a":"What MSL changes to keep it safe","h":"You cannot be locked out","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"None of this can shut you out of your own instance. msl <instance> does not use SSH at all — it reaches the guest by a private channel between the Mac and the virtual machine. A lost key costs you a remote login, never the machine.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-safety.html#privacy","a":"What MSL changes to keep it safe","h":"What stays yours","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"The tunnel's own connection is kept away from your ~/.ssh entirely: MSL does not read your config and does not offer your private keys to a relay.","s":""},{"u":"public/public-safety.html#honest-limits","a":"What MSL changes to keep it safe","h":"What MSL cannot promise","c":"Reaching it from anywhere","x":"A free relay is somebody else's machine. Your SSH session is encrypted end to end inside it, so the relay cannot read it — but it can see that a connection exists. Use your own server if that matters. A closed lid still sleeps the Mac, and a sleeping Mac answers nothing. Anyone with the address can try. They will not get in without a key you allowed, but they can try.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap.html","a":"The MSL Admin Portal","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"The Admin Portal — MSLAP — is how a Mac running MSL can be told what it is allowed to do. A school, a company, or you on your own Mac can deny particular things: creating instances, reaching the internet, sharing the Mac's home folder, copying an image. Open it from Window ▸ MSL Admin Portal.","s":"What an administrator can restrict, and what MSL promises about it."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap.html#audiences","a":"The MSL Admin Portal","h":"Two audiences, one window","c":"Admin Portal","x":"The window is built for two people who want opposite things from it. Somebody using a managed Mac opens it to find out why something refused them. That is the whole top half: who set the policy, what it says, and the reason they wrote. A locked-down tool that will not say why it refused is the thing people hate most about managed machines, so MSL always says. An administrator opens it to see the resolved rules and set them up. That is behind a password. Nothing is restricted by default A Mac with no policy installed works exactly as it always did. MSLAP only ever takes things away, and only when somebody installs a policy saying so.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap.html#limits","a":"The MSL Admin Portal","h":"What it is not","c":"Admin Portal","x":"MSLAP decides what MSL will do. It is not a device management system and does not pretend to be: It cannot stop somebody using a Linux shell inside an instance they are allowed to run. Root inside a guest is the guest kernel's business, and nothing on the Mac side can change that — so MSL does not list it as something it can restrict. It assumes a Mac being administered by MSLAP is a Mac being administered. If an organisation deploys a policy, the person using that Mac is not a local administrator of it — that is what device management is for. On your own Mac you are the administrator, and there is nothing to defend against. Every restriction MSL claims has a place in the code that actually refuses it. A capability that nothing enforces is not listed as enforced — see What can be restricted.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap.html#next","a":"The MSL Admin Portal","h":"Where to go next","c":"Admin Portal","x":"You want to Read Set it up on this Mac Setting it up See the full list of switches What can be restricted Know how much a policy really guarantees How strong a policy is Understand a message that refused you Reading a refusal Deploy to more than one Mac Managing several Macs","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-setup.html","a":"Setting it up","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"Open Window ▸ MSL Admin Portal on a Mac with no policy and the window says This Mac is not managed, with a Set Up… button.","s":"An administrator password and a signing key, and what each one is actually for."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-setup.html#three","a":"Setting it up","h":"The three things, and why they are three","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Setup asks for a password and makes a key. They do different jobs, and blurring them is how somebody ends up believing a password protects a file that anyone can edit. What it does What it does not do Administrator password Unlocks the editing view in this window. Protect the policy file. Anyone who can read the disk can read the file. Signing key Makes the policy tamper-evident: edit it and it stops verifying. Stop it being deleted. Where the file lives A root-owned location cannot be changed by a non-administrator. Stop an administrator of the Mac. Only all three together make a policy enforceable. How strong a policy is explains which rung a given setup is on, and MSL always tells you rather than letting you infer it.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-setup.html#policy","a":"Setting it up","h":"Setting a policy","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Unlock with the password and the window lists all the capabilities with a switch each. Turn off what this Mac may not do, write a reason — it is shown on every refusal — and apply. MSL signs it and installs it for your user. That is the tamper-evident rung. To go further: msl admin enrol ~/Library/Application\\ Support/MSL/policy.json which copies it to /Library/Application Support/MSL/ as root, where somebody who is not an administrator of this Mac cannot change or remove it.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-setup.html#checking","a":"Setting it up","h":"Checking it","c":"Admin Portal","x":"msl admin status msl admin status <instance> The first shows the whole policy and its strength; the second resolves it for one instance, which is what actually applies when that instance does something. Set a policy that denies something harmless first — system.doctor, say — and run the command it governs. Seeing the refusal once tells you more about how this works than reading about it.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-setup.html#undo","a":"Setting it up","h":"Undoing it","c":"Admin Portal","x":"msl admin unenrol Removes the policy. If it is in the root-owned location this asks for your Mac password, because that is the point of it being there.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-capabilities.html","a":"What can be restricted","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"A policy is a list of capabilities that are denied. Everything not denied is allowed, so a new MSL feature is never silently restricted by an old policy. msl admin capabilities prints every one with what it covers and where it is enforced.","s":"The full list of switches, grouped, and the rule that keeps the list honest."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-capabilities.html#groups","a":"What can be restricted","h":"The groups","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Group Covers instance Creating, deleting, starting, stopping, renaming, cloning, locking instances resources Memory, CPUs, disk size, fixed or dynamic memory distro Installing and removing distros, custom images, where images come from shipment Importing and exporting .mslship files network The VPN, DNS, the sandbox network gate, the traffic monitor, forwarded ports public Publishing an instance to the internet, and each backend sharing The Mac home share, the clipboard, MSL Files, the Finder mount, the file picker peripherals USB, audio, camera, microphone graphics GPU acceleration, per-app graphics, scaling apps Launching Linux apps, launch commands, Launchpad, the Dock, scanning integrations Links and mail opening on the Mac, the global menu bar, shortcuts, sign-in system Experimental features, updating, startup, uninstalling, the terminal, SSH, diagnostics admin Removing or editing the policy itself Denying a group denies everything under it: network covers network.vpn. Whole segments only — denying net denies nothing.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-capabilities.html#scopes","a":"What can be restricted","h":"Scopes","c":"Admin Portal","x":"A rule applies to or to instance names, including lab-. So a lab Mac can forbid copying the teaching image while leaving a student's own instances alone. Scopes match by instance name, so renaming or cloning an instance could escape a rule written about it. MSL refuses a rename or clone that would do that, naming the capability — see Rename and clone.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-capabilities.html#honesty","a":"What can be restricted","h":"The honesty rule","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Every capability says where it is enforced: Tier Means daemon mslhd refuses the request. Binds the app and the command line alike. app Refused in the app or CLI, for things the daemon never sees. interface only A hidden control, and nothing more. That last one is deliberately unflattering, and MSL labels it rather than hiding it. system.terminal is interface-only: hiding MSL's Terminal tab does not stop anybody typing msl <instance> in Terminal.app, and the refusal on screen says exactly that. A capability with nowhere that refuses it is not shipped as enforced. A test walks the whole list against the real enforcement sites, so the catalogue cannot drift into a list of promises nothing keeps.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-strength.html","a":"How strong a policy is","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"msl admin status prints a Strength line, and the panel shows the same. It is measured from the file, never inferred from where it sits. Rung What it means Someone can still Advisory A policy the person using the Mac can simply edit. Change it to whatever they like. Tamper-evident Signed. It can be deleted, not forged. Delete it, which is visible. Enforceable Signed and root-owned. Nothing, unless they are an administrator of this Mac.","s":"Three rungs, what each one actually guarantees, and why MSL tells you which you are on."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-strength.html#measured","a":"How strong a policy is","h":"Why it is measured","c":"Admin Portal","x":"A policy under /Library/Application Support/MSL is not automatically enforceable: that folder is group-writable by admin on some systems, and a policy placed there by an ordinary user would otherwise claim the top rung it has not earned. MSL checks the file's owner and permissions and reports what it finds. Say it, do not imply it Somebody deploys on the strength of that one line. It is the deployment MSL is least willing to be vague about.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-strength.html#signing","a":"How strong a policy is","h":"Signing","c":"Admin Portal","x":"A signed policy is checked against the public keys this Mac has pinned. Three different failures, reported differently, because they mean completely different things: Reported Means not signed Nobody vouched for it. signed by a key this Mac has not pinned Somebody vouched for it, but not somebody you said to trust. the signature does not match It has been edited since it was signed.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-strength.html#broken","a":"How strong a policy is","h":"When a policy is broken","c":"Admin Portal","x":"If the policy is present but will not verify, MSL falls back to the last policy that did verify rather than to no policy at all. Editing a policy to break it must not be a way to remove it. It also does not refuse everything on a corrupt file. A machine that fails closed on an unreadable policy is a bricked machine, including the owner's ability to fix it — so MSL says so loudly, repeatedly, and keeps working on the last good rules.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-strength.html#local","a":"How strong a policy is","h":"Local admin mode","c":"Admin Portal","x":"If you set MSLAP up on your own Mac, the signing key is on that Mac. Anyone with administrator access could re-sign a policy. What it still buys is that casually editing it breaks it — which is the whole point for one person locking down their own machine. A fleet keeps its key somewhere else entirely; see Managing several Macs.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-refusals.html","a":"Reading a refusal","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"A refusal looks like this: ERROR refused by MSL Admin Portal: instance.delete is not allowed - Ubuntu is the working instance - no deleting it. Set by Abi. Four parts, and each is there on purpose. Part Why instance.delete The exact capability, so you can look it up or ask for it by name. is not allowed What happened. Nothing was changed. the reason Written by whoever set the policy. Set by Abi Who to ask. A managed Mac that will not say why it refused is the thing people hate most about managed machines, so MSL always says all four.","s":"What a refusal message tells you, and what to do about it."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-refusals.html#broader","a":"Reading a refusal","h":"Denied by something broader","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Sometimes you will see (denied by public). That means the administrator denied the whole public group, not that one capability. Useful, because asking for public.relay back when the whole group is denied is the wrong conversation.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-refusals.html#never","a":"Reading a refusal","h":"Things that are never refused","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Anything that makes the Mac less exposed goes through, whatever the policy says: Unpublishing an instance, removing an authorised key, forgetting a notification address Closing a forwarded port, detaching a USB device Locking an instance, removing a peripheral share, turning startup off Clearing a per-app launch command Otherwise denying a capability after somebody had already turned something on would trap it on, with no way back. Queries are never refused either: you can always read what the rules are.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-refusals.html#app","a":"Reading a refusal","h":"In the app","c":"Admin Portal","x":"The same thing in a window: a control that a policy forbids is disabled with the reason as its tooltip, or the panel explains it where the control would be. The reason is always the administrator's own words.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-fleet.html","a":"Managing several Macs","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"Local admin mode keeps the signing key on the Mac it manages, which is fine for one person and wrong for an organisation. For several Macs, keep the private key on a machine you control and give each Mac only the public half.","s":"Sign on one machine, deploy to many, and keep the key off them."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-fleet.html#authoring","a":"Managing several Macs","h":"On the machine that holds the key","c":"Admin Portal","x":"msl admin keygen msl admin sign policy.json keygen prints the public key. Keep the private half here and nowhere else.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-fleet.html#deploying","a":"Managing several Macs","h":"On each managed Mac","c":"Admin Portal","x":"msl admin pin <base64-public-key> sudo msl admin enrol policy.json pin says \"trust policies signed by this key\". enrol puts the policy in the root-owned location. Those two together are what the Enforceable rung means. The key never arrives A Mac that has only the public half can verify a policy and cannot create one. That is the difference between a fleet deployment and a local one, and it is the whole reason to do it this way.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-fleet.html#updating","a":"Managing several Macs","h":"Updating a policy","c":"Admin Portal","x":"Sign a new one and enrol it. Macs pick it up on the next check — nothing needs reinstalling, and an instance that is running keeps running.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-fleet.html#nomdm","a":"Managing several Macs","h":"What this does not need","c":"Admin Portal","x":"No server, no agent, no account. A policy is one signed JSON file; getting it onto the Macs is whatever you already use for files — your MDM's file deployment, a script, a shared drive.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-lineage.html","a":"Certifying and withdrawing an image","h":null,"c":"Admin Portal","x":"An instance carries a signed lineage saying where it came from — see Where an instance came from. A lineage certificate is you saying something about one of those lineages. msl admin certify <instance|lineage> --label \"Acme Standard Image 2026\" msl admin revoke <instance|lineage> --reason \"leaked\" You can name an instance rather than hunting for its lineage.","s":"Sign a statement that an image is yours, or that it is retired."},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-lineage.html#unforgeable","a":"Certifying and withdrawing an image","h":"Why this one is different","c":"Admin Portal","x":"An instance's own identity is signed by a key on the Mac that made it. A certificate is signed by your MSLAP key, which a fleet keeps somewhere else — so nobody holding only one of your Macs can make one. It is the only part of this that holds even on a Mac you no longer trust.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-lineage.html#certify","a":"Certifying and withdrawing an image","h":"Certifying","c":"Admin Portal","x":"A certificate says this image is ours. Combined with denying instance.acceptUncertified, a Mac will run only images you have signed for.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-lineage.html#revoke","a":"Certifying and withdrawing an image","h":"Withdrawing","c":"Admin Portal","x":"A revocation says this image is retired. Every instance of that lineage then refuses to start, and no capability can set that aside — a revocation a policy could override would be no revocation at all. A withdrawn image stops starting on every Mac the certificate reaches. The instances are not deleted and their files are untouched, but nobody can boot them until the certificate is removed.","s":""},{"u":"admin-portal/mslap-lineage.html#deploy","a":"Certifying and withdrawing an image","h":"Deploying one","c":"Admin Portal","x":"certify writes the file under your own Library. For a fleet, copy it to each Mac at /Library/Application Support/MSL/lineages/ with sudo, where somebody who is not an administrator cannot add or remove one. msl identity <instance> says whether the certificate it found is root-owned or merely advisory.","s":""},{"u":"updates/updates.html","a":"How updating works","h":null,"c":"Updates","x":"MSL checks whether there is a newer version and tells you. It never downloads or replaces anything by itself.","s":"Where MSL looks for a new version, how often, and what it will and will not do on its own."},{"u":"updates/updates.html#what","a":"How updating works","h":"What happens","c":"Updates","x":"MSL reads the list of releases from its repository. It works out which of them are newer than the copy you are running. Of those, it works out which ones are meant for your copy — see Release tags. The best one becomes the offer, and you are told about it. Choosing to update opens the release page in your browser. You download and install it yourself. Installing stays manual, deliberately MSL runs a background service and a virtual machine with your files in it. Replacing its own binaries underneath a running instance is not something it should do quietly while you are working.","s":""},{"u":"updates/updates.html#frequency","a":"How updating works","h":"How often it looks","c":"Updates","x":"Once a day at most. If the check fails — no network, the service is unreachable — MSL waits an hour, then two, then four, up to a day, rather than asking again on every launch. A failed check is never reported as a problem. Not being able to reach the internet is not an MSL error.","s":""},{"u":"updates/updates.html#ordering","a":"How updating works","h":"Every release is read, not just the newest","c":"Updates","x":"The release list is ordered by date published, which is not the same as version order: a small fix to an older version can be published after a big new one, and would appear first. MSL reads them all and compares versions properly, so a 1.0.9 patch published last week never looks newer than 2.0.0.","s":""},{"u":"updates/update-tags.html","a":"Release tags: DNUO, UBP, FP and the rest","h":null,"c":"Updates","x":"A release tag can carry a marker saying who that release is for. It looks like this: v<major>.<minor>.<patch>[-<MARKER>[-<minimum>]] So v2.4.0-UBP-2.1 is version 2.4.0, marked UBP, with a minimum of 2.1.","s":"What the marker on a release tag means, and why a release can be mentioned without being offered."},{"u":"updates/update-tags.html#markers","a":"Release tags: DNUO, UBP, FP and the rest","h":"The markers","c":"Updates","x":"Marker Means If you are below the minimum (none) An ordinary release — DNUO Do not update older installs Never mentioned UBP Unsafe-behaviour patch — offer it at once Mentioned, but you cannot take it UBPDN Unsafe-behaviour patch, newer installs only Never mentioned FP Feature patch Mentioned, but you cannot take it FBDN Feature patch, newer installs only Never mentioned","s":""},{"u":"updates/update-tags.html#minimum","a":"Release tags: DNUO, UBP, FP and the rest","h":"What the minimum is for","c":"Updates","x":"The minimum is the oldest version of MSL a release is any use to. A patch that fixes something introduced in 2.1 cannot help you if you are on 2.0 — the problem is not there. So v2.4.0-UBP-2.1 is urgent for anyone on 2.1 or newer, and for anyone older it is merely mentioned: you are told it exists, and told it is not for your version.","s":""},{"u":"updates/update-tags.html#outcomes","a":"Release tags: DNUO, UBP, FP and the rest","h":"Mentioned, offered, or silent","c":"Updates","x":"This is the difference the DN in a marker makes: You are at or above the minimum You are below it UBP / FP Offered Mentioned, cannot be taken UBPDN / FBDN Offered Not mentioned at all DNUO Offered Not mentioned at all A release being mentioned rather than offered is not a problem to solve. It means the newer version exists and does not apply to the copy you are running. Urgent patches still reach you A release marked UBP is one where the old behaviour can do something you would not want. Those are raised as soon as they are found, rather than waiting for the next time you happen to look — which is the whole reason the markers exist rather than every release being equal.","s":""},{"u":"updates/update-notes.html","a":"What the update notice tells you","h":null,"c":"Updates","x":"When there is a release worth telling you about, MSL shows the version, what kind of release it is, and whatever notes the release carries.","s":"Where the text in an update notice comes from, and what to read before taking one."},{"u":"updates/update-notes.html#source","a":"What the update notice tells you","h":"Where the notes come from","c":"Updates","x":"Each release can ship a short plain-text file alongside it. MSL fetches that and shows it in the notice. It is written to lead with anything you have to do by hand. If a release has no such file, the notice says the release page has notes worth reading and points you there, rather than inventing a summary.","s":""},{"u":"updates/update-notes.html#before","a":"What the update notice tells you","h":"What to check before updating","c":"Updates","x":"Anything you must do by hand. The notes lead with this when there is any. Whether instances need to be stopped. MSL will tell you; when in doubt, stop them. Your own settings. Updating keeps them. A new version that turns something on by default changes only that switch, not choices you have made. Your instances are not touched Updating MSL replaces MSL. Instances, their disks and their snapshots live separately and are left exactly as they are. Uninstalling explains where they live and what removing MSL does and does not take with it.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-wont-start.html","a":"An instance won't start","h":"Start is greyed out","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Why What to do A spinner is showing — it's busy. Wait for it to finish. The capacity pill is orange — four are running. Hibernate or shut one down. Suspending doesn't free a slot. See Concurrency. There's an **Install Distro** button instead. The distro isn't downloaded. See Installing a distro.","s":"Greyed-out Start buttons, refusals, and what each message means."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-wont-start.html#messages","a":"An instance won't start","h":"Messages, and what they mean","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"\"4 instances are already running … suspend or shut one down first\" Every slot is taken. Hibernate or shut one down. \"'other' is already running and shares distro's disk with 'this'…\" Another instance of the same distro is running. Only one can use the disk at a time — stop that one first. See Shared disks. \"didn't start: the filesystem check at start found a problem\" Check-at-start is on and found damage. Repair the disk, then start it. \"check-at-start is on, but e2fsck isn't installed on this Mac\" Install e2fsprogs (brew install e2fsprogs), or turn the check off on the Maintenance card. \"maintenance is running on this instance's disk\" A disk tool is working on it. It can start once that finishes. \"the guest kernel panicked while booting — its filesystem is probably damaged\" Linux couldn't start from its disk. With the instance stopped, check the disk, then repair it. The log has the details: ~/Library/Logs/MSL/mslhd.log. \"shell connection timed out\" The connection to the instance got stuck. Try again — MSL starts a fresh machine. \"MSL's background service isn't running\" See The service isn't running.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-service.html","a":"The service isn't running","h":null,"c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Everything in MSL goes through its background service, mslhd. When it isn't running, nothing can start.","s":"When MSL's background service is missing, stopped, or out of date."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-service.html#signs","a":"The service isn't running","h":"Signs","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"With no instance selected: \"MSL's background service isn't running\", and a Start it button. Errors ending \"MSL's background service isn't running.\" The Tools tab's Background service card says Not running. Linux apps in the Dock don't open.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-service.html#fixes","a":"The service isn't running","h":"Fixes, in order","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Click Start it (or quit and reopen MSL, which starts it too). Make sure Start MSL's service automatically is on, on the Tools tab. Run msl doctor in Terminal — it checks whether the service is reachable and whether MSL's tools are installed. See Logs and msl doctor. Read ~/Library/Logs/MSL/mslhd.log — the last lines usually say why it stopped.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-service.html#outdated","a":"The service isn't running","h":"\"an older version\"","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"The Maintenance card may say \"MSL's background service answered, but not in a way this version understands\". That's the service from before your last update, still running. The new one takes over the next time you log in — log out and back in to switch now.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-apps.html","a":"Linux apps won't appear or open","h":"The grid is empty","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"\"No GUI applications found\" — nothing installed has a .desktop entry. Command-line programs never do. Install a GUI app inside Linux, then Refresh. See Your first Linux app. \"No applications yet\" with **Install Distro** — the distro isn't downloaded yet.","s":"Empty app grids, apps that won't open, and windows that misbehave."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-apps.html#missing","a":"Linux apps won't appear or open","h":"I installed something and it isn't there","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Click Refresh — MSL only reads the list when asked. Check the source picker isn't filtering it out (choose All), and that the search field is empty.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-apps.html#wont-open","a":"Linux apps won't appear or open","h":"It won't open","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Check Because Is the Graphics gate cut on the Sandbox tab? It stops new windows opening. Is the capacity pill orange? Opening an app has to start the instance, and every slot is taken. Is the background service running? It's what runs Linux apps. See The service isn't running. Does it run from the Terminal tab? Type its command there — errors print right in the terminal. An orange \"Couldn't start app\" banner includes the reason. Apps started from the Dock or Spotlight have no terminal, so they write problems to ~/Library/Logs/MSL — Open Logs on the Tools tab.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-apps.html#dock","a":"Linux apps won't appear or open","h":"An app in my Dock does nothing","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"If the instance it belongs to has been removed, its Mac apps were removed too — but a Dock tile can outlive them. Remove the tile. msl doctor lists apps pointing at removed instances, and msl doctor --fix cleans them up.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-apps.html#drawing","a":"Linux apps won't appear or open","h":"The window looks wrong","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"mslgd is MSL's own display server, and some apps use parts of X11 it doesn't draw perfectly yet. Try the app from another source (a Flatpak, say). See Living with Linux windows.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-apps.html#clipboard","a":"Linux apps won't appear or open","h":"Copy and paste doesn't work between apps","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Not yet — only within one Linux app. See Copy and paste.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-ssh.html","a":"SSH won't connect","h":"\"isn't answering on port 22\"","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Is the Network gate cut? On the Sandbox tab. Cutting it unplugs the virtual network card SSH uses. The app's own terminal and msl shells still work, because they don't use the network. Is the instance running? SSH needs it up. Click Set up again on the SSH card — it redoes everything, including restarting the SSH server.","s":"Not answering on port 22, stale details, and setup that fails."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-ssh.html#stale","a":"SSH won't connect","h":"\"Last known details\"","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Not a problem: the card is showing details from when the instance last ran. It rechecks once the instance is running.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-ssh.html#failed","a":"SSH won't connect","h":"Setup failed","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"The card shows the reason and a Try again button. Setting up installs things inside Linux, which needs the instance running — and, the first time, may need the network.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-ssh.html#tools","a":"SSH won't connect","h":"VS Code or another tool can't find it","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"They read ~/.ssh/config. Check the Host msl-work entry is there; Set up again rewrites it. Copy all details on the card gives you everything to paste elsewhere.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-disk.html","a":"The disk is full","h":null,"c":"Troubleshooting","x":"There are two different \"full\"s, and the Storage card on the Overview tab shows both.","s":"Running out of space inside Linux, or on your Mac."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-disk.html#guest-full","a":"The disk is full","h":"Full inside Linux","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"\"No space left on device\" inside Linux, or the usage bar is orange. Make room inside: run Clear caches inside Linux from the Maintenance card, and delete what you don't need. Raise the limit: drag the Storage card's slider up. The disk grows and Linux's filesystem is stretched to match at the next start. Let it grow by itself: with a dynamic disk, Grow automatically when it runs low adds 8 GB at a start whenever it's 85% full, up to the limit.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-disk.html#host-full","a":"The disk is full","h":"Full on your Mac","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"A dynamic disk only takes the space it has written, but that grows as Linux writes — and it never shrinks back, even after deleting files inside Linux. The Storage card shows what it really takes (\"Actually used on your Mac\"), and its footnote shows your Mac's free space. See Reclaiming disk space for what can be deleted.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-filesystem.html","a":"Filesystem errors","h":"Signs","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"\"Read-only file system\" when writing — Linux switched the disk to read-only after finding a problem. \"Input/output error\" on files. The instance won't start: \"the guest kernel panicked while booting\". Check for disk errors on the Maintenance card finds something.","s":"Read-only filesystems, I/O errors, and instances that won't boot — how to check and repair."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-filesystem.html#steps","a":"Filesystem errors","h":"What to do","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Save anything you can — copy important files out to /mnt/mac or with MSL Files. Shut the instance down. Check Disk on the Maintenance card (needs e2fsprogs on your Mac). If it finds problems, Repair Disk…. A backup is taken first. Start the instance and see how it is. If things are worse, Restore… puts the old disk back. When all's well, Discard the backup. No e2fsprogs? Try the maintenance boot.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-filesystem.html#prevent","a":"Filesystem errors","h":"Keeping it from happening","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Shut Linux down properly — sudo poweroff inside it, or msl --shutdown — rather than the app's Shut Down, which stops it at once. See the note on Shut Down. Don't restore snapshots after the disk has changed. Consider checking at every start.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-clock.html","a":"The clock is wrong","h":null,"c":"Troubleshooting","x":"A frozen or hibernated instance's clock stops, so after a long pause it can be hours behind. That isn't only cosmetic: it breaks secure web connections (\"certificate is not yet valid\"), confuses make and git, and causes errors that look unrelated. MSL fixes the clock of running instances automatically after your Mac wakes. Anything else: Sync clock with the Mac on the Maintenance card. It says how far off it was. Check the time inside Linux with date.","s":"Wrong time inside Linux after sleep or hibernation, and why it matters."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-maintenance.html","a":"The Maintenance card says…","h":null,"c":"Troubleshooting","x":"The card says What to do \"This distro isn't installed yet, so there's no disk to check or repair.\" Nothing to maintain yet. \"Checking what's available…\" Wait a moment. \"MSL's background service isn't answering…\" See The service isn't running. \"…answered, but not in a way this version understands…\" An older service is still running. Log out and back in. \"e2fsck isn't installed…\" Copy Install Command, then run it in Terminal. \"Shut the instance down first…\" Stop it. See the note on Shut Down. \"This instance has a hibernated session…\" Start it, then shut it down, then try again. \"Maintenance is already running on this disk.\" Wait for it to finish. \"Start the instance to use these.\" The utilities run inside Linux. \"MSL's background service didn't answer in time. It may still be working — check again in a moment.\" A long job may still be running. Wait, then check again.","s":"Every message the Maintenance card can show instead of working, and what to do."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-memory.html","a":"Memory trouble","h":"Your Mac gets slow","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Instances' memory can't be paged out, so too much of it leaves macOS short. Hibernate instances you aren't using — suspended ones still hold their memory. Lower their memory on the Overview tab — especially above the three-fifths warning. Use dynamic memory, so idle instances give memory back.","s":"Your Mac gets slow, or programs inside Linux are killed for running out of memory."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-memory.html#guest-oom","a":"Memory trouble","h":"Programs in Linux get killed","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"When Linux runs out of memory it stops a program to survive — often the biggest one. Linux images don't use swap, so there's no slow fallback first. Raise the instance's memory (manual), or its maximum (dynamic), then restart it. On dynamic memory, the live readout's Guest stalling on memory shows when it's struggling.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html","a":"Linux has no internet","h":null,"c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Work down this list. Each step rules out one layer, and the first one that fails is the answer.","s":"Working out where the connection stops: the gate, the guest, DNS, or your Mac."},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html#gate","a":"Linux has no internet","h":"1. Is the network gate open?","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"The Sandbox tab's Network gate detaches the virtual network card. It is kept across restarts, so an instance you sealed last week is still sealed. This is by far the most common cause, and it looks exactly like a broken connection: no internet, no local network, SSH not answering. Note that msl shells, the Terminal tab and MSL Files keep working with it closed, because they do not use the network — so \"the terminal works but nothing downloads\" points straight here.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html#address","a":"Linux has no internet","h":"2. Does the guest have an address?","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"In the instance: ip addr ip route You want an address on the main interface and a default route. If there is no address, ask for one: sudo dhclient -v","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html#ping","a":"Linux has no internet","h":"3. Can it reach anything by number?","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"ping -c 3 1.1.1.1 Working here but not by name means DNS, which is the next step. Failing here with the gate open points at your Mac's own connection — check that the Mac itself is online.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html#dns","a":"Linux has no internet","h":"4. Can it resolve names?","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"getent hosts deb.debian.org cat /etc/resolv.conf \"Temporary failure in name resolution\" from apt is almost always this. /etc/resolv.conf should name a resolver; if it is empty or points somewhere unreachable, that is the fault.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html#clock","a":"Linux has no internet","h":"5. Is the clock right?","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"A clock that is badly wrong breaks anything using certificates, which looks like a network problem: downloads fail, apt complains that release files are not valid yet. See The clock is wrong.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/trouble-network.html#symptoms","a":"Linux has no internet","h":"What each symptom usually means","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Symptom Usually Terminal works, nothing downloads The Network gate is closed ping 1.1.1.1 works, names do not DNS Everything fails, the Mac is also offline Your Mac's connection apt says release files are not valid yet The clock SSH refused but shells work The Network gate, or SSH is not running Watching it live The Traffic Monitor's Guest connections lane shows what Linux itself has open, read from Linux's own network stack. It is the quickest way to see whether an app is even trying to connect.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/logs-and-doctor.html","a":"Logs and msl doctor","h":"Logs","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"MSL's logs are in ~/Library/Logs/MSL. Open Logs on the Tools tab opens the folder. mslhd.log — the background service: every start, stop, power event and error, with the details behind the short messages in the app. Mac apps started from the Dock or Spotlight write their problems here too, since they have no terminal.","s":"Where MSL writes what it's doing, and the command that checks your installation."},{"u":"troubleshooting/logs-and-doctor.html#doctor","a":"Logs and msl doctor","h":"msl doctor","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"In Terminal: shell msl doctor It checks for things no other command shows, and lists each with ✓, a warning or a problem: Whether MSL's command-line tools are installed. Whether the background service is reachable. Every instance, and whether its distro is downloaded. Leftover files from removed instances, and cached app lists for them. Mac apps still pointing at removed instances. How much space the disk images take in total. It only reports. If it finds leftovers, it lists them and tells you to run: shell msl doctor --fix which deletes exactly what it listed — nothing else.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/reclaim-space.html","a":"Reclaiming disk space","h":"What takes space","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Everything is in ~/Library/Application Support/MSL: File What Size rootfs.img (Alpine), and rootfs-<distro>.img for the rest — rootfs-debian.img, rootfs-rocky.img and so on Each distro's disk. Whatever Linux has written. vm-<instance>.state A hibernated session. About the instance's memory. vm-<instance>-<snapshot>.state A snapshot. About the instance's memory. <disk>.pre-repair A backup from a disk repair. Grows as the repaired disk changes.","s":"What takes space on your Mac, what can go, and what can't be shrunk."},{"u":"troubleshooting/reclaim-space.html#options","a":"Reclaiming disk space","h":"What you can do","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Discard repair backups you no longer need, on the Maintenance card. Remove instances you don't use — that deletes their hibernated sessions and snapshots. See Removing an instance. Delete a distro's disk image, with every instance of that distro stopped. The space comes back immediately; the next start downloads the distro fresh. Deleting a disk image deletes its files Everything inside Linux on that distro — for every instance using it — goes with it. Copy out anything you want to keep first. Hibernated sessions and snapshots of that distro no longer match a fresh disk, so delete those too, or remove the instances.","s":""},{"u":"troubleshooting/reclaim-space.html#doesnt","a":"Reclaiming disk space","h":"What doesn't work","c":"Troubleshooting","x":"Deleting files inside Linux doesn't shrink the image A dynamic disk grows as Linux writes, but deleting files inside Linux doesn't hand the space back to your Mac. Clear caches inside Linux makes room inside the instance, not on your Mac. The only way to get that space back is to delete the image and start fresh.","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html","a":"msl --help","h":null,"c":"Command line","x":"Type these in Terminal.app. msl --help prints the same list, and msl files opens MSL Files.","s":"Every msl command in Terminal.app, grouped the way msl --help prints them."},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#get-started","a":"msl --help","h":"Get started","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does msl What you have and what to type next msl install <distro> Download a distro, then set up its first instance - name, CPUs, memory and disk msl run <instance> Open a shell. Short form: msl <instance> msl run <instance> <command> Run one command; exits with its exit code, like ssh msl run Open a shell in the instance you used last msl -- <command> Run one command in the instance you used last msl distros Every distro you can install, and which you already have msl files Open MSL Files, to browse your instances from the Mac (also --files) msl help This list, in your terminal msl version Which MSL this is (also --version, -v)","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#shell-options","a":"msl --help","h":"Shell options","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does -u, --user <name> Log in as this user instead of your default account (root is always there) -d, --distro <distro> The distro for a new instance: alpine, debian, ubuntu, kali, arch, fedora, rocky, alma, centos, oracle, opensuse, nix, or custom:<image>","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#instances","a":"msl --help","h":"Instances","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does msl list Every instance and whether it's running (also: instances, ls) msl list online Every distro you can install, with its download size msl list installed The distro images already downloaded to this Mac msl new [name] [sizes] Create an instance without opening a shell. No name: asks for name, CPUs, memory and disk msl resources [instance] [sizes] Show or change CPUs, memory and disk (also: config) msl storage [instance] [show/fixed <size>/dynamic <size>] Disk size, and whether its space is reserved on your Mac msl status [instance] Whether an instance is running, paused or off msl suspend / resume [instance] Pause in memory and continue - keeps everything exactly as it is, apps and all msl hibernate [instance] Save to disk and stop. Survives a Mac restart, but Linux apps are closed and reopened - only suspend keeps their windows msl --shutdown [instance] Power off cleanly; every running instance if none is named msl snapshot save/restore <instance> <name> Save or return to a named point msl snapshot list [instance] The snapshots an instance has msl forward <instance> <mac port>:<linux port> [--ssh] Reach a guest's port at localhost on this Mac msl forward <instance> list What is forwarded for an instance msl unforward <mac port> Close a forwarded port msl vault <instance> [status/unlock/lock/adopt] Whose key opens its container, and supply one you hold msl protect <instance> Move its disk into an encrypted container, so a copy of the file is useless msl unprotect <instance> Move it back out, as a plain file msl identity [instance] Where an instance came from - whether it is an original or a copy msl rename <instance> <new name> Give an instance another name, carrying its disk and settings with it msl clone <instance> <new name> Make a second instance from this one - its disk, accounts and settings msl remove <instance> [--keep-disk] Delete an instance. Removing a distro's last instance also deletes its disk msl remove-distro <distro> [--yes] Delete every instance of a distro and its disk msl <instance> --shipment [-o FILE] Package the whole instance into one .mslship file msl shipment info <file> What a shipment contains, without installing it msl shipment install <file> [--name N] Install a shipment as a new instance msl lock [instance] [on / off] Ask for Touch ID or your password before this instance starts msl dns [instance] [auto / cloudflare / google / quad9 / <address>] Which resolver the instance uses for name lookups msl vpn [instance] [status / install / remove] The hide.me VPN client inside one instance; you log in with your own account","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#sizes-for-new-and-resources","a":"msl --help","h":"Sizes, for new and resources","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does --cpus <n> How many CPU cores the instance gets --memory 6G / 2G-8G A fixed amount, or a range that grows and shrinks with use --disk 64G Disk size; your Mac only gives up space as files are written --disk-fixed 64G Disk size, reserved on your Mac up front","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#linux-apps","a":"msl --help","h":"Linux apps","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does msl apps list / scan [instance] The instance's GUI apps; scan reads them again from Linux msl apps install <instance> <app> Make a Mac app for it in ~/Applications/MSL (install-all: every app) msl apps uninstall <instance> <app> Remove that Mac app msl apps pin / unpin <instance> <app> Add or remove its Dock tile","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#connect","a":"msl --help","h":"Connect","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does msl ssh [instance] [--print] SSH in, setting access up the first time; --print shows the command msl images [list/open] Your own images in Custom Images; use one with --distro custom:<image> msl images new <image> --distro <distro> Start a custom image as a copy of an installed distro msl usb list USB devices on the Mac: which macOS driver holds each one, and which instance has it msl usb attach <device> [instance] Give that USB device to a running instance. <device> is a location ID or vendor:product from the list. A device macOS has no driver for goes straight through; one it does asks for your password and goes back on detach msl usb detach <device> [instance] Take it back; macOS picks it up again msl usb test [instance] Attach MSL's own virtual test device - checks passthrough works with nothing plugged in","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#maintenance","a":"msl --help","h":"Maintenance","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does msl doctor [--fix] Find leftover state, missing images and uninstalled tools; --fix cleans up msl fix-home [instance] [--dry-run] Give files back to your Linux account after apps that ran as root left root-owned files behind msl keyboard [--keysyms] The Mac keyboard layout, and what Linux apps see msl kitty [on / off / <words>] The cat at the top of every shell, with the greeting under her. With words, she says them in a speech bubble instead; on and off decide whether she turns up in shells msl greeter [on / off] The greeting line under the shell banner msl update [--download / --install] Check for a newer MSL; download it to your Downloads, or download it and replace this one msl install-tools From a build: install msl and mslhd into Application Support msl uninstall [--everything] [--dry-run] Remove MSL and keep your Linux; --everything deletes that too msl help [--no-app] This help, and MSL Help in the app; --no-app just prints","s":""},{"u":"cli/cli-help.html#experimental-and-debugging","a":"msl --help","h":"Experimental and debugging","c":"Command line","x":"Command Does msl gui-native <instance> [app] Run a Linux app on MSL's own display server msl gui <instance> [app] Run a Linux app through XQuartz msl cage-view <instance> [app] A Wayland (cage) session in a Mac window msl cage-bridge-test / cage-input-test <instance> Wayland frame and input round-trip checks msl power-test <event> Replay a power event: sleep, shutdown, logout, lowbattery, wake, battery...","s":""},{"u":"reference/shortcuts.html","a":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Everywhere","c":"Reference","x":"⌘N New Instance ⌘R Refresh (main window) ⌥⌘F MSL Files ⇧⌘? Open the Help menu — MSL Help is in it ⌘W Close the window ⌘Q Quit MSL — instances keep running","s":"Every keyboard shortcut in MSL, in one place."},{"u":"reference/shortcuts.html#main","a":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Main window","c":"Reference","x":"⇧⌘T Traffic Monitor (while the Sandbox tab is showing) ↩ Create, in the New Instance sheet ⎋ Cancel, in the New Instance sheet","s":""},{"u":"reference/shortcuts.html#help","a":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"MSL Help","c":"Reference","x":"⌘F Search the guide ⎋ Clear the search","s":""},{"u":"reference/shortcuts.html#files","a":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"MSL Files","c":"Reference","x":"Finder's own shortcuts, deliberately. Views ⌘1 as Icons ⌘2 as List ⌘3 as Columns ⌘4 as Gallery ⌃⌘S Hide or show the sidebar ⇧⌘. Show Hidden Files ⌥⌘P Show Path Bar ⌘/ Show Status Bar Getting around ⌘[ Back ⌘] Forward ⌘↑ Enclosing folder ⌘↓ Open the selection ⇧⌘G Go to Folder… ⇧⌘F Recents ⇧⌘O Documents ⇧⌘D Desktop ⌥⌘L Downloads ⇧⌘H Home ⇧⌘C Computer ⇧⌘A Applications ⇧⌘U Utilities ⇧⌘I iCloud Drive ⌘F Search this folder Files ⌘O Open ⇧⌘N New Folder ⌃⌘N New Folder with Selection ↩ Rename ⌘D Duplicate ⌘C Copy ⌘V Paste ⌥⌘A Select All Items ⌥⌘C Copy as Pathname ⌘I Get Info Space Quick Look ⌘Y Quick Look ⌘R Reveal in Finder ⌘⌫ Move to Trash","s":""},{"u":"reference/shortcuts.html#symbols","a":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Reading the symbols","c":"Reference","x":"Symbol Key ⌘ Command ⇧ Shift ⌥ Option ⌃ Control ↩ Return ⎋ Escape ⌫ Delete","s":""},{"u":"reference/msl-command.html","a":"msl command reference","h":null,"c":"Reference","x":"msl talks to the same background service as the app. Run msl help for this list in your terminal.","s":"Every msl subcommand, what it does, and its options."},{"u":"reference/msl-command.html#shells","a":"msl command reference","h":"Shells and commands","c":"Reference","x":"Command Does msl [instance] An interactive shell. An instance named after a distro (msl fedora) needs no setup. msl [instance] <command>… Runs one command, like ssh; its exit code is returned. msl -- <command>… The same, on the default instance. -u, --user <name> Which Linux user. Every distro also has an unprivileged msl user. -d, --distro <distro> Which distro, for an instance being used for the first time.","s":""},{"u":"reference/msl-command.html#instances","a":"msl command reference","h":"Instances","c":"Reference","x":"Command Does msl list (or ls, instances) Every instance. msl new <name> [--distro <d>] [--cpus <n>] [--memory <size>] [--disk <size>] Creates an instance, sized, without opening a shell. --memory 2G-8G makes it dynamic. msl resources [instance] Its CPUs, memory and disk. Also msl config. msl resources <instance> --cpus 4 --memory 6G --disk 128G Changes them — the same settings as the Resources and Storage cards. --disk-fixed <size> reserves the space up front. msl forward <instance> <mac port>:<linux port> Opens that port on this Mac, reaching the guest's. Bound to 127.0.0.1 only, so nothing outside this Mac can see it. Reaches a server the guest bound on 0.0.0.0; for one bound on 127.0.0.1 - which Flask, Vite, Rails and manage.py runserver all do by default - add --ssh, which forwards through the guest's own sshd and so connects from inside. Refused while the instance is sandboxed: a forwarded port would be a way out of the box. msl unforward <mac port> Closes it. Never refused by a policy - it reduces what this Mac exposes. msl admin certify <lineage|instance> [--label \"...\"] Signs a statement that this image is yours. The one tier a local user cannot forge: instance identities are signed by a key mslhd can read, a certificate by your MSLAP key — which a fleet keeps on another machine. Copy the file to /Library/Application Support/MSL/lineages/ on each Mac (needs sudo) so a non-administrator cannot add or remove one. msl admin revoke <lineage|instance> [--reason \"...\"] Withdraws an image. Every instance of that lineage then refuses to start, and no capability can set it aside — a revocation a policy could override would be no revocation at all. msl vault <instance> status Whether its disk is in an encrypted container, and whose key opens it. msl vault <instance> adopt Moves the container to a passphrase you hold instead of this Mac's own key. After this nothing on the Mac can open it until someone types it, including MSL after a reboot — and there is no recovery if it is lost. This is the rung that resists a determined administrator of this Mac; the default one does not. msl vault <instance> unlock / lock Supplies that passphrase for this session, or forgets it and closes the container. The key is never written down, so a reboot asks again. msl protect <instance> Moves the instance's disk into an encrypted container (AES-256), so a copy of the file is useless on any other Mac. The key is derived from the instance's identity and never stored. A real copy of the whole disk, so it takes minutes and needs that much free space while it runs; the original goes only after the copy inside has been read back and checked. What it does not do: anything inside the instance stays readable to anyone who can open a shell in it, and a determined administrator of this Mac can still reach the key. msl unprotect <instance> Moves it back out as a plain file. Refused where the policy denies instance.clone — an administrator who sealed an image did not mean it unsealed. msl identity [instance] Where an instance came from: a fresh original, or a copy, and how many copies deep. Every instance is issued a signed lineage when it is created; a clone keeps its parent's lineage and gains a generation, so a copy can be told from an original even if it arrived outside MSL. Reports only — nothing is refused on an identity in this version. msl rename <instance> <new name> Renames it, carrying its disk, accounts, settings and per-app overrides. Refused while it is running, and while it has hibernated memory or a snapshot: a guest's network address is derived from its name, and macOS will not restore saved state whose address has changed. Its Launchpad entries and Dock pins are removed, since a generated app launches an instance by name. msl clone <instance> <new name> Makes a second instance from it - the disk is an instant APFS copy, so it costs nothing until the two diverge. The copy keeps the original's SSH host key until the guest makes a new one. Refused while the original is running: copying a mounted filesystem captures it mid-write. msl remove <instance> [--keep-disk] Removes it and its saved state. Removing a distro's last instance also deletes its disk, unless --keep-disk. msl status [instance] Its state. msl suspend [instance] Freezes it in memory. msl resume [instance] Unfreezes or starts it. msl hibernate [instance] Saves it to disk and stops it. msl --hibernate [instance] The same — for every running instance if none is named. msl --shutdown [instance] Asks Linux to power off, then stops it — every running instance if none is named.","s":""},{"u":"reference/msl-command.html#setup","a":"msl command reference","h":"Distros, disks and snapshots","c":"Reference","x":"Command Does msl install <distro> [--manifest <url>] Downloads, verifies and installs a distro: alpine, debian, ubuntu, kali, arch, fedora, rocky, alma, centos, oracle, opensuse or nix. msl storage [instance] show Shows its disk settings. msl storage [instance] dynamic <size> A disk that takes only what it has written, up to <size>. msl storage [instance] fixed <size> A disk that reserves <size> up front. msl snapshot save <instance> <name> Saves a snapshot. msl snapshot restore <instance> <name> Restores one. msl snapshot list [instance] Lists them.","s":""},{"u":"reference/msl-command.html#apps","a":"msl command reference","h":"Apps","c":"Reference","x":"Command Does msl apps list [instance] Its Linux GUI apps, from the last scan. msl apps scan [instance] Reads them from the guest again. msl apps install <instance> <app> Makes a Mac app for it in ~/Applications/MSL. msl apps install-all [instance] The same, for every app found. msl apps uninstall <instance> <app> Removes that Mac app. msl apps pin <instance> <app> / unpin Adds or removes its Dock tile.","s":""},{"u":"reference/msl-command.html#other","a":"msl command reference","h":"Connecting and checking","c":"Reference","x":"Command Does msl ssh [instance] [--print] Connects over SSH, setting it up on first use. --print shows the command instead. msl doctor [--fix] Checks the installation; --fix deletes only what it listed. msl power-test <event> Runs MSL's response to sleep, shutdown, logout, switchaway, switchback, lowbattery or wake by hand; battery shows what the battery watcher sees. msl install-tools From a build: installs MSL's tools and its login item. Experimental commands msl gui, msl gui-native and a few cage- commands are experiments from MSL's development, for trying other ways of showing Linux graphics. The Applications tab is the supported way to run Linux apps.","s":""},{"u":"reference/where-things-live.html","a":"Where MSL keeps things","h":null,"c":"Reference","x":"Where What ~/Library/Application Support/MSL Everything MSL keeps: distro disks, hibernated sessions, snapshots, repair backups, settings. …/rootfs*.img The distro disks. See Reclaiming disk space. …/vm-<instance>.state Hibernated sessions. …/vm-<instance>-<snapshot>.state Snapshots. …/<disk>.pre-repair Backups from disk repairs. …/ssh/msl_ed25519 MSL's own SSH key. …/AppCatalog/<instance> Each instance's cached list of Linux apps, and their icons. …/bin MSL's command-line tools, including msl. ~/Library/Logs/MSL Logs, including mslhd.log. ~/Applications/MSL/<instance> Mac apps made for Linux apps. ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.msl.mslhd.plist The login item that starts the background service. ~/.ssh/config One Host msl-<instance> entry per instance with SSH set up. Inside Linux, your Mac home folder is at /mnt/mac.","s":"Every folder and file MSL uses on your Mac."},{"u":"reference/glossary.html","a":"Glossary","h":null,"c":"Reference","x":"Term Meaning Balloon How dynamic memory hands memory back: a device inside Linux that inflates to return memory to your Mac and deflates to give it back. Check at start An optional disk check before every start. See Checking at every start. Distro A Linux distribution: Alpine, Debian, Ubuntu, Arch, Fedora, Rocky and more — see Choosing a distro. Dynamic disk A disk that only takes the space Linux has written, up to a limit. Dynamic memory Memory that starts at a maximum and gives back what isn't used. e2fsck, e2fsprogs The Linux filesystem checker, and the package it comes in. Installed on a Mac with brew install e2fsprogs. Fixed disk A disk whose whole size is reserved on your Mac up front. Gate One of the four Sandbox switches. Guest Linux, inside the virtual machine. Hibernate Save an instance's memory to disk and stop it; starting resumes exactly where it was. Host Your Mac. Image A distro's disk, as one file on your Mac. Shared by every instance of that distro. Instance One named Linux machine. Maintenance boot Starting Linux in a minimal mode to check its own disk. Manual memory A fixed amount of memory, reserved while the instance runs. msl MSL's command-line tool. mslgd MSL's own X11 display server, which turns Linux windows into Mac windows. mslhd MSL's background service, which runs every instance. /mnt/mac Where your Mac home folder appears inside Linux. Posture The Sandbox tab's summary: Open, Partly sealed or Sealed. Slot One of the four instances MSL will run at once. Snapshot A saved machine state under a name. Stall Time Linux programs spend waiting for memory — a sign of memory pressure. Suspend Freeze an instance in memory; resuming is almost instant. vCPU A virtual processor. vsock The direct channel between your Mac and a virtual machine, which doesn't use the network. X11 The protocol Linux GUI apps draw with.","s":"Every term in MSL, A to Z."},{"u":"reference/credits.html","a":"Credits and licences","h":null,"c":"Reference","x":"The full story is in the About MSL window — choose MSL ▸ About MSL.","s":"Who made MSL, the licences it's under, and where to read more."},{"u":"reference/credits.html#about-window","a":"Credits and licences","h":"In the About window","c":"Reference","x":"About — the melon, MSL's logo; the version; and the mascot, illustrated by neekocat_2025. Licence — the full text, and why one repository has two licences. Contributors — everyone who has landed a commit, read live from GitHub, each with their own colour. Extras — what MSL is for, and the story of writing an X11 server from scratch.","s":""},{"u":"reference/credits.html#licences","a":"Credits and licences","h":"Licences","c":"Reference","x":"MSL is free software. mslgd — the X11 server, its per-app window hosts and the guest-side tunnel, and anything derived from them — is under the GNU General Public License v3. Use it, change it, share it; if you share changes, share their source too. The app ships with mslgd built in, so the app as a whole is GPL v3. Everything else — the app, the msl command, the background service, MSL Files, the guest daemons and the virtual-machine plumbing — is under the MIT licence, so anyone can use it for anything. Thank you For trying a small, stubborn project that set out to make Linux feel at home on a Mac. If something in this guide is wrong, or missing, that's a bug too. ♡ (˶ᵔ ᵕ ᵔ˶)","s":""}]};
